      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Society at Goodwood and Cowes.—Goodwood parties are now a customary feature at most of the great west Sussex houses. The motor car has brought many places within easy reach of the race meeting, that were formerly not within diiving distance and the result has been to add greatly to the fashionable Goodwood gathering. The King, as usual, has been staying at Goodwood House, where the list of guests entertained by the Duke of Richmond for the week is much the same year after year. Among other notable Goodwilod hosts are the Duke and Duchess of Norfolk, at Armillffel Castle, and Lord Leconfleld at Fetworth House, the latter being, with one exception the wealthiest of the unmarried peers. From Goodwood, fashionable Society moves on to Cowes, and Regatta hostetises are more than usually numerous. Lord and Lady Ornaonde are invari- ably fully engaged in doing the honours of the occasion, and American Society is always largely represented at Cowes. So many Americans are enthusiastic yachts- women; several of whom bold masters' certificates and sail their own craft. Altogether prospects for the Cowes Regatta week are very brilliant this year, and the presence of the Home Fleet will add to the gaiety of the scene fri the Solent.</p>
        <p>Over-Feeding.—Food-reformers say that the modern ' craze for athletics is only the echo of the need for , exercise to work off the effects/of over-eating. Most et us, they say, are brought up to eat, and add that the living at an ordinary country house would be slow death' without exercise. But the .cult of the moderate or scientific diet is spreading, and includes many notabili- ties among its converts. The Duchess of Portland is a confirmed food-reformer, and recently gave a vegetarian luncheon, at which Mr. Arthur J. Balfour was present. I Many other Society hostesses are also practically vege- tarians, including the Duchess of Bedford, Lady 1 Plymouth, Mrs. Asquith, dui Lady Paget. There is no doubt that over-eating is one of the most prevalent evils of modern life, and it is that which fills the foreign ' Hydros and Spas, whither-Society people are now flock- ing in search of " cures "and remedies. Making our Flesh Creep.—There Ls no doubt that with modern ideas and training, women are becoming stronger and more masculine in their ways, and a pro- fessor of the University of Chicago has been telling a class of horror-striken girls that, as a consequence women will, in course of tine, come to wear beards like men. Happily he added, after making their flesh creep with such a startling announcement, it will not be in our time, or that of the next generation, but that eventually it will be the natural result of women taking to bicycling, cricket, swimming, hockey, paper-chasing, fencing, rowing, and athletics generally. One would like to ask that professor why a lioness does not grow a mane like a lion? But whether or net there is any justificatson for his theory, he is a wise prophet, seeing that many generations are to pass away before the world will know whether he has spoken with wisdom or folly. Women and Sea-Bathing.—Ever since women and girls took to swimming they have been accused of being more concerned to show off an attractive figure or costume than by a natural liking for the water. It is a . stock subject of all the comic papers at this time of the year, and a horrible suspicion that there is just a shade of truth in is aroused bi the reports from various sea- side places that sea-bathing is not nearly so popular with the fair sex as it has been in recent years. As yet the summer has not been a favourable one for sea-bath- ing, but it is suggested that this is not the only reason fcr the falling off. It is hinted that the novelty having worn off, there is not the desire to make a display that has somewhat ceased to attract attention, and that that is the real reason for the non-appearance of lady bathers. As it is always difficult to prove a negative, one cannot say that it is not so, but it is probable that a spell of real summer weather would speedily show these insinua- tions to be baseless.</p>
        <p>Cities Noted for the Beauty of their Women.— Bruges, the ancient Belgian city, not far from Ostend, is famous for the beauty of its women, and was last week the scene of an historic pagaent similar to the recent celebrations at St. Albans and Bury St. Edmund's. In the 14th and 15th Centuries Bruges 'was the leading commercial capital of the whole world, and there is no doubt that its women owe something of their reputation for beauty to the wealth and splendid attire which made the citivins of Bruges the envy of Europe. But in most countries there is a town or • city which has a reputation for the ideal beauty of its women. In France it is the Southern-aity of Arles ;in Italy, it is Veranica ; and in our own Isles Limerick is favoured as surpassing any other city for the beauty of its women. These, however, are only relative distinc- tions, because there are far more beautiful women in London than could be found in any other city in the world.</p>
        <p>The Craze for Pagennts.—We are told that pageants are useful, inasmuch as they teach history to people who otherwise would not know any. No doubt such a statement is correct, and no doubt the masters of pageants are fully acquainted with the appearance the folks who simulate their ancestors ought to present. Unfortunately however, so many people taking part. in the recent pageants have quite overlooked the fact that the head and feet are the most imrortant items they have to consider, and that if these are right the dress will, in all probability, pass muster even though it is not quite so perfect in detail as it should be. At the moment folks are talking quite freely of" padging," and designers have been enjoying a busy time. A New Colour.—" Twine" may now be reckoned among the favourite colours, and for holiday wear, it will mean hard work to find a better one. " Twine " has a happy knack of proving very becoming to the average complexion, it harmonizes with almost every other colour' it is deliciously cool and fresh-looking, and.last- but by no means least, it does not show the dust. " Twine "is really the beige that leans to grey, I and is entirely without that dangerous tinge of red so I often seen in tussore.</p>
        <p>Lace Coatees.—Very dainty and pretty are the new lace coatees, which in reality are small boleros fastening at the back. Needless to say the real lace specimens are the most desirable, still many of good imitation lace, are not to be despised. These charming little garments which are edged all round with a tiny lace frill, the sleeves being of the kimono order, and quite sbort are intended for wear over a plain white muslin blouse, the muslin being of the softest and most supple quality. The accompanying skirt should be of white embroidered linen.</p>
        <p>More Weird Notions.—Some very wonderful and curious rooking aigrettes are appearing in the latest headgear. aigrettes that at first sight appear to be madeup of dried grasses, such as one very often sees in the parlours of country cottages. Tt wonli be hard to fix upon a more, inartistic and altogether undesirable ornament the " grasses " being abundant, and sticking out aimlessly in all directions. Children's Millinery.—The tenden# to elaboration in children's millinery, unfortunately increases as time goes on, and nowadays it is no unusual thing to see a little girl in a hat that could very well be worn by her mother. A child's hat should never be raised by means of bandeaux, from the head, it should, further always be round and perfectly plain, with no kinks in the brim, and the only trimming that should be allowed is a simple ribbon ruche, or, a band of wide ribbon taken round the crown and knotted in front or at the side, in a good bow. Elaboration is always a mistake for children and the wise mother is the one who follows the example of Royal mothers generally. The Princess of Wales, and the Duchess of Fife for example have always dressed their children very simply, Princess Mary of Wales invariably appearing garbed in the plainest garments possible. _ _ _</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>SEAL CAUGHT ON THE LANCASHIRE COAST. A finveal, about four feet long, was seen basking on he rocls at Grange. near Morecambe, on Monday evening. It was driven on to a sand bank, and a largo crowd assembled to watch it. It is nearly twenty years since a seal was caught in the neighbourhood.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday oftern-oon William Ralph Watson, grave- digger at the old Parish Church, Congleton, committed suicide by poisoning himself with weed-killer, after. being discharged. •</p>
