      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WOMAN'S REALM.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-NOME.</p>
        <p>A LYDIA BECKER MEMORIAL,</p>
        <p>A memorial of Miss Lydia Ernestine Becker, who fought so long and ardently for woman's suffrage in the days when it was not so popular as it is now, has been offered by Miss klargaret Ashton, on behalf of the subscribers, to the Town Hall Committee of Manchester. It takes the form of a bust in marble, slightly less than lib size, by Harno Thornycroft, and it rest i in a shell surrounded by a classic wreath. The like- ness to Miss Becker is considered very :rood. Miss Becker' was secretary to the Woman's Suffrage Committee. which , began its work in 1367. and subsequently of the National Society for -Woman's Suffrage. She was the first woman member of ,the Manchester School Board, re- taining her position on that body from the time of its constitution in 1870 until her death on July 18th, 1690, at Geneva.</p>
        <p>FASHIONS IN NECKI7</p>
        <p>The übiquitous stole has this season given place, says London Opinion, to the neck ruffle, fashioned of feathers. &lt;dyed all manner ef light colours from pale pink and blue to soft tones of grey or heliotrope, and finished with long bows of black velvet ribbon. Ostrich or long feathers alike are used to manufacture .the new ruffle. which is worn Slightly drooping off the •shoulders behind,. and is most becoming. With simple linen gowns. the vogue for stiff 'collars still remains, these being finished with the n:ost minute of silk bows in tiny checks and spots, with .a plain silk hemstitched border..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>SMART PLAIN</head>
        <p>A SMART PLAIN</p>
        <p>At no other time of the year does one seem to be, quite so lAdly in need of an odd skirt as at the holiday season. Lounging on beach or in woods and fields_ or scrambling over seaweedy rocks, in the skirt belonging to a costume rapidly transforms it into a disreputable rag, totally different in colour from .its less-used coat. An extra skirt is, then, a positive economy as</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>1:...,:;, : \ :,t, * '4:fs's.\‘ :'., .i ' . '.....,,• t' ,• ''.l'.••• ''.' , ' a 4: ~.,.., ~; ,‘,, . ~•:,:• ,::,:' '. :: ~, 4.": '.•• .1 '- . : ,' : , - , ; s.'..i ..'S -. ~.. - - •‘s . *'.•;:' ' --- - 5,•'• '": - • _&gt;--'' •c:::‘,. —..,----, _ --,_-_— ............. _....4 _ ~_</p>
        <p>well as a great convenience. The smart affair pictured in our sketch is, I think you will agree, just the thing for knockabout holiday wear. It is cut, as you will see, with seven gores, the back being arranged in a deep inverted pleat. Each seam is overlaid and machined about half an inch from the edge, adding greatly to the smartness of its appearance. A line of machin- ing outlines the hem, and suitably finishes the practical little skirt. DIFFERENCE BETWEEN HOUSE AND HOME.</p>
        <p>John Strange Winter, in an article in the Young Woman, on the difference between a house and a home, says: "In my opinion a house is never a home unless it contains evidences of the pursuits of its inmates. Yes, my dear madam, I know it is an awful nuisance when your young son worries your life out on the subject of post- age stamps, when he is almost too impatient to let you open a letter from a foreign country be- cause he wants the stamp—not because he has not a similar one in his album, but because he can 'swop' with some schoolfellow. It is annoying to find that, foi the sake of quiet, he has taken possession of your drawing-room, and has appa- rently played a sort of paper-chase with dupli- cates and anippings. It is vexing when you find saucers of sticky warm water on your polished oak dining-table, which is the pride of your soul —and something more to your parlour-maid. But take my advice and bear with it, you don't know what that hobby may do for your boy later on, nor how the fact that he was let to have a -real pursuit of his own may help him to make a home when yours is dispersed to the four winds of heaven. Bear with it, my dear madam, bear with it "</p>
