      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>LALI. 1t15t113 ItEbttlt j</p>
        <p>THE CARRIER CAT</p>
        <p>BY MRS. COULSON KERNAHAN,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Huse of Riminon,” "Trewinnct of Guy's," "The Avenging of Ruthanna," "No Vindication," "An Unwise Virgin," &amp;e.</p>
        <p>"We'll have one of those plum-puddings, Mary Ann," said Miss Wilhelmina Strutt to her hand- maiden. "Then, with the cold chickens and the veal and ham pie and a salad, we shall manage. I wish people would give longer notice before they ask themselves to luncheon! Mary Ann, what are you making faces for ? " The girl was, in truth, contorting her counten- ance in her efforts not to laugh. "Speak girl, and don't be sillly ! " said Miss viTilhelmina, wrathfully. "Oh, please inissus—mum, Mr. Charlie and Mr. Robert they come in early this morning to pack their bags to go up the river----" began the girl. Then she suddenly stopped short. "Well, girl, and what if they did? What on earth has that to do with the luncheon ? " exclaimed the spinster. Out came Mary Ann's next words like an avalanche:</p>
        <p>"Why, missus—mum, they've took the plum- pludding and the chickens and the veal pie with 'm." .</p>
        <p>"And it s Monday! cried Miss Wilhelmina; on the verge of tears, "and nothing to be got at the shops that is fit to eat. Oh, those boys! A nice thing to be saddled with three children, and no husband to keep them in order! Those boys have always done as they liked with me, and their s'ster the same. Where's Miss Anthea ? "</p>
        <p>"She's in the back garden calling the cat, missus—mum, and he's strayed again," said the maid.</p>
        <p>"Well, go and tell her to leave the cat alone, and come to me at once; and you go and 'get the beds made, and hurry for your life," said Miss Wilhelmina, all in a flutter. A moment afterwards a young and very pretty girl rushed breathlessly into the breakfast parlour, carrying in her arms a huge black eat. Miss Wilhelmina's eyes. fastened upon the animal, which was her especial pride and joy, and discovered a white tape round his neck. "Oh, those wretches of boys, they've been trying to hang you, my treasure ! " she lamented, taking possession of her pet. Bring the scissors at once, Anthea—oh, be quick!" And she passed a finger between the tape and the cat's neck to see how tight it was. 'Why, it's quite loose ! " she exclaimed, "and what is this ? '</p>
        <p>Anthea, who had round the scissors, came running to see what her aunt had discovered this time.</p>
        <p>"It's a bit of paper fast to the tape. There's writing on it," said Miss Wilhelmina, cutting the tape hurriedly and unfolding the slip of paper, on the outside of which was written: "To Pussy's Mistress,—Will pussy's mistress write her name on a piece of paper and let pussy bring it to the writer of thiS ? " read Miss Wilhelmina. "Well, I never! What impudence!" she com- mented. "Of course, we shall do nothing of the sort! It must be that old gentleman at the big corner-house, whom the charwoman says is so fond of cats."</p>
        <p>"Oh, I know!" cried Anthea, joyously, "the little old man who goes out in the big carriage and looks lost in it."</p>
        <p>"He has live cats, and they all feed at table with him," said Miss Wilhelmina. "I noticed that he was staring at me in church the other Sunday. I was wearing my new bonnet with the nasturtiums, Anthea, you will remember." Anthea did remember. A good many people had stared at that amazing piece of millinery. "But the luncheon! I had nearly forgotten. Mr. Howe is coming, and those brothers of yours, came in this morning and ransacked the larder, and there is nothing—absolutely nothing—to eat. Get on your hat, child. You must fetch a chicken, and order in some things from the grocer's. I will make out a list while you put your things Son." •</p>
        <p>Anthea ran upstairs. But she did not imme- diately put on her hat. She took a pencil and riper and wrote the name " Wilhelm i rm. " Then she captured Tommy and tied the paper to his neck, carried him into the garden, and dropped him over the fence. Her dark eyes were brimming over with merriment. In the meantime her aunt had rapidly written ,out a list of her requirements and placed it con- spicuously on the hall table. Then she repaired to her chamber and planted herself before her mirror.</p>
        <p>"No one would take me to be fifty-three," she observed to herself. "This yellow transformation Jo Its well with my complexion. It matches the dye of the back hair so well too. Older and worse-looking women have found a husband, and those boys are such a handful. His age, too, is so suitable." "Missus—muni I came in a strident voice from outside the door.</p>
        <p>"Well, what is it, Mary Ann ?" "The butcher, missus—mum." "Order ale of lamb." "I'll get that mauve satin made up," meditated Miss Wilhelthina. "Missus—mum !"</p>
        <p>"Oh, bothe: that girl!" cried the spinster. "I wonder what she wants this time? lam coming down, Mary Ann."</p>
        <p>"Auntie," cried Anthea, her roguish eyes dancing, "Tommy. has brought a rose with a paper fast to it With `Wilhelmina ' on kt .and 'sweets to the sweet '."</p>
        <p>"How on earth did he find out my name I wonder ? " asked the spinster, flushing. "And what impertinence to call me by it!" But for all that Miss Wilhelmina was inwardly delighted. She felt she would rather like to settle down into married life—and the old gentle- man kept a carriage. "I have found out his name," volunteered Anthea.</p>
        <p>"Oh, have you, and what is it, then?" in- quired her aunt, in a tone she tried to make laelcin in interest—hut failed.</p>
        <p>"He is a Mr. Redfern, and he made his money on the Stock Exchange." said Anthea. "Oh, I know—all that ' and 'bears business, and he must be a man or sense to have retired while he had got his money." "He has his son home now," Anthea observed, demurely. "Then he has a son ? r queried Miss Wilhelmina. "I wonder how he would hit it off with Charlie and Robert," she added inwardly.</p>
        <p>"He is tall and good-looking, and not a shrimp like his father," went on Ant Plea boldly. "How dare you call Mr. Redfern a shrimp, Anthca! lam surptised at you " exclaimed her aunt. "Well, he reminds mes of one," affirmed the girl; "but his son is. so.niee." "Have you seen him, then?" "Yes; he was out in the carriage with his father. Besides, I have seen him in his garden." "But how do you know it was his son ?" .</p>
        <p>"Because I as.ked Mrs. Riley, who knows about everyone, and she told me. She says 11.9 is just back from college—Oxford. She says father dotes on him."</p>
        <p>"That -is a wrinkle," the spinster said to. herself. Aloud she said: "And a very admirable trait in his character, lam sure." . "Auntie, auntie, loot! There is the carriage, and—yes, it is stopping here!" Miss Wilhelmina glanced at herself in the sideboard mirror hastily, adjusted the lace at her neck, find- waited for the bell to ring. Her heart was beating so tumultuously that her hand strved to her side The bell clanged. Both ladies listened with hated .breath. Footsteps in the hall were heard. Then the drawing-room door was closed, and Mary. Ann appeared with a card, bearing the name of Mr. Eustace Redfern. "You need not come," said Miss Wilhelmina to her niece.</p>
        <p>_ Antbea.danced rottnd the room as soon as her aunt had gone, laughing, and bringing a row of white teeth and two adorable dimples into view. In the meantime Miss Wilhelmina had entered the drawing-room in quite a girlish fashion, extending a hand to her visitor with shy graciousness. "We are neighbours, Miss Stria," began the little old gentleman, who had old-fashioned, courtly manners, "and I thought it would be pleasant to know each other—pleasant for me, I mean."</p>
        <p>"For us both," corrected Miss Wilhelmina. "My son is home,;' went on the old gentleman, "nrsd I helix you have two nephews and a niece</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>The young people might enjoy a game of tennis in my garden.' "I am quite sure they would," assented Miss Wilhelmina. , "Just now the boys are up the river, but they will soon be back.'" "I have another object in calling too," went on Mr. Redfern. Miss Wilhelmina's breath .came fast. "Our vicar told me that you are collecting for the children's school treat, so I thought I would bring my subscription." "Thai is very kind of you," said Miss Wilhelmina' faintly. "Not at all—not at all," said the old gentle- man. "Of all things I believe in making young folks happy—not that I would leave older people out. I don't think the heart grows old with the body, do you, Miss Strutt ?" "No," said the spinster, stoutly. "I don't think years make age. You know the old proverb, 'A man is as old as he feels, and a woman as old as she looks.' "</p>
        <p>"Then you must be young," said Mr. Redfern, with a courtly bow. Miss Wilhelmina blushed genuinely. "You are fond of cats, are you not, Mr. Redfern?" she asked him.</p>
        <p>"Yes, fam fond of all animals," said the old gentleman. "You possess a particularly fine Tom, as I happen to know. He frequently visits me."</p>
        <p>"Now it is coming!" thought Miss Wilhol- mina.</p>
        <p>"Did he bring the ipse ? " went on the old gentleman. "I do hop?you were not offended?" "Oh, no. It was a'pretty thought," said ,the lady, with a girlish smile. "Only to write the Christian name on the note was, perhaps, a little—a very little unusual." "A liberty, perhaps, but scarcely unusual," objected Mr. Redfern. "Men who fall in love have always done these things. Isn't that your experience ? " "Oh, Mr. Redfern!" "Ah, well, Miss Strutt, you are not going to tell me you are without experience in these matters. I should not believe you if you did." "I may have had my little episodes," acknow- ledged Miss Wilhelmina, "but never anything serious—that is, on my side." "Ah I see. You have made the poor men suffer. Well, ynu must make up for your un- kindness of the past by being kind now. " "One learns by experience," said the lady, hastily. "I shall not be cruel in future." "That's as it should be, Miss Strutt. And now won't you bring Miss Wilhelmina over to tea in my garden to-morrow, and not wait for the return of the boys ? " The spinster started slightly, and looked at her visitor in a puzzled way. "My niece is called Wilhelmina, after me, but we always call her by her secorld name—Anthea," she said.</p>
        <p>"Well, I like Wilhelmina best," asserted the old gentleman. "Perhaps I have a tender reason for my preference—but you did not answer my question." "Oh, yes, we will come with pleasure," assented Miss Wilhelmina.</p>
        <p>Mr. Redfern rose to go, and his hostess rang the bell with a hand that trembled.</p>
        <p>When the sound of the carriage wheels had died away Miss Wilhelmina sought her niece. "I want you, dear," she said, caressingly. "Yes, auntie ? " Anthea's eyes were full of mischief. "Come and sit at my feet, child," said the elder woman, in a low, tender voice. Anthea pushed a 'stool near to the chair on which her aunt was seated, and settled herself upon it, looking up with eager curiosity into the face which had .grown almost beautiful in the last half-hour. Anthea, casual as she was for the most part, could not but observe this new something in her aunt's face. The fountain of youth had broken forth in the dried-up old heart. To be beloved—to be chosen after all these years! "Remember, child, that what I am going to tell you won't make one bit of difference to your place in my heart," began Miss Wilhelmina, "'I-shall love you and the boys just the same. I will tell you, then, that Mr. Redfern has told me that he cares for me. He has not actually asked me to marry him, but he felt his way, so to speak, and I know weir his intentions. He is good and kindly and of a suitable age—and, Anthea, you and the boys will be hopping out of the nest in the natural course of things, and I shall be left a solitary old woman—unless— unless I consent to marry." Anthea had grown pale during this recital. She felt that there was a horrible mistake, and that it was partly her work. She felt she could never forgive herself if she had for her amuse- ment raised false hopes in the heart qf this kindly woman who had been a mother—and such a good mother too—to herself and her brothers. But after all she might be mistaken, and her aunt might be right. "What did he say to you, auntie ? " she asked, without any sparkle of fun in her eyes. "Well, child, for one thing he said he hoped I was not offended by Tommy's bringing the rose. Then somehow he knew your name was Wilhel- mina, and he said he preferred it to Anthea for a tender reason._ And he was SO nice about you children too. Oh! it was quite easy to see what he' meant. I have promised to take you to have tea in his garden and a game at tennis with his son. I wonder how Charlie and Robert will take it ?" Anthea took one of her aunt's wrinkle hands and kissed it. One or two tears fell upon it. "I do hope you will be happy—always, auntie," she said, with a little choke. Miss Wilhelmina caressed the girl's dark hair after her motherly fashion. "I do feel happy, dear—for—oh! I did dread old age and loneliness." Anthea sprang to her feet and faced her aunt with eyes full of a noble resolve.</p>
        <p>"Auntie dear, you shan't face old age and loneliness. If—if you don't marry, then I will never leave you." She ran out of the room and upstairs' shut herself Sin her bedroom, and, flinging herself upon the floor, sobbed. as if her heart would. break. "It is all my fault—my wicked fault ! " she sobbed. "I know it was for me, that rose, and I wrote 'Wilhelmina' on the paper and made all this mischief—and Mr. Redfern was trying to get aunt round to bring me and Eustace together— oh! I know it, and poor auntie thinks-- Oh, what a little. wretch I am! But I won't leave her to old age and loneliness—no, I won't—not even for him. Oh, it serves me right ! " Meaw ! Meaw ! Scratch! Scratch! Yes, Tommy was at the door. _</p>
        <p>Anthea rose and let him in. Another message sure enough. The girl tore the missive from the cat's neck and read as well as her swollen oyes would allow.</p>
        <p>"To-morrow I shall see you close. My father has been to see your aunt. Will you prove as kind as you are beautiful, Wilhelmina ? EuQtaee. •</p>
        <p>-Unknown to their elders these yeang people had made acquaintance by the eyes alone, at the distance of two gardens—Tommy had done the re4t. But how would it all eud now?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>111.</head>
        <p>111.</p>
        <p>In a big old-fashioned garden with a green sweep ot lawn and glowing flower-beds, a tea- table was set under the shadow of an ancient oak tree. Mr. Redfern was himself arranging a huge dish'of strawberries in tempting fashion. The tennis nets were out, and comfortable basket- chairs were placed invitingly. A young man of athletic build paced restlessly to and Ito in his white flannels. Presently he joined his father. "You are the very best of dads," he said, affectionately. "And you really think Miss Strutt did understand your hints and approved ? " "I am.sure of it, Eustaee. She is an extremely nice woman, and most sympathetic. I have admired your little girl myself for a long time. I often watched her playing with her cat in the garden—a sweet, pretty bit of roguish girlhood she looked. Moreover, the vicar tells me that she is as good as she is beautiful. May Heaven speed your wooing, my lad! and may you be as ::appy as your dear mother and I were. It is a good 'omen that her name is the same ,as your mother's—but I called your Mother Mina.` Miss Strutt tells me they call your little lass Anthea." "I wonder why she wrote Wilhelmina ' on that piece of paper and not .Anthel ' then?" queried Eustaee. "A girlish vagary, I suppose," said Mr. Red- fern. "See, there they come!" The old gentleman trotted down the carriage drive to meet his guests. Eustace followed modestly behind. Hopes and fears battled within him. Tommy had brought no answer to hk; last note. What if the airl had only been amusing</p>
        <p>TEAwith,vratfau,a,be!. I ad:!ltions. A Breakfast CUP for a id.</p>
        <p>' lit' 4401`•. ' N 3 • •" t. et: U 4 ....,0 . ..:' ~. . ,'.'; 7Srei</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Examiner, Ssturda,y, August 3, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Ssturda,y, August 3, 1907.</p>
        <p>herself She always looked brimming over with • fun. If so—but he dared not think of it. He had feasted his eyes on her joyous young beauty across two gardens till he was sick with longing I for her. He had wasted caresses and dainties galore on her cat. because it had lain in her I rouna arms. -What if she !mu only been amusing herself?</p>
        <p>But it was not a merry face ihat he saw now. It was a mournful little countenance that Anthea presented, and the pretty mouth had a pathetical little droop at the corners. She was wearing a white frock and a big picture hat and looked altogether adorable, but why those sad looks? Was she regretting her folly ? But Miss Wilhelmina was all smiles. She was wearing her mauve satin dress, and had on her nasturtium bonnet. Her golden transformation shone in the July sunlight. But the odd part of it was that her face seemed to have grown youth- ful to match her hair. She really appeared quite good-looking. Eustace waited while his father greeted the visitors. Then he was introduced. At last Anthea's hand touched his. What thrill it gave him! Their eyes met, and she blushed diviPely. n the meantime Mr. Redfern had led Miss &amp;mitt to a seat under the oak tree in his courtly f-hion, and had arranged a cushion at her back. Eustace performed a like office for Anthem. T 11( n a maid brought out a teapot and hot cakes. When tea was over, Mr. Redfern told the young ! fell:6 to have a game at tennis while he enter- ; tallied Miss Strutt.</p>
        <p>"What a beautiful place you have, Mr. Red- fern," said Misd„Willielmina, glancing round. "An ideal old ElYilish garden. I had no idea that those high walls of yours shut in so much be aid y." "And I knew that the fence round your garden shut in incomparable beauty," said the old man thinking of Anthea. "Oh, you are too complimentary," laughed the spinster.</p>
        <p>-"Not at all. Mr. Redfern. Miss Wilhelmina looked down.</p>
        <p>I only' speak the truth," affirmed</p>
        <p>"And when beauty goes with goodness," went on the old gentleman, warming to his subject and determined to make hay while the sun shone. "When beauty goes with goodness, and a man has it given to him, then he ought to go down on his knees and thank Heaven for it. That is my opinion. Eustace had a good mother—one in a. thousand. It is from her he gets his good look:. I have none to boast of as you can see for you .- self. Yet she was fond of me. Oddly enough her name was Wilheimina. That is why I was glad it was that child's name." He pointed over to a flying white figure carrying a poised racket. "They make a pretty pair, don't they.? ". Mr. Redfern meandered on. "Don't you think Eustace is a well-made fellow? As a boy at St. Paul's, he won so many cups that it was quite a business getting them engraved. The sideboard, is quite full of that lad's silver trophies. My friends told me that he was too good at athletics to succeed in anything else. But they were wrong, Miss Strutt. He won a scholarship at St. Paul's that took him to Oxford. Those boys whose parents can afford to pay for them, always do get the scholarships. Well, he tool; his degree in law, and now 1 thought of giving him a year's travel. He has never given me a moment's anxiety in his life, Miss Strutt, and he has always kept a warm corner in his heart for his old dad. It is no wonder if I don't want him thwarted in his heart's desire."</p>
        <p>"I quite sympathise with your sentiments Mr. Redfern," said Miss 'Wilhelmina, wondering vaguely what Eustace's heart's desire might be. While this conversation went on the two young people, tired of their game, had wandered off together into a more secluded part of the garden. Anthea's face was still the reverse of gay. "Are you angry with me about something,Mip. Milward ? " he asked, suddenly. "I habe watched you so often and I have never seen you grave till to-day." "I am angry with myself," broke out the girl, tears of mortification filling her dark eyes,</p>
        <p>"And why are you angry with yourself?" he asked her, concernedly. "Do you regret sending me a little message by Tommy ? " "You see," said Anthea, ipetulantly, "I tho4ht the first message was from your father to my aunt. Tommy is her cat. And—and I wanted to help things on. I had s-en you, and I thought how nice it would be to have you for —for another brother, so I wrote aunt's name on a paper and tied it to Tommy. And now—now poor auntie thinks your father is in love with her—and it's all my doing—and she's a dear— and I am too horrid for words!"</p>
        <p>At this point Anthea began to cry in good earnest.</p>
        <p>Naturally Eustaco sought to comfort her, and only one way occurred to him. He put an arm about her and hissed awayAhe angry tears. "Dear little girl !" he said, compassionately, "never fear, it will all come right; only I don't want to be another brother '—l want to be something much nearer and dearer."</p>
        <p>"But I promised auntie never to leave her old and lonely," sobbed the girl. . _ _ - _ - -</p>
        <p>He lifted her face with his hand and looked into her eyes. "I don't think your aunt will be left old and lonely," he told her. Mr. Redfern had by this time taken his com- panion into the house to look at the silver cups Eustace had won. "How proud you must be of your boy ! " ex- claimed Miss Wilhelmina.</p>
        <p>"Not much more proud than you are of that charming niece of yours," rejoined the old gentleman. "We have one thing in common at heart, our love for the children," said the spinster, whole- heartedly. "We shall find we have many things in common, I suspect, as we get to know each other better," the old gentleman rejoined, "but we must remember that those young people are not 'the children' any longer. You were good enough not to be offended by the messages my son tied to Tommy's neck. A novel way to go a-woo- ivg, I allow—but what is the matter, Miss Strutt ? Are you ill ? " Poor Miss Wilhelmina! A bolt had fallen from ehe blue. She saw the horrible, the humiliating mistake into which she had fallen. She was covered with shame. But she pulled herself together bravely, feeling thankful indeed that she had at least not betrayed the state of her mind by some unlucky word. "Oh, it is nothing. the heat is rather excessive. I am quite all right.' You were saying ? " "Come into the air, my dear lady. lam son 3 I brought you into the house. It was eulpabl.; thoughtless." . "Not at all, Mr. Redfern," broke in the spinster, with a little laugh which sounded any- thing but gay. "You were speaking of the young people." Slowly they walked towards the shady oak tree and once more sat down.</p>
        <p>"Eustace loves your niece, Miss Strutt, and I fancy she at least likes him from the looks I have intercepted between them. You would not object ?" Mr. Redfern's tone was tremulous with emotion.</p>
        <p>"Oh, no," said Miss Wilhelmina, stoutly. "It is so good see happiness—even through other people's eyes." The old gentleman looked steadily at his com- panion for some minutes, thinking that only an unselfish, kindly soul could have kept the lines out of her face. ..e</p>
        <p>Then he glanced across the lawn. There were, Eustace and Anthea. Their manner told t tale. These two, whose hands had met but an hour ago, were loverv- . .</p>
        <p>The old gentleman brought his gaze back to his companion's face. She had ,seen too, and a tear glistened in her eye. "We shall be lonely—you and I, when they leave us," he said, as if in sudden inspiration; and then: "But need we he lonely? Could wr not keep each other company ? " She looked at him inquiringly. She would not commit a second mistake. _</p>
        <p>"Miss Strutt, will you be my second Wil- belmina ?" he said. "It is perhaps not fair to ask you till you have known me longer, but--" "If you think I could make you happy," she said, in a low voice. When Eustaee and Anthea canie upon the elderly pair later, Eustaee whispered: "Didn't I tell you it Woulkall come right ? " [THE END.]</p>
        <p>dding a portion of the match of Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., who for this purpose broke his journey to Prestwich.</p>
        <p>e There was a large entry for the Sir Gilbert Greenell silver challenge cup which was played for on Saturday on the green of the Conservative Club, Sankey-street. There were many surprises during the match, notably when P. Bradburn defeitted T. Elli!on by 15-5. J. Holland played well until he met %V. Millington. During this game Holland fell off his form and was easily defeated. Lee played a capital game all throueh the match, and well deserved to win out-right. The final between that play3r and Millington was a" ding dong " game and until the last wood was bowled it wis not possible to name the winner. The luck, however, lay with Millington, who won the cup by 15 points to 14. In a few well chosen words the Chairman of the Bowling Green Committee (Mr. John Wells) presented the winner with the cup and the runner up with a gold medal. The cup has to be won three times before it becomes the property of any individual.</p>
        <p>Crostield's met with a reverse on Saturday when they journeyed to Garswood Hall. Only eight out of their ,thirty two players recorded wins whilst more than 'lair failed to reach double figures. The colliers in the end claimedithe victory by 143. At the close of the match the visitors were entertained to an excellent tea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Given fine weather to-day, the championship will be hanging in the balance. Twm teams Warrington and St. Helens are in the running and both meet on the Arpley ground. The visitors have slightly the better average as With a match in hand they are only one point behind, whilst in Warrington's favour is to be recorded that two easy matches had to be declared drawn whilst St. Helens have not had to abandon a single fixture. The local men are confident of victory and certainly everything is in their favour.</p>
        <p>The League tables to date are : FIRST DtvistoN. P. W. L. D. Pts. Warrington St,. Hel6ns 9 ... 7 ... 1 ... 1 ... 15 Earlestown Prescot St. Helens Recs. Newton-le-Willows lO ... 2 ... 7 ... 1 ... 5 Stanley</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>SECOND DIVISION,</head>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION,</p>
        <p>Earlestown - JO -i- l' 1 17 Prescot Warrington St. Helens Recs. ll 4 6 1 9 Newton St. Helens 9 3 6 0 6 Stanley</p>
        <p>As was generally expected Warrington had no diffi- culty in disposing of Newton on Saturday. Newton batted first and Cresswell reaped a rich harvest of wickets. He was practically unplayable and six out of his eight victims were clean bowled. Callon accounted for the remaining two and Newton closed their innings for 56.</p>
        <p>Warrington claim no small amount of talent in the bowling line,but so far only three men have been tried, Cresswell and Callon having borne the brunt of the attack. Cresswell heads theaverages which are :</p>
        <p>0. M. R. W. AVG. Cresswell Silcock Callon</p>
        <p>Warrington have a failing for making bad starts when batting, and on Saturday they followed the usual i course. J. Callon was beaten n the first over, and three runs later Cook found Wright's wickets. Podmore and Connor made a short stand, and the score was 22 when Podmore was held by the bowler. Seven runs later Connor was out for 14, whilst at 33 the Warring- ton professional was sent back. Bibby and Turton pro- ceeded to finish off the match, and the score was 79 When Bibby met with a nasty accident and had to retire. The Warrington innings closed for 105. A pleasing feature was the return to form of Turton (24) who made his highest total for the season.</p>
        <p>The batting averages of the eleven are : • Times not Highest</p>
        <p>Inns. out. score. Total. Avge. J. W. Bibby 6 ... 1 ... 52 ...226 ... 45.20 T. Wright C_resswell B. Silcock Duckworth 7 ... 1 ... 44 ... 74 ... 12.33 Richardson.. rurton 7 ... 1 ... 24 ... 69 ... 1150 J. Callon 8 ... 1 ... 24* ... 58 ... 828 Podmore 5 ... 0 ... 22 ... 40 ... B'oo</p>
        <p>Newton second shared the fate of their colleagues when opposed. to Warrington second on the Arpley ground. Batting first they proceeded to run up 14. The only feature of their innings was 26 by Robinson whose total would have been greater had he not been run out.</p>
        <p>Coming to bat Warrington fared but indifferently until Thompson and Partridge were together. Thompson hit out_vigorously and ran up 47. Partridge on the other hand was not lazy and played a careful game until the Newton total was passed when he too took risks and by aggressive tactics took the sting out of the bowling and credited himself with 25. Lowe (11) and Taylor (19), two new players, hatted well and promise to consider- ably strengthen the scoring power of the side.</p>
        <p>In the matter of averages the second team came out well as the appended figures show : Times not Highest . Inns, out score. Total. Avge. Thompson 4 ... 0 ... 83 ...151 ... 37'75 Hawtin - 6 ... 1 ... 66* —176 ... 35'20 Thewlis Dolan _ __</p>
        <p>Partridge Houghton Bainbridge 3 ... 0 ... 27 ... 39 ... 1300 R. G. Pierpoint Bennett Plinston Baxter 6 ... I ... 10* ... 29 ... s'Bo</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. RESULTS TO DATE. St. James' lO ... 7 ... 1 ... 2 ... 16 Cheshire Lines Engineers ll ... 7 ... 4 ... 0 ... 14 Orford Barracks Mackies Wilderspool Brewery 9 ... 3 ... 5 ... 1 ... 7 Bewsey 'Wesleyans Castle Rubber Work5.... Walker's Brewery</p>
        <p>St. James', as will be seen. still lead and a period of inactivity would appear to have \had an ill effect on Orford Barracks the erstwhile champions, who have a great deal of leeway to make up. On Saturday they came a bad cropper on the ground of the Wilderspool Brewery team being beaten by 48 to 33. The result was all the more surprising as Wilderspool have of late done very badly and suffered several successive defeats.</p>
        <p>Bewsey Wesleyans cut a sorry figure when opposed to the Cheshire Lines Engineers, replying to a total of 52 with 17. Walker's Brewery secured a much needed victory at the expense of the Castle Rubber eleven. In the match between St. James' and Mackie's not a single player reached double figures but whilst the, champions with one exception all scored, five ducks were bagged by the printer s.The final scores were 41 and 29 in favour of St. James'.</p>
        <p>Penketh suffered for their temerity in tackling West Derby 2nd as the home team were much too strong for them, and gave them a dose of leather hunting such as they do not desire repeating this season. The West Derby total was carried to 184 and in response Penketh scored 81. For Penketh Ward batted well for 39.</p>
        <p>There was an exciting finish in the match between Arpley Athletic and Warrington St. Elphin's on Satur- day, Arpley winning by one run with the totals ut 73 and 72. A striking feature of the match was the number of extras-38— and evidently the stutnpers on both sides require a little education in the art of keep- ing wickets. Two individual scores of 19 were recorded by T. Bibby and C. Chambers.</p>
        <p>Lovely-lane had an easy task set them by WeMeld Athletic, and won by 28. Maybury set them a good example by scoring 24, and was the only double figure man of the match.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire mills bad to bow the knee on foreign soil on Saturday, being defeated by Liverpool St. Cyprian's. The visitors bad first knock and were all out for 30 whilst St. Cyprian's proceeded to run up 37.</p>
        <p>The third round of the Works Competition was entered upon on Tuesday, when Walker's Brewery, who by the way are somewhat fancied for the championship, defeated P. and K. (No. 1). Last year the two teams mot in the final and As the result was on that occasion reversed, Walker's taste the sweets of revenge, It was their hitting powers which won them the match and in reply to 45 they scored 70 in 50 minutes for the loss of G wickets. Cook, who was their principal scorer was twice missed in the first over!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>The principal item in the bowling world on Saturday was the match between Lancashire and Cheshire Association, and Yorkshire, at Prestwich. Magnificent weather favoured the teams and a good crowd watched the play in the return county match. Much increased interest was caused in the match on account of the first visit of the Lancashire President, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M,P. for Warrington. He had motored over from Warrington, and all through his stay he took a keen interest in each game, being instructed in the mysteries of thumb bias and finger peg by the deputy President, J. W. G. Coombs. Mr. Crosfield is an enthusiastic supporter of all forms of sport, and, in a neat after-dinner speech, said he believed in such a healthy and harmless game, which did so much to bring men together in friendly rivalry. •</p>
        <p>Local interest was centered in the doings of the three Warrington representatives who all won their games. Albert Cleave, against W. Ladge, Dalton L. was 8-1, 16-4, and then ran out 21-6. Though George Holbrook, Warrington C., only won by 3, it was with good play against J. West, Taylor-hill. G. Ball, Warrington, and H. Heaton, Lockwood, were level at 14, 16, and 17. Then with two couples Ball won 21-17. The full scores were :—Lancashire 465, Yorkshire 452, a majority of 13, added to the 20 obtained in the first encounter, making the aggregate win for Lancashire 33.</p>
        <p>The return match between the Warrington Works Association and the Eccles Workshops Association, was decided at Eccles and resulted in a win for the home team by 17. Last year the game at Bank Quay was won by Warrington with a handsome margin and on the two matches Warrington claim a balance of 138 points. Con- siderable pleasure was occasioned by the presence</p>
      </div>
