      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>POOR CHILDREN'S SUMMER CAMP.</head>
        <p>POOR CHILDREN'S SUMMER CAMP.</p>
        <p>Warrington sent its second detachment of girls to the Poor Children's Camp on Saturday. Numbering about 100,they left the Drill Hall shortly after 10 o'clock in order to board the 10.28 train, Prestatyn being reached in time. The special performances of "The Ticket-of• Leave Men" given by the Catitiolic Amateur Dramatic Society in April last, for the benefit of the camp realized the handsome sirplus of .7E20, and great credit is due to ,the officialebl the Society for their strenuous efforts on behalf of the camp. The lion. Treasurer also desires to acknowledge the receipt of 4s. proceeds of the offertory at the Volunteer Church Parade at St. Paul's Church on Sunday last and £2 10s. from a special collection at the Training College Chapel. • The work done by the Warrington Poor Children's Camp Committee was officially recognized on Wednes- day when the °amp was visited by the Mayor, Alder- man Smethurst. His Worship, who was accompanied by the Mayoress and a number of ladies and gentlemen interested in the work,left for Prestatyn at 10.28 arriv- ing about 11.30, The walk to the Camp, which is situated about a mile from the town was somewhat fatiguing, the sandy nature of the road by no means assisting the pedestrian. On arrival the visitors inspected the camp and saw the children at dinner, They were then entertained to lunch in the pavilion at the close of which 'Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins in a short speech spoke of the pleasure which the Committee felt at the visit .of the Mayor. Mr. Itansome, hon. treasurer, supported the remarks which were cordially acknowledged by Alderman Smethurst. During the afternoon the children indulged in various games, the Mayor showing his interest by presenting prizes in the various events. After tea the girls assembled on the verandah and rendered some very beautiful part songs old English glees, and recitations. The items were given with very considerable merit and the visitors were pleasantly surprised by the way in which the chil- dren remembered what they were taught at school. The youngsters afterwards went for a short walk into the surrounding country. The visitors were favourably impressed with what they saw, the working of the camv under the charge of Mrs. Wilkinson leaving nothing to be desired. Towards evening the party returned to Prestatyn, leaving for Warrington by the 6.53 train. Any visitor from Warrington will be cordially welcomed at the Camp, a visit to which will amply.' epay any who make the journey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Mar.</head>
        <p>Mar.</p>
        <p>:THE UNION BANK OF MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>LIMITED.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>I The Annual General Meeting of the Union Bank of 'Manchester Limited was held at the Head Office of the I Bank, York-street, Manchester, on Wednesday. The chair was occupied by Mr. G. C. Haworth, (Chairmb,n of the Bank), who was supported by Sir Anthony Marshall ( Vice-Chairman), Colonel C. H. France Hayhurst, DL.. !llessrs. Charles Behrens, M. Cababe, H. M. Addey, J. R. Oliver, A. H. Bright A. J. Fletcher, and P. Forrester, (the General Manager.) The Chairman said : In submitting the report for the past half-year, which has been printed and placed in your hands, f propose that it be adopted. The profits for the past half-sear, although not larger than the pre- ceding half, yet exceed the corresponding period in 1906. In comparisons it is only fair to refer to the corresponding times in previous years. In a bank;as trade, there are respective conditions characteristic of the June half and of the December half. Taking that ground, I may congratulate you upon the increased profits as compared with former periods, and what I am I glad to add is, that the profit has been earned ' with only a small increase in expense. The amounts placed to Bank Premises and Pension , Fund are the same, while that placed to Reserve is £2,000 more that it was in June 1906. In addition, I must remind you of the reduced value of securities, and the higher the class of security the greater has been the reduction. Thus we have, as i against t4e depreciation of Consols, placed to our I Consols Reduction Account the sum of £7,500. One means of extending our operations is by increasing the 1 number of our branches. The latest has been at Nant- wich, where we have taken over the bank of Messrs. Downes and Co. The business is small, it is true, but 1 it is of the highest class in that locality. In the transfer I we have not lost an account, and to secure its continu- ance of progress we have the co-operation of the late proprietor. The next item it is my duty to draw your I attention to is that of £4,671,194 due to customers. This is a little less than it was last half-year, but the slight reduction is caused by customers using so much of their money in trade instead 'of placing it in the Bank. There is a sub- stantial increase, however, compared with last June. The Billa.of Exchange gindicate the fluctuations of our business. The total cash, investments, and bills of exchange are important items in the balance sheet of every bank, as showing the cash that can be handed across the counter practically without notice. That amount, as you will find, is £2,057,049, being equal to 44 1-16 per cent, of our liabilities. As a Board we make no apology for continuing the dividend paid for some years past, although we might have increased the percentage, but on that point we have no desire to rival other banks, preferring to continue, as hitherto, fortify- ing our position against the enemy of bad times; for whilst it is true we have enjoyed some good years, it is certain that lean years will follow. Mr. W. H. Verdin said he had very much pleasure, as a considerable shareholder and one of the oldest clients of the Bank, in seconding the resolution'which had been proposed by the Chairman. He thought that they, as shareholders, would like to congratulate the Directors on the very excellent report which the latter had submitted to them on that occasion. Might he, as coming from Cheshire, say how pleased he was to see that they had opened a branch at Nantwich, which besides being a source of profit to the Bank, would also be a convenience. The Bank, as he said some years ago, seemed to have taken a fresh lease of life, and he believed the Union Bank would go on increasing and strengthening itself, so that it might become one of the largest Banks in the provinces. The Chairman then put the resolution to the meeting, and it was duly carried. Mr. A. H. Bright, moved the re-appointment of Mr. Charles Behrens as a Director. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Behrens, in thanking the shareholders for re-electing him a Director, said he WM very much obliged to them and his co-Directors for the confidence reposed in him, and it would always be his endeavour to merit such confidence.</p>
        <p>Mr. M. Cabab4 pro-po-sed : "That Colonel Charles , Hosken,France-Hayhurst, J.P., D.L., be and is hereby appointed a Director of the Company." It occurred to him that, as a very old shareholder, he might be allowed to make one or two remarks on the general position of the Bank in confirmation in every respect, of what their Chairman had said. They would not have failed to observe that the Union Bank had done what he thought no other bank in the country had done, namely, met the depreciation on Consols out of their profits. Some banks had, might he be per- mitted to say, muddled the thing up, and one did not know what they had written down or what provision they had made. The Union Bank was in the happy position of being able to take £7,500 from the net profits and write down half a million Consols to S3l. This, he said, pot the latter on really a 3 per cent basis. It was very unsafe to prophesy anything about Con- sols as to what the future might be, but in view of the combination of circumstances which had resulted in reducing them to a 3 per cent basis, he thought there was a reasonable expectation that they need not have much fear of their going below that. Mr. liesketh said he had great pleasure in seconding the resolution, which was carried. Mr. J. E. Balmer moved: That Messrs. Jones, Crewdson and Youatt be and are hereby appointed Auditors of the Company, and that the remuneration be 300 guineas,to include all travelling expenses. Mr. Kerfoot said he had pleasure in seconding the above resolution. Mr. Dutton moved : "That the cordial thanks of the shareholders be given to Mr. Forrester, General Manager, the Administrative Officers, the various- Branch Managers, and the other officers of the Bank for their services during the past half-year." Mr. Holt seconded the resolution. Mr. Forrester, in repling, said he thanked them very. much for their kind expressions, not only on his own.. account, but on behalf of the other members of the staff. He could assure them that everything was work- ing smoothly and that there was no friction of any kind. He said it would be invidious for- him to select any section of the staff for special commendation, but he thought that the Branch Managers played a very important part in the success of the Bank, and they, one and all, worked with great zeal and enthusiasm, and- he thought their ability compared favourably with similar gentlemen in other banks, and the improvement in the business which had been • referred to was in a large measure due to their efforts. A vote of thanks to the Chairman concluded a very harmonious meeting.</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL AND NORTH WALES STEAMBOAT</p>
        <p>SAILINGS.</p>
        <p>In connection with week-end sailings of the Liver- pool and North Wales steamers the company have made ample arrangements for week-end traffic. On Saturday, 3rd, "La Marguerite" leaves as usual at 10.45 a.m., and at 2 p.m. "St. Tudno " will be an extra boat for Llandudno, and followed at 2.30 p.m. by "St. Elvies " for all places to Menai Bridge. Passengers by afternoon boats can avail themselves of late trips from Llandudno. viz.." La Marguerite" " St. Tudno " and "Snowdon" for Liverpool, in addition to which those who prefer a long sea trip can travel by " St. Elvies "to Menai Bridge, returning direct to Liver- pool. Special low fares are announced for these after- noon excursions. ,</p>
        <p>On Sunday, Augrist, 4th, "La Marguerite" "St. Tudno ""Snowdon" leave with return connection from Llandudno for half-day passengers. On Bank Holiday special excusions are announced by "St. Tudno "for Llandudno and Holyhead, "bt.Elvies" for Llandudno, Beaumaris, Bangor, and Menai Bridge, due back in Liverpool the same evening. Passengers by these steamers can have about gix hours ashore at Llandudno. The last boat for Nath Wales on Bank Holiday will be " La Marguerite," at 10.45 a.m., for all places ty Menai Bridge. There will be the usual early morning boat on Tuesday, 6th August, due in Liverpool at 10.15 a.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>HANDY GUIDE TO LONDON.</head>
        <p>HANDY GUIDE TO LONDON.</p>
        <p>Law Courts, Strand.—Sometimes called Courts of Justice by successful litigants; other things by others. Supported by involuntary contributions to provide a livelihood for the Bar of England. Do not wake the judges. Rohn of Lords.—lnstitute for the cure of insomnia. England's mighty dead sleep in the neighbouring Abbey. Others here.</p>
        <p>House of Comntous.—Fine smoking-rooms and library. The cheapest lunch in London. Cigars of the finest brands.</p>
        <p>Albert Hall.—Devoted to Primrose Lea`gue gatherings and other vocal exercises. Has for years been chosen by Madame Patti as the scene of her\annual farewell performance.—" Landon Opinion."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>KILLED. BY FURNITURE POLISH.</head>
        <p>KILLED. BY FURNITURE POLISH.</p>
        <p>A jar of mixture composed of turpentine and beeswax, which had been left on a stove at a house in Dover took pire recently, and ignited the clothing of a child named Hetty Sarah Gillman, aged 73 years, who was fearfully burned. She died at the hospital, and at the inquest on Tuesday a verdict of accidental death was returned.</p>
      </div>
