      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>• `-,•?:. _ ,</p>
        <p>Great men, said EDMUND THE GRANT TO BORNE, are the guide-posts LORD CROMER. and land-marks in the State. By their achieve- ments we know the worth of a nation. They emphasise its progress, they stand for the expres- sion of its will. Great Britain has been blessed by the possession of many great men in the depart- ments of the State, and in no department has she been better served than by her Pro-Consuls—men who have carried to distant parts of the earth the spirit of her own life, who have taught the foreigner the meaning of liberty, and shown the way to apply the principles of government to new lands; and from out the long procession of men who have worked in the building of the Imperial structure the name of Lord CROMER stands supreme. Modern Egypt regenerated rescued from the bonds of Moslem tyranny, free from the slavery of past years, and set upon the way of prosperity is CROMER. To the great adminis- trator is due almost all the change which has been wrought in the land of the Pharaohs. Other men there were who stood at his side in time of stress and strengthened him, but even so the burden fell upon Lord CROMER. He it was who dealt with the well-meaning but vacillating KHEDIVE in the early days, and insisted that the modern methods which were to be Egypt's salvation should prevail. He it was later, who, by force of will, overawed the new KHEDIVE, and made him understand that work which had been accomplished should not be set back to please his caprice. The grant which Parliament has made to Lord Cnomics is only a just acknowledgment of the debt under which the nation lies to him. Egypt is for many reasons necessary to the Enpire, and the sacrifices which have been made for it by this country have been dictated by a coriviction on the part of English statesmen that no effirt should be spared to maintaia the position we have acquired for ourselves in Egypt. Without the service rendered by Lcrd CROMER it would have been impossible to secure that the occupa- tion of Egypt should be alike of benefit to the Egyptians and to ourselves. As to the opposition to the grant raised by the Irish and a few other Members of the House of Commons, we will only say that it was based upon a conception of the effect of British rule in Egypt which is not that of the ordinary man. The Government through the mouth of the PRIME MINISTER and Sir EDWARD GREY, has shown the ' highest appreciation which those who are charged with the business of the Empire have of the long- continued labours by which Lord CROMER succeeded in bringing order out of chaos, in giving to Egypt a stability in national credit of which a less courageous man might have despaired, in con- ferring an educational system upon her children which is the supreme hope of her future. The grant of £50,000 is not an adequate reward for services such as this. It is an expression of a nation's gratitude to a man who in evil times carried through a great work calmly and with high courage, never shrinking before difficulty, never unduly elated by success.</p>
        <p>The by-elections of late have THE BY-ELECTIONS, not been altogether satis- factory to the Liberals or to the Government, but they have been fraught with far more menace to the Conservatives. At Jarrow and Colne, candidates who were Socialists won the fight, though only at Colue did the victor get in on his Socialist ticket. There was a Liberal and a Tory on the losing side in each of these three cornered fights. The Tories affected to get some satisfaction from the fact that the Government nominee was defeated, but it must have been melancholy satisfaction, for the • success- ful candidates are more diametrically opposed to the " Constitutional party " than even the Liberals. They will have none of Tariff Reform nor of Toryism. The Socialist is, in some ways, a Liberal with the brake off—going full-speed ahead for reform and somewhat reckless of the fitness of times and things. The by-election on Wednesday this week in the North-West Staffordshire Division possesses no consolatory features for the Conservatives. Mr. Stanley, the Liberal-Labour mining candidate, has been returned by a larger majority than was recorded on the flood-tide of 1906, and against a strong local candidate. But the Tory was a Tariff Reformer, and the hard-headed electors of !North West Staffordshire dealt a crushing blow at the Protectionists. They have been of late somewhat elated and inclined to believe that their propaganda was gaining ground among the people. Mr. Stanley's triumph by a majority of 2,349 votes should give them pause, the victory is so unmistakeably one for Free Trade. All through the contest the Conservative canffidate had insisted that Tariff Reform was necessary to "save the industries of the country." Be drew his illustrations largely from a supposed injury done to the pottery trade through the free import of china earthenware, and as the pottery towns stand in the division, and Mr. Twyford is himself a pottery manufacturer, it was believed that his appeal would divert at least an appreciable number of voters from the Liberal to the Conservative side. Apparently it was so much wasted effort. Mr. Twyford and others will need to use better clay for the moulding of public opinion—particu- larly in Staffordshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
