      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <p>lk!:-,',7.V.:,..'4:4?:.,,'.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>Mr. A. J. King, M.P., for ANOTHER MAIDEN the Knutsford Division, has SPEECH. not been long in following the example of the -Warrington Member, Mr. Crosfield. He rose and addressed the House of Commons for the first time on Thursday evening, and was congratulated by Sir Edward Grey, the Minister for Foreign Affairs, whom he inter- rogated with regard to the attitude of this country's delegates to the Peace Conference at the Hague. We should like, at this point, to add our cordial congratulations to the many Mr. King has doubtless received, and to say also that we are sure his maiden effort in Parliamentary debate wilt' give great pleasure to the constituents of the 'far-spreading Knutsford Division. Mr. King quoted the " Tribune " to the effect that the British delegates to the Hague had so far done nothing to further arbitration or prevent war. This was• the .statement to which Sir E. Grey's attention was drawn, and altogether Mr. King showed the proper concern of a 'Quaker and a representative of the people for international peace. He hoped that some day this country would be able to send a message to the Hague Conference that we had decided to reduce if not abolish, our army and navy and refer all dis- putes to arbitration. Sir Edward Grey, in a ; courteous and re-assuring reply, pointed out that an appeal for the reduction of armaments had been ; made emphatically and in the most public way by the Prime Minister, and supported in the House by himself. He still remained "impressed with the almost pathetic helplessness of mankind under the burden of armaments." The British •delegates had supported the United States in reference to general arbitration and were -in favour of the transformation of the Hague Conference into a permanent tribunal. With regard to leading the way in a reduction of Ekrmaments, the nation which did so stood a chance of martyrdom. They must proceed at such a pace ,as would carry the leading nations of the world with them. Mr. King is entitled to considerable gratitude for having drawn such an important pronouncement from Sir Edward Grey on the great subject of "peace on earth." The sincere .opinions of the respected Minister for Foreign Affairs cannot fail to have weight in the Counsels •of Europe and the Conference at the Hague.</p>
        <p>So much has been written THE CHILDREN'S in commendation of the CAMP. work of the Poor Children's Summer Camp that the general public should be familiar with the benefi- cent operations of this excellent organization. Each year children from the Poorest parts of the -town are sent to the seaside and enjoy an all too brief holiday amidst the beautiful and health- giving surroundings of the Welsh coast. Little wonder that those who are wont to spend their days in dark and dingy streets look forward with keen pleasure to the week of free enjoyment by the sea. This year the camp has been as successful as ever. To-day the second contingent of girls will return and the boys will begin their holiday. It is pleasing to record that civic recognition has been accorded to the work of the camp this week, the Mayor and Mayoress having visited Prestatyn and expressed themselves highly pleased with the way In which the youngsters are looked after by the band of willing helpers. His Worship's kind words will no doubt afford great encouragement to those Who have the success of the camp so keenly at eart and we hope others will take the opportunity of visiting Prestatyn and judging of the value of the institution.</p>
        <p>Professor Sayce never fails BETTER THAN THE to astonish. The Professor ARABIAN NIQIIITS. of Assyriology at Oxford University is a very wuider- ful man. Nothing much more charming appears in the "Arabian Nights "than his account in the 'Contemporary" read from 4,000-year-old letters of the social life of Assyrian settlers in Cappadocia in the Abrahamic Age. One stands in wonder, as the clouds pack away, to see the same sunlight lying on the plains we will gild the summer landscape to our own eyes this afternoon. Twenty-six years ago Dr. Pinches noticed at Paris a cuneiform tablet in an enig- matic language. Two years later Professor Sayce asked Sir William Ramsay, who was going to Cappadocia, to get him more of them. Sir William obtained five. These, with others subsequently, Phewed by dialect, grammar, and date, that Kara Eyuk—" the Black (burnt) Ruin'—fourteen miles from Kaisariyeh—was the centre of great traffic, mining, and merchindise in the day of the Babylonian legislator Khammu-rahi. Pro- fessor Sayce constructs a varied, animated, and -human picture of this civilisation from thPse tablet-letters. Here is money lent without interest, a fine merely being exacted if the money iS not returned by a specified date; here are Partners in buEiness ; here are weeks of five days from which the Greek week of ten may have come; records of a big trading company like the East Indian or the Hudson Bay; mortgaKes • the first references in history to coins (truly said mortgages; that the Lydians first struck coins) ; references to the '1 postmen" who carried the letters; to bank- notes, to market gardeninc,,,to dress, to wardrobes, to journeys by moonlight, to death duties—in Rood sooth—and alas! to divorce. This all happened 4,000 years ago, and its record shows us an Asia Minor passing notes from hand to hand as Part of the Babylonian Empire in Abraham's day. Its the time of Moses it was the seat of another and native empire, deriving its civilisation and Writings from Assyro-Babylonian colonists. "When the hosts of Agamemnon encamped before Troy," as Professor Sayce picturesquely Puts it, Asiatic culture was already old.</p>
        <p>Our Transatlantic cousins, BOGUS YANKEE rightly or wrongly, have a ANCESTRY. reputation for banging the big drum. Everything over there is conceived, and carried out on a colossal scale. Creation is licked by the national megalo- maniac. Consequently, when the played-out old home of the Pilgrim Fathers is referred to it is regarded not as an island but an ?yot, where it is impossible to hustle lest the inhabitants get pushed into the sea. Notwith- standing all men in a republic being technically equal, there is perhaps more pride of family or o sate in the land of the stars and the stripes than in our own constitutional monardhy, which Prefers an aristocracy to a plutocracy. Evidence of this fact has, of late, become very noticeable by the tablets affixed in many parish churches in this country. At one time, it was the custom for the successful tradesman over here to go to the 'College\ of Heralds, or employ a heraldic agent to trace out for him a coat-of-arms and a family tree. Revelations of such bogus escutcheons have, of late years, led to a decline of the English shop- keeper indulging in such expenses. But the wealthy American has gone on where purse-proud John 'tall left off, and the spectacle of the numerous Memorials fixed up by *Uncle Jonathan in no end of Anglican parish churches multiplies the passengers of the little Mayflower in an alarming manner. he must have been below water-mark if half. of the people who went over in her, according to all these memorials, reached the other side. Clearly, the submarine is no new invention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY. The New Appointments.</head>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY. The New Appointments.</p>
        <p>There was a long and heated discussion at a special 'Meeting of the Warrington Co-operative Society last a, vening on the president's proposal that the rules no altered to give the committee full power to appoint a secretary. Tt was stated that a manager had been 41,1)Pointed, and that the committee had in view a tiarton-on-Trent gentleman for the secretaryship. Several members strongly urged the claims of Mr. Albinson, an old servant of the society, to the post. It transpired that there was a likelihood of his being "Pointed assistant secretary and ultimately the alteration of rules was approved.</p>
        <p>ANDED. Walter Burns, from Antrobns, was charged at the I?iceasional Court, on Wednesday, before Mr. Henry eild, with having stolen a coat and vest valued at one I)43und the property of John Mannion—Prisoner was 'remanded until the 9th of August.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
