      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The report that Mr. W. C. Anderson was to be the Labour candidate for Warrington at the next election is hardly likely to be confirmed. Mr. Anderson has this week been approached by the Oldham I.L.P. with the view of contesting the " hilly-milly "town.</p>
        <p>* - The question "Is niarriage a failure? " would probably be answered in the affirmative by a Totten- ham girl. She was married on Wednesday and on Thursday applied for a separation order. An unfeel- ing bench refused the order and sent her home.</p>
        <p>A rule of the road will have to be made for the guidance of Channel swimmers. So many emulators of Captain Webb are announced to essay the feat this week-end that there is serious danger of collisions. The "boys" might be painted different colours or carry bells on their heads—or toes! * * * It was the closing day of the local manceavres. An officer, seeking his own lost legion, asked one of a file of wet and weary men: "Are you the West Riding ? " "No," he replied bitterly, with a glance at the officer's comfortable mount, we're the unmentionable Buffs, walking." * * *</p>
        <p>"Have you any children?" demanded the landlord. " Yes," replied the would-be tenant solemnly, "six— all in the cemetery." " Better there than here," said the landlord consolingly, and executed the desired lease. In due time the children returned from the cemetery, whither they had been sent to play. But it was too late to annul the contract. * *</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Howes, the borough Member's popular election agent, has just published a volume of personal reminiscences under the title "rwenty-five years' fight with the Tories." It contains plenty of entertaining and instructive reading, for Mr. Howes, since he commenced to " feni " for himself at the age of nine, has had a varied and exciting career. The price of the book is 4s. 6d. and a large sale is already assured. * * *</p>
        <p>A Frenchman just arrived in England, who had been taking English lessons on the voyage from a fellow- passenger, complained much of the difficulty of our ,grammar, especially of the irregular verbs. "For instance," said he, ze verb, to go. Did you ever see one such verb ? " And with the utmost gravity he read from a sheet of paper: "I go, thou departest, he clears out, we cut stick, you make tracks, they absquatulate. Mon Dieu! Mon Dieu! What irregular verbs you have in your English language." * *</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night the members of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee had the unusual experience of feeling satisfaction with the treatment meted out to them by the Warrington Vorporation. This arose out of the settlement of the lighting question on terms distinctly advantageous to the village. In the past, on this and other matters, curses have been freely heaped upon the ahgust heads of the Corporation and probably the municipal authorities will give thanks that they have at last found a little favour in the eyes of the village fathers. * * * _ _ _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>The following remarliable- tests of sobriety were applied by a Liverpool doctor to a man suspected of being drunk, and he passed them sudcessfally : Marching along a defined line. Walking the same line blindfolded. Standing on one leg without falling. Picking a coin up from the floor with his teeth. Pronouncing "British Constitution," "Royal Artillery," and "The ragged boys ran a race round the rugged rocks." _ . _ _</p>
        <p>The Ni.nch said the man must be "a bit of an acrobat," but they wondered ,why the doctor did not try him with "truly rural." tr. o _ _</p>
        <p>Owing to the wet summer, the dust nuisance has not been so pronounced as formerly, but practically every dry day emphasises\ the fact 'that the nuisance still exists. We notice that though the Cheshire County Council offered to district councils to con- tribute one-third of the cost of treating the roads with dust-laying preparations. Nine urbnn councils did not reply, ten declined the offer and twelve accepted, while ten rural councils declined and two parish councils declined. Most of the members of local authorities think that either the motorists or the State should pay this extra cost of road-protection. It is a decided hardship when the cost falls upon the ratepayer.</p>
        <p>Sir Goren Barnes on one occasion at dinner corn plained of a headache, nnd being asked the cause, replied curtly, "scent." So small a cause can ruffle the judge's serenity and affect his well-being. Women of the present day carry the abuse of scent to an extreme. The ladies of the Divorce Court love per- fumes, and as each witness stands near the judge and waves her dainty bit of cambric saturated with scent, which penetrates quickly to the sensitive nerves of the judge, the air is rapidly filled with the odours of patchouli, white rose, heliotrope, and that particular new.scent which a talented authoress once described as a mixture of the smell of leather and birdseed. - LADY VIOLET GREVILLE in "London Opinion." * * *</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. Bracecamp7s address to the Volunteers at the Church parade on Slnday, was of a practical, commonsense character, except in one respect. The rev, gentleman took the opportunity—not a very appro- priate one—of fostering the military spirit by address- ing the civilian members of the congregation to the effect that ". he hoped the day was not far distant when they would be compelled to come forward and serve their country." At first, we thought the preacher was looking forward to an invasion of England, but he was merely preaching the doctrine of compulsory service. Surely the pulpit might be put to better and more Christian use than the advocacy of a nation under arms. We look upon the Rev. W. Bracecamp as an enlightened man and a sincere minister of Christ, but we think he must have forgotten the Bible teach- ing that swords are better turned into plough-shares and pruning hooks.</p>
        <p>Au exhibit contributed by the children of the Bolton elementary schools is one of the most tentertaining features of the great Lancashire Show at Bolton. The children have written essays on homely farmyard topics, and these to the number of some thousands are on view for all who care to inspect them. Quaint, indeed are some expressions used by the child essayist. "The horse," one of the cia says, " is a very useful animal in navigation." Another writes that the cow , "resembles a horse greatly, but is different in the head." Many essays on the pig indicate that an old slander on the domestic habits of the Irish peasantry thrives in Bolton. "The pig is mostly found in ' Ireland, where it is treated like one of the family" ; "people often kiss their pigs ; "in Ireland the pig is treated like a dog, it walks about the house just like a dog, and is generally called "the gentleman what pays t rent," and so on. Another treats sympathetically of :the wallowing habits of the pig. "-When it is a very hot day the sun sometimes dries the pig's skin, which makes it itch all over, aud then the pig rolls in anything that is round it." A. girl in Standard 11. varies her essay on the horse with a touch of original observation: I once saw a horse drinking soapy water." The essays are full of the child touches that are always refreshing to the grown-up, and one of their merits is that they are manifestly genuine. * * * Mr. Grayson, the newly-elected member for Colne Valley, has no need to learn the prayer which is attri- buted to a Scotchtnan, who askecl Heaven to " gie , him a gude conceit o' himsel'." The hon. gentleman (says the London correspondent of the "Liverpool Post and Mercury ") has only been a few days in the House of Commons, and he has already taken the intellectual measure of that Assembly. It is some- what impertinent, not to say indecent, on the part of this young and by no means brilliant person to be talking of the third-class intellectual mediocrity of the House of Commons. Unfortunately, the rhembers of the Labour party seem to ' encourage Mr. Grayson in these childish displays of vanity, but probably a little more experi- ence of the House of Commons will lead him to take a more modest estimate of his own powers. When he talks, as he did to a newspaper correspondent, of Liberals moving uneasily when Mr. Balfour rises to speak, he makes himself exceedingly ridiculous. The Liberals always listen to Mr. Balfour with interest, and often with pleasure, even though he does form part of a " third-class intellectual mediocrity." f Mr. Grayson's idea that they are terrified by the leader of the Opposition is a fair test of his capacity for judg;ng either men or manners in Parliament, his 'opinion is quite untrustworthy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>• • ,</p>
        <p>" 1111111111.11.11111.11</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 3, 1907</p>
        <p>Now is the time when all Liberals entitled to vote should see that their names appear on the register. 'lt is regrettable when people allow themselves to be disfranchised, through their own apathy, especially when claims can be made with so little trouble and without expense. * Crosfield's band has experienced a highly successful season, having achieved the splendid record of five first prizes, three second prizes,and three third prizes. The members may be pardoned for entertaining feel- ings of pride, for their performances have called forth very flattering remarks from the critics. We hope some day to hear of the band lifting tb.e first prize at Belle Vue.</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. T. Rozee, in charge of a Church Association Van, thus reports in the "English Churchman" (July 17th) : RUNCORN, —I found a curate at the van door demanding to know who gave us permission to stand our van in their parish. When I told. him, he wanted to know what I was going to dd. I told him my mission, and then he was not satisfied, and said it would do no good, because the town was steeped in Wesleyanism, which was leading them to hell.'</p>
        <p>• Contentment is the smoother of invention.—GßANT. A widow and her weeds are soon parted.—PuuTzEa. Shallow men believe in luck; strong men in cause and effect. —EMERSON.</p>
        <p>_ The low are beneath morality, the high above it. G. 13. Sri kw. •</p>
        <p>You may lead an ass to knowledge, but you cannot make him think. —MLIIFORD.</p>
        <p>Seeking information is a moment's shame; but not to learn is surely a lasting shame.—MAsAvosui. If a man cannot attain the length of his wishes he may have his remedy by cutting them shorter. - COWLEY. _ _</p>
        <p>_ The misfortune of the artistic temperament is that so many people have the temperament and so few the a rt.—BAGSHOT. - .</p>
        <p>A strong man has living blood in his veins, and he shows his character not by despising —still less in denying his emotions—but in exalting them.—JouN OLIVER 11 ()WM.&amp;</p>
        <p>The Soldiers' Memorial. This week the following insceiption has been placed on the base of the soldiers' memorial in Queen's Gardens ;—" This memorial was unveiled by General Sir Redvers Buller, V.C., on the 21st February, 1907." Go to the Park! The greenhouse in the Park will be opened on Sunday to the public for the show of gloxinias, and will remain open as long as the plants are in bloom. Just now they are at their best and well worth a visit. Local Athletic Success. At the Salford Police Sports, on Saturday, A. 11 Powell, Warrington, won the third prize in the quarter mile cycle race off the 35 yards mark. Chemical Firm's Generosity. On Tuesday, Mr. Cowley, hon. secretary of the Victoria Infirmary, Northwich, received a cheque for £450 from Messrs. Brunner, Mond, and Co., chemical manufacturers, to liquidate the debt on the extension of the institution, which amounts to £6,000. The firm had previously given a contribution of £l,OOO.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BIEVEFIT.—IINDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANcASHIRE.—Advt. 'Harrington Students' Successes. In connection with the July matriculation examina- tion for the Universities of Manchester' Liverpool, Leeds and Sheffield, the following local successes were recorded : First division, J. H. Dodds, Manchester Grammar School; Dorothy Johnson, Warrington Secondary School; Robert Lee, Warring- ton Grammar School. Second division, Percy F. Boardman, Edith Isaac, Roy R. Rice, Elsie N. Swaine, Warrington Secondary School. Arthur Wright, Warrington Granitmar School. Football Club Sports. The Warrington Football Club Sports will be held at Wilderspool on Monday, and, given fine weather, success is assured. Prizes to the value of £75 are offered and there has been a splendid entry including : —Mc Dermott, Dublin; " Burgess, Macclesfield : Beswick, Warrington; Jaffir Rumjahn, Liverpool; Houghton, Warrington; Bennett, Manchester; Yates, Macclesfield ; Souter, Manchester; and many local athletes. The sports will commence at two o'clock. Knutsford Division Liberals. In connection with the linutsfurd Division Liberal Association a demonstration will be held in Alderley Park, near Chelford, on Saturday next. Lord Stanley of Ali:leder will preside, and the speakers will include the Hon. A. L. Stanley, M.P, Sir John Emmott Barlow, Bart., M.P., Mr. Alfred J. King, M.P., Mr. George Harwood, M.P., Mr. James Tomkinson , M.P., and Mr. Henry Vivian, M.P. Further particulars appear in our advertising columns.</p>
        <p>21bs BLACKBERRY &amp; APPLE JELLY. Sd.—ASIILEY'S Advt.</p>
        <p>Important to Voters. It is most important that all Liberals entitled to the vote should make certain that their names are on the register, and take care that they are not disfran- chised by their own neglect. The occupiers' list for the coming year was on August Ist affixed to the doors of places of Ix orship and public buildings, and Liberals should make a point of examining it, and if their names are not on the register they should com- municate with the Liberal agent, Mr. J. Howes, jun., Liberal Club, Bewsey7street, who will give informa- tion as to making cut claima &amp;c. Liberals in the KnutsfGrd Division may obtain forms of claim and all particulars from Mr:Joseph Smith, Liberal registration agent, Cranford-avenue, Knutsford.</p>
        <p>NEW BLACK CURRANT JAM.—ASKLEY'S Sankey st.— Advt.</p>
        <p>Assessment Committee. A nieeting of the Warrington Union Assessment Committee was held at the Workhouse yesterday morning under the presidency of Mr. A. J. Jolley. There were also present : —Dr. Patterson, Messrs. S. Davies, J. Billiage, J. Evans, W. Gill, A. Brooks, J. C. Sutton (clerk), W. H. Woodcock (assistant overseer for, Warrington), E. Foster (assistant overseer for Penketh), H. Houghton (assistant overseer for Newton), and R. R. Meaeock (valuer.) —The Valuer reported that he had visited Newton Paper Mill, and found it closed down. The last of the stock was leaving on the day of visit. The mill was not being dismantled, but rather kept in readi- ness for re-atarting, and could be set going in a - few weeks' time. Having regard to that fact, he thought it would be advisable to let the present assessment stand for a time awaiting possible early re-occupation. Failing this, a reduced assess- ment to cover the warehousing of machinery and plant should be made later in the financial year. With regard to the General Elliot Hotel, Croft, he had carefully gone into the matter and had surveyed the premises and considered the acc)unts. On the, evid- ence adduced he could not recommend a reduction of the assessment. —The report' was adopted. GARDEN TEN-rs.—Tennis, Croquet, and other out- door sports.—J. 5.., GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Adv4</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. W. Bede Houghton. Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mr. W. Bede lionghtou, who passed away at his residence, Fairfield-road, Latchford, at 8.40 on Sunday morning. The deceased ger tleman, who was in his Gist year, had a severe attack of influenza three weeks ago and complications ensued: Last week it was seen that his condition was grave and .he gradually sank and died at the time stated. Mr. Houghton was one of the hest-known Warringtonians and was held in respect by a wide circle. For many years he acted as manager for Messrs. Fell, grocers and tea merchants, Sankey-street, and ultimately he entered into business on his own account at the Conservative Club Buildings. He held the presidency of the Warrington Off-Licence Holders' Association for five years. He was a staunch adherent of the Roman Catholic Church and had been prominently identified with St. Mary's Church, Butter- market-street,ever since the opening of the present hind- some edifice. In politics be was a strong Conservative but he never took part in public affairs,although he had on several occasions been asked to contest various wards for municipal honours. Much sympathy has been expressed with his widow and family in their bereavement. —The funeral took place at the Cemetery on Wednesday in the presence of a large gathering. The Revs. Father Hinde (brother-in-law of the deeeased), and Father Roche performed the last rites. The chief mourners were : —Messrs. Thomas and Walter and Master Harold Houghton (sons), and amongst others presentS were Messrs. Mathias, Unsworth, J. Elude, tV. Hinds and J. Eckersiev. Many beautiful floral tributes were received. TRAVELLING TRUNKS, DRESS BASKETS, eo. Try \J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge-street, War- rington.—Advt. Presentation to a Forester. Opportunity was taken at the meeting df "Court Mercy "(240) of Foresters, on Monday, of recognizing the services to the Order of Bro. George Maybury, P.D.C.R., who for many years has acted as treasurer cf the Court. The District Chief Ranger (Bro. Joseph Owen), in handing a handsome liquor cabinet to Bro. Maybury spoke highly of the services rendered by the recipient in friendly society work. His diligence was not confined to Forestry, but in Sunday school work Bro. Nraybury's cheerful presence had been very encouraging. The hope was expressed that though he was retiring from the position of treasurer the Court would always be able to command his advice, which was invaluable in the furtherance of their Order, (Applause) Bro. L. Davies, district secretary, spoke of the valuable services rendered to the Warrington District by Bro. Maybur), anl especially to Court no, during the past 21 years. Bro. Pater Peacock,P.D.C.R., endorsed, and thanked the retiring treasurer for the readiness he had always displayed in coaching young members, and in inspiring confidence in those who were anxious to become active Foresters. Other members who sp3ke were Bros. T. Smith, P.C.R. A. Catterall, C.R., G. W. Baines, S.B , W. Bulfock P.D.C.R. Harry Rogers and Geo. Reeves, S.C.R. In acknowledging the gift Bros. Maybury expressed his thanks for the handsome present. What he bad done in the furtherance of Forestry had more than amply , been repaid by the progress of the Society of which , they were all so proud.</p>
      </div>
