      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>CRACKERS.</head>
        <p>CRACKERS.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>MOTOR-BUS AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A public meeting was held la Widnes on Monday for the purpose of forming a guarantee fund for £lOO with a view to retaining the motor-bus at present running in the town for another month as an experiment. There was a large attendance, over which Councillor Clarke presided. It was stated that during five days the bus has carried 1,628 passengers, and that the total revenue amounted to £l7,although it had not been systematically worked. After some discussion it was resolved to proceed with the experiments, and before the close of the meeting £Bl was subscribed. Amongst the guarantors are Messrs. Kimmis and Gossage. Mr. A. Webstgr, Alderman Quinn, and Councillors Clarke and Caldwell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>BRA.VE RUNCORN LAD.</head>
        <p>BRA.VE RUNCORN LAD.</p>
        <p>There was an interesting little ceremony at Bethesda Sunday school on Sunday afternoon when John Lister (a scholar of the school) was presented with a beautiful Bible (International Teacher's Edition) with the follow- ing inscription printed in gold :—" Bethesda Congrega- tional Sunday School, Runcorn. Presented to John Lister (by the teachers and scholars) in recognition of his heroic efforts in endeavouring to save from drown- ing Edwin Green, in the Ship Canal, Runcorn, June Ist 1907. 'Ye have done it unto He ' (Alt. xxv. 49). B. B. Folkard, superintendent: F. Chringhall, secretary," The Superintendent made the presentation. He said the teachers and scholars were proud of Lister. He extended to him their bret wishes.</p>
        <p>BIBLE CLASS PICNIC.</p>
        <p>1 On Satwday afternoon the members of the Runcorn 1 Vicar's Bible Class had their annual piqnic, which on this occasion was to Delamere Forest. Over 50 persons joined the trip, and an enjoyable drive was experienced. The Old Choirboys' Association were also having their annual outing at the same place, and an impromotu cricket match was arranged. The Bible Class totalled 11 runs, while in spite of admirable bowling by Riley,. I the former choristers replied with 4:3, thus winning an exceedingly interesting and exciting game. Tea was partaken of at Hardman's, and the sharpened appetites . did ample justice to an excellent spread. After a further ; ramble in the forest,the return journey was commenced and Runcorn was reached in due course, and without mishap. The outinz was one of the most enjoyable yet experienced. The Ven. Archdeacon Wood accompsniol , his Bible Class.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>DEAF MUTE'S THEFT.</head>
        <p>DEAF MUTE'S THEFT.</p>
        <p>i At Widnes on Monday, before Alderman S. Owen and Alderman S. Quinn, Herbert Cookson aged twenty- one, deaf and dumb was charged with having stolen a ' watch and chain, the property of E. Handley. The com- plainant, who is also deaf and dumb, was represented by his wife, who is likewise a deaf mute. Maggie Kenyon,who acted as interpreter,gleaned from Elizabeth Handley, with the aid of a deaf-and-dumb alphabet, that on the 22nd July. Cookson visited Handley, and had ' tea. On the following day his wife placed a watch safely away in the bedroom about eight o'clock in the morning. She went out at two o'clock in the afternoon and returned at half-past four. The watch and chain were afterwards missed, and the prisoner was arrested the following day. He tried to dispose of the watch, which was valued at £5, at a pawnshop, stating in writ- ing that he wanted money to pay his mother's rent. Detective-sergeant Davidson said that the prisoner was a bad youth, and had on one occasion thrashed his father. The accused was tined £l, including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>GENERAL BOOTH AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>GENERAL BOOTH AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>General Booth and his staff had an enthusiastic welcome on Tuesday as he passed through Garston at seven o'clock on his way to Widnes. So dense was the crowd that the cars were compelled to halt, and then proceeded at a slow pace. He arrived in Widnes at 7.45 p.m., and was accorded a noteworthy welcome. Stand- ing up in his motor-car, he waved his greetings back to the crowd. At the Alexandra Theatre another huge demonstration was held, presided over by the Mayor (Alderman S. Owens). The members of the Corpora- tion, as well as the representatives of the Wesleyan i bodies, occupied seats on the platform. Amongst the ! general's officers present were Colonels Whalley and Wilson. The General gave a detailed account of his life's endeavours connected with the Salvation Army, and his efforts to place on a substantial basis his "Darkest England Social Scheme."' He also gave vivid illustrations of thesuccess of his many projects to cope with the problem of bettering the criminal classes, and the working out of their redemption. General Booth stayed in Widnes over night, and on Wednesday morn- ing crossed the Mersey by the Transporter Bridge, en route to Middlevvich.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>At ,these sessions on Monday before Messrs. A. I , Thomas and John Littler, James Smith was charged ' with having wandered abroad and slept in an out- building in Waterloo-road during Sunday night. He said he came from Widnes late on his way to the Workhouse. It was raining and he was very wet, and seeing a light went into the building. He had been in the Workhouse three years. Previously he had worked in Runcorn for 14 years. Prior to that he was ie. the Army and was through the Crimean War and ten years in India. P.C. Waring said he found the defendant sleeping in the kitchen of the Bridgewater Canal stable. Defendant promised to return to the Workhouse and was discharged.—Fiarold Stringer (15), Heath-road, was summoned for having thrown stones in Heath-road on July 21st. P.C. Boyle said he saw him throw stones at a pigeon on a building. The Chairman said stone- throwing by boys was very dangerous. They seldom I hit what they threw at, and often broke a window or hit someone. Defendant promised not to offend again and was fined 2s. M.—Cornelius Duckett, Church-street, ; was fined 2s. 61. for having allowed a donkey to stray lon the highway at Halton on July 22nd. lie said the ! donkey was out in a field and must have been let out by lads. P.C. Whalley proved the case. THE STURDY BEGGA,R GETS ONE NION CH. ; At the Widnes Police Court on Nlon4ay, before the Mayor and Alderman S. Quinn, Thomas Clarke, a power- fnl-looking man, described as of no abode, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly on -Sundav evening. He was further charged with being.—P.C. Stephenson said that on Sunday he saw the prisoner in Victoria-road drunk, behaving in a very disorderly manner. and begging from people. Wnen refusal he used filthy langnage. He went. to Mr. Winfield, of the Railway Hotel, who was standing at his door with another gentleman. He asked for money, and became very disorderly when refused.—Wr. \Vinfleld said the prisoner had been in the habit of coning to his house ; begging. He (witness) had thr.ratened several titll33 to call the police in to him. He was " a confirmed beggar for beer," ani terrorized people into giving him money. He had begged from witness and a gentleman who was standing with him.— ; The ,charge of drunkenness was withdrawn. The Chair- . man'said the magistrates were determined to support I the police in their efforts to stop begging. Prisoner ! must go to gaol for a month with hard labour.—James Smith was charged with begging in Mersey-road.— P.C. Leven said he had received complaints that the prisoner had been begging from people and using had ! language when refused.—Prisoner was committed for one month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>FXTAL BURNS.</p>
        <p>John Middlehurst, sixty-eight, Argyle-street died in the St. Helens Hospital late on INtonday night after very great snffering. Deceased while at work, at the Greenbank Chemical Works, in June last, was badly burnt about the legs by an accident with a caustic pot. He was conveyed to hospital and treated carefully, but after lingering for over a mon'h, death took place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>DRUNKEN BRUTE SENTENCED.</head>
        <p>DRUNKEN BRUTE SENTENCED.</p>
        <p>Martin Murray, labourer, Pigot-street, was charged at St. Helens, on Monday with drunkenness and com- mitting an aggravated assault on Bescie Marshall, a girl of thirteen —Caief-inspector Goodall said that on Saturday night. at a quarter to twelve, the girl was going from Woodville-street to meet her parents, who had been to Prescot by train. In Charles-street a man came running up behind her and tried to throw her down. He swore and threatened her. She screamed "Murder," and ran into C nporation-street, where he was spoken to by two men.--John Carroll, greengrocer, of College-street,spoke to seeing prisoner running after the girl. He shouted, and prisoner went to a man in front of the theatre and assumed a threatening atti- tude. The girl then came up and told Carroll what had happened, upon which Murray ran away, but was caught by Carroll and handed over to the police.— Mr. Goodall said that for some time there had been a good many complaints about females being molested. —Murray was fined 21. 6d. and costs for drunkennms, and sent to gaol for a month's hard labour for the assault.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>GLASSWORKER'S REMARKABLE DEATH.</head>
        <p>GLASSWORKER'S REMARKABLE DEATH.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens Town Hall on Wednesday, Mr. Brig- house, county cOroner, inquired into the death of John Thomas Street, aged twenty-one, of 1. Cooper-street, who died early that morning from injuries received at Messrs. Pilkington's Plate-glass Works, Cowley-hill. Mr. Dickinson rePresented the glass firm. The Coroner said it was a comparatively simple, but a very extra- ordinary case. Decessed was a sandwheeler. and on Tuesday night stopped work at half-psst eleven for supper. At half-past twelve he resumed work along with a youth of seventeen, named Leigh. When they got into the sand store deceased took hold of Leigh by the back of the neck and assumed a dramatic attitude pretending to be the villain in a play. He was holding Leigh when he broke away and ran into the grinding shed, and jumped on to a transporter—a carriage by which the glass and heavy materials were moved. Leigh got safely across, but deceased slipped and fell with his mouth on the handle of the hoisting gear. The handle was about twelve inches long, and it worked serious mischief. Leigh saw that Street was bleeding severely from the mouth, and called Foreman Hartley. who conveyed the unfortunste young man to Dr. Jackson's. Dr. Bailey here found that the injuries were very extensive. His lips were cut, his front, bottom teeth knocked out, and his tongue was almost severed from the roots, and the wind-pipe injured. Dr. Jackson was called up. but the poor fellow gmdnally bled to death, and died in the surgery before three o'clock.—Joseph Leigh, of 35, Central-street, described</p>
        <p>the incident, and Joseph Lyon, of 67, Rigby-street, said he saw deceased slip and fall on the handle which entered his mouth.—William Hartley, of 173,Speakman- road, said he at once realised that his "first aid "would be of no use, and got deceased off to the doctor. Deceased and Leigh were plrfectly friendly, and it was only the result of a lark.—The Coroner said it was a very sad case indeed,but was evidently a clear accident. They were sorry for the young man's parents.—A verdict of Accidental death "was returned. ST. HELENS YOUTH'S FALL.</p>
        <p>Christopher Hall, a young man, living in Friar-street, was charged on remand at St. Helens on Thursday with stealing a quantity of clothing from the unoccupied house of Ar. Reginald flndden, at Haresflnch. Elan was seen hiding some parcels in a field off %foss Bank-road on Friday and Police Constable Span saw him, but as the case was not clear against him Hall was allowed to go. lie was re-arrested by Detective Staveley,and later told Inspector Dunn that he had got through a window and taken the clothing out of the attic. He pleaded that he had behaved himself during the time he had been in work, but he had been out of employment for some time. Hall had been dealt with on eight charges of stealing having carried out a raid on the St. Helens Cricket Club pavilion last year. He was remanded on bail for that offence and during the time he was at liberty he committed several thefts. Hall was sent to gaol for three months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>PAGE</head>
        <p>PAGE</p>
        <p>2. Serial Story by Charles Garvice. Women's Chat and Fashions. 3: Notes on Games, etc. "The Carrier Cat," by Mr. Coulsin Kernahan Football Fixtures. Union Bank of Ma;nchester. 6. Police News. Children's Corner. District News. 7. Cricket and Bowling. Science Notes. Stockton Heath and Padgate News. Stockton Heath Show. The sixth annual show in connection with Stockton Heath Garden Society will be held in the Park, I •Wilderspool, on Saturday next. Lady Brooke, Norton ' Priory, will perform the opening ceremony. The Society's orchestra will play from 2.15 to 5.30 and the ; Walton band will play for dancing from 6to 9 p.m. Lancashire .Cricket. The Lancashire v. South Africans match attracted I a great crowd to Old Trafford on Thursday and yester- day. Batting first Lancashire made 169 (Spooner 57 , and Sharp 52), but the visitors replied vigorously yesterday with the huge score of 42g. Faulkner was itop scorer with 106 not out. In their second innings, I Lancashire collapsed in pitiable fashion before Nourse ' and Snooke, and were all put out for 95. Liverpool Pageant. Next week will be a memorable one in the history of Liverpool for it will witness the historic pageant to commemorate the 700th anniversary of the city. The occasion will be rendered additionally interesting ,by the visit of the Channel Fleet. The Cheshire Lines Committee announce that they will issue cheap excur- sion tickets to Liverp:ol each day from August 3rd te 9th. The Rev. E. J. Willcocks.- wp regret to state that the Rev. E. J. Willcoctks, the respected headmaster of the Warrington Grammar School, is lying seriously ill at his residence, " Heath- field," Latchford. On inquiry - last night we were informed that on Thursday he passed a bad night, and that there was no improvement in his condition A Pint of Whisky before Breakfast Mary Mills applied at the Warrington Police Court yesterday for a separation order from her husband, • Richard Mills, 13, Catherine-street, on the gremud . that he was an habitual drunkard. She alleged that he got drunk six titves a week, and that he coulck • drink nearly a pint of whisky before breakfast. The case was adjourned for a month. Damaging Hay. , Walter Baines, jun., 6, Chester-road, and Linrence Knight, 68, Wilderspool-road, were each fined ss. at the Warrington Borough Court yesterday for damag- ing a stank of hay in a field off Chester-road. —Mr. Browne, who prosecuted, said the defendants were on • the top of the stack for 20 minutes dancing and throwing it about. He added that the total damage , ; done to the stack by different boys was a tenth part of the value of the hay. Theft of Flowers. A boy named John Elvin Heesom, 14, Ship-yard, I Warrington, was summoned at the Borough Court, yesterday for plucking flowers in Queen's-gardens on July 25th. Detective Muakett spoke to seeing the defendant and another boy plucking flowers m the ! gardens. He chased them and caught the defendant, who dropped the flowers he had taken. Defendant said he was very sorry. The Chief-constable said con- siderable damage had been done recentkv, and the • case was brought forward as a warning to other boys. ; Defendant was reprimanded and ordered to , pay the costs. Wife Desertion. At the Warrington Police Court, yesterday, Fi'ederick S. Machell, Howley-cottage, was summoned for deserting his wife.—Defendant did not appear.-- Mr. Arthur Browne, who appeared for the com- plainant, said the parties were only married in February last, and but for the interference of the defendant's mother they might have been happy together. The defendant had sold all the furniture, and had gone to Canada, leaving his wife unprovided for.—The magistrates granted the complainant a separation order with an allowance of 10s. a week. " Branch N.B.W.T.A. • By kind invitation of Mrs. Dodds the "Y " branch spent a very enjoyable afternoon at Penketh House on Thursday. The party numbering 30, left Sankey-street by 'bus other, friends walking. Mrs. Dodds and daughters received their visitors and games were indulged in until tea. After tea Mrs. Beswick proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Dodds and the Rev. C. Harvey Cook humorously se,conded.Mrs. Moore then on behalf of Mrs. Beswick presented Miss Dodds with three volumes in recognition of her services to the" Y" branch.ars.Dodds very suitably respmded to the vote of thanks and thanked Mr. Beswick on behalf of Miss Dodds. The party then adjourned to the gardens, the time being spent in tennis and other games and •in listening to the gramaphone. The party reached Warrington about 7.30 after spending a most enjoyable afternoon. ..e Labour Bureau. During the month of July there have been regis- tered 163 persons for employment, making 910 registered during the present year. Fifty-six situa- tions have been offered by employers, and employment has been found for 27 men, 9 youths, 6 women, and 4 girls, making a total of 46. Of the 46 situations pound 14 were for temporary work, and :32 for employ- ment of a more permanent character. There are now on the register out of employment 171 persons. The following is a list of the different trades :—General labourers, 21 ; wire trade labourers, 10; iron trade labourers, 24; building trade labourers, 3 ; carters and vanmen, 9 ; handymen, 5 ; engine drivers and firemen, 12; other sundry trades, 15; youths, 15; girls, 8; women for charring, and daily work, 44; girls for shops and restaurants, 5. During July con- siderably more have registered their names for emplonent at the Bureau than during any of the previous months of this year (except January when the number was 174). This is attributed to quiet trade in a few of the large works, and to two other works where the men were put on short time. Some have unwisely preferred to throw themselves out of work rather than remain on short time. FEW PEOPLE HAVE EYES ALIKE. Thousands have ruined their sight by neglect, by wearing other people's glasses or glasses for which they were not carefully and thoroughly tested.—JOHNSTON WATSON. Eyesight and Spectacle Warrington .Low Specialist, 81, Bridge at., Warringto. Low prices and sattisf action guaranteed.—Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <p>. , • . _ _ _-____. ___- - - - -</p>
        <p>- -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWFON</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWFON</p>
        <p>OLDEST POST-NIISPRESS IN TEIE WORLD.</p>
        <p>Acting as post-mistress at linishant, Ayrshire, at 92 years of age, and in the G wernment's employment for 54 years, Mrs. Limond has just received a congratu- latory letter from Nfr. Sydney Buxton, the Post-master- General. Mrs. Limond has lived under five sovereigns, and has occupied the same house in Minishant fot 64 years. She writes a clear, legible hand, and her books and statements are models of neatness.—Nirs. Limond is aunt to Mr. John Limoud, of Wargrave-road, Earlestown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>STOLE THE GOOSE.</head>
        <p>STOLE THE GOOSE.</p>
        <p>At Newton Petty Sessions last Thursdayjohn Martin was charged with stealing a goose, worth Ss. the, property of William Houghton, Chapel House, Burton- ' wood.—Prosecutor deposed to missing the goose, and John King, collier, said he bought it from the prisoner, who saia it was his mother's. He gave him 3s. for it.— P.C. Cartruell deposed to apprehending the prisoner,who admitted the offence.—Prisoner pleaded guilty, but said it was not much of a goose—it only weighed about Ilbs.—Fined 10s. or 14 days imprisonment. CRUSHED TO DEATH.</p>
        <p>Thomas Turne—t-,-C-011ie-r,aged 2S, of Twenty-eight-row, Haydock, was crushed to death on ‘Vednesday afternoon at the Southport Pit, Parr, belonging to Nlessrs. R. Evans and Co. Turner resided with his mother. Tie was working as usual getting coal. About half-past ten yesterday morning his mate left him in the working place to take a full box of coal out to the haulage. Ten minutes later he cot back, but found deceased's lamp extinguished and no movement of any sort. Re called, but got no ansiver, an on further investigation found that a huge stone had fallen out of the roof and brought the place tlown on Turner. After considerable trouble the poor man was dug out, when it was found that his back and left leg had been broken, and that his death had been a particularly sudden one. ODDFELLOWSFIIP.</p>
        <p>The quarterly Tne-e-tin:OTtbe—Va-tfonal Independent Order of Oddfellows of the Warrington District was held at the Prince of Wales Hotel, Earlestown, on Saturday. The Grand Master (8r0..1. W. Meachin) and the Deputy Grand Master (Bro. Thomas Litton), took their positions before a good assembly of delegates, representing the four lodges belonging to the district. The Grand Master, in opening the meeting, gave all the delegates a hearty welcome, and said he hoped the busi- ness to be done would result in the welfare of the district and the N.1.0.F. in general. It was decided that the district should hold its anniversary, and a com- mittee from the lodges was appointed to make the necessary arrangements.</p>
        <p>_ EARLESTOWN MAN I.‘,IIIZTED AT CUnTiL7. A wedding of interest to local people took place on Thursday last weak at Carlisle P esbyterian Church, the contracting parties being Mr. Jo eph Hodkinson, estate agent, of Crow-lane West. E tries own, and Miss Jeatima, Grieve, third d tughter of Mr. A. Grieve, of Carlisle. The bride is well known in this district, having for some years been the first assistant at the Manor School (Tnfants' department). The bride was charmingly attired in white silk, wraith of orange blossom, and embroidered tulle veil. She carried a lovely shower bouquet composed of white sweet peas, &amp;c., the gift of the bridegroom. She was accompanied by her father, who gave her away. The bride was followed by her niec6 ( \Liss Margaret Grieve) and nephew (Master Harry Williamson). The former was in white silk and carried a basket of flowers (the gift of the bridegroom). There were two bridesmaids, NEVI Grieve (sister of the bride) and Miss Sole. They wore dresses of helio eolienne, with hats to match, and carried hem:pats of shaded heliotrope sweet peas, the gifts of the bride- groom. The Rev. James Christie. 8.A., condactei the service, and the organist played" The wedding march" and other bridal music. Afterwards a reception was held at Esk House, Stanwix, the residence of the bride's parents. A large number of valuable and useful presents. were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>LABOUR DEMONSTRATION AT ASPITON. MR. J. A. SEDDON, ALP., AND TIIE GOVERNMENT.</head>
        <p>LABOUR DEMONSTRATION AT ASPITON. MR. J. A. SEDDON, ALP., AND TIIE GOVERNMENT.</p>
        <p>The Newton Division Labour party scored a huge success on Saturday in the demonstration held at the park, Ashton-in-Nlakerfield. Ashton has been the scene of a good many gatherings of the representatives of Lebour, but Saturday's demonstration was memorable in that the members of the Miners' Federation were joined by large bodies of organised workers representing all classes of labour. Close on 10,000 men and youths walked in Procession accompanied by nine brass bands, the townships represented being Ashton, Haydock' St. St. Helens, Newton, garlestown, Garswood, Golborne, Billinge, Rainford, Brynn, and Burtonwood. The miners' lodges, whicl have largely increased in numbers during the past twelve months, formed the mejority, but the Gasworkers' and General Labourers' Union, steam enginemakers, boilermakers, Amalgamated Society of Engineers, machine workers, carpenters and joiners, bakers, waggon makers, smiths and strikers, painters, glass-workers, and the Typographical Society were present in large numbers. The afternoon was favour- able in every way, and three of the local Members of Parliament attended, along with prominent Labour. men and Socialists from all parts of the division. Mr. J. A. Seddon. M.P. for Newton, on rising to speak, met with a great ovation. He referred to the emancipation that was wroughefor many workers at the last election. When they went to Parliament the Labour men were told they knew nothing about the high game of politics. That might be trite; but there was a branch of education which the Labour mon had had, and which the other parties had not. Most Labour men had had more dinner-times than dinners— (laughter)—and if they wanted sympathy and activity on behalf of Labour it was to the men who had sprung from the loins of democracy and knew what an empty cupboard meant that they must look. He found that whenever a question was brought forward that touched the pockets of the employers every Liberal and every Tory mineowner and capitalist, came together as by a natural law to oppose it. When they asked for the deletion of the serious and wilful misconduct clause from the new Compensation Act it was a Radical of Radicals that at once jumped up to oppose them. On grand committees capital always looked after itself, and by direct and also indirect opposition made the Parliamentary life a farce. The immediate work of the Labour Party was to alter the ',political machine, and to make the House of Commons the national workshop, net merely the best club in the world, the national gas works, but the national workshop where Labour measures would be passed for the benefit of the workers. Some one had shouted, " What about Lord Cromer ? " Well, all he could say was that the proposal to give a man who had been drawing a large salary, and had a handsome pension, such a sum as £50,000, was a true example of how Parliament treated the rich, and at the same time its leaders always said they had nothing to spare for the poor. For twenty-five years old age pensions had been dangled before the people of this country. Every party, Liberal and Tory, had appealed to the country on the promise of old age pensions. Mr. Asquith had laid a nest-egg as a sort of preparation for old age pensions. At least Mr. Asquith had said that, but fie had not got the nest-egg yet. - (Laughter). He s tid that if trade kept good for another year or two he would have the nest-egg. That was where he stood. It was something like as substantial a statement as if he (Mr. Seddon) were to say to any of the men who were fond of back- ing their fancy," Well,i t you bac4-. Las, winner next year you will win." (Laughter.) It was all very funny. He (Mr. Seddon) had been in the country a few times, and he had generally found that the nest-egg was a" mug" egg. (Renewed laughter and applause.) They wore not to have old age pensions this year, nor the year after, an.l he had a very strong suspicion Viet unless they strengthened the hands 'of the Labour Party it Parliament they would not only have to wait until next year, but they would have to wait for the great hereafter when they had all entered glory (laughter and applause). The Labour ' party said that if old-age pensions were good for their Cromers they were good for the poor old veterans of labour. (Applause). They asked working men not to be gulled by the promises of their opponents. For the first time since the extension of the franchise industrial democracy had got its head above water ; for the first time it had got an articulate voice on the floor of the , House of Commons. The old parties were standing aghast. Both the Liberalsand the Tories did not know ' which way to turn. There werergood men in each party and when the great dividing•issue came they would find the best men joining with the democratic forces in the country, and capital would be on the other side arrayed against them. The battle had begun, and could never end until they had entered into their possessions. The only hope of the workers was to get back to that gift which was given to them by Mother Nature—the land of their birth. Instead of allowing a few people to annex the source of all wealth for their pleasure and profit, let it come into the use of the whole of the people of the country. (Applause). •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>CROSFIELD'S BAND.</head>
        <p>CROSFIELD'S BAND.</p>
        <p>High Praise.</p>
        <p>Crosfleld's Band holds the record for the present season's successes, and it is made up as follows : five first prizes, three second prizes, and three third prizes.</p>
        <p>The current issue of the" Musical Mail "says : " Crosfields ie the finest brass band in the country." "The Brass Band News,"referring to the band at York Contest, July 20th, says : "It will be a long time before any one of that vast audience forgets the entrancing performance of Cros- field's Band. Well might the Judge (Mr. 0. Hulme) say 'they had gained the full marks, and not a slip or fault to record.'</p>
        <p>It TY-as-a—d-r-e;m of loveliness. Irwell Springs were not far behind; beautiful tone, splendid time, but their soloists do not shine as Crosfield's did. 'TI Trovatore ' as played by either of these, is worth a shilling of anybody's money, and I would gladly pay it once a week for that piece alone. Crosfields' playing was as free and natural as the birds singing." The band will play the following programme in the Park on Sunday at 3.15 p.m. : Hymn Selected March "Viva Pettee " Rimner. Overture " Tancredi " Rossini. Selection (Bell, Vue Test Piece July 13th). Selection " Wagner" Arr Halliteell. Selection "Verdi " Arr Round. " Golq Save the King." Con lucter, Mr. G. F. Bedforth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>WATER POLO.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO.</p>
        <p>This has been a disastrous week for the Warrington water polo team, for they have suffered two decisive reverses at the hands of Wigan. On Wednesday even- ing they journeyed to the colliery town with a very weak team and were hopelessly outplayed, Wigan triumphing by fourteen goals to nil. There was a crowded attendance at the Warrington baths on Thursday evening to witness the return match. Warrington were again poorly represented and the wonder was that the score against them wns not higher than, seven goals to two Wigan combined 'splendidly and were smart on the ball but their shooting often lacked judgment. In the first half Warrington defended the shallow end. Wigan started in determined fashion and not many minutes elapsed before Critchley registered two goals. Warrington made a plucky fight and Allen and W.Ashton equalised the scores. Wigan kept up a hot bombard- ment, and S. Walker, the home goal keeper, was cheered for some magnificent saves. His fine display was indeed the outstanding feature of the first half. Half-time arrived with the teams equal. On re-starting Wigan carried all before them and goals were added by Critchley (two), Bentham and Saunders (two). Warrington never had a look in and Wigan ran out winners by seven goals to two. Prior to the polo match the second round of the works squadron competition was decided, the Corpora- tion defeating Sankey Wire Mills and Rylands' register- ing a victory at the expense of Monks, Hall and Co.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>NO RELIGIOUS TESTS.</head>
        <p>NO RELIGIOUS TESTS.</p>
        <p>Freeing the Welsh Secondary Schools.</p>
        <p>The new regulations for secondary schools in Wales, which came into force on August Ist, present three main features of interest—namely, increased grants, the abolition of religions tests, and greater freedom to local i ties in the formation of the curriculum and time- table of their schools.</p>
        <p>The liberal snirit in which the regulations have been framed is aimirably expressed in a lucid introductory note by Nlr. Alfred T. Davies, the secretary of the nesly-constiteted Welsh Department of the Board of Education. He says : The Board wish to see every school made accessible to every class of the people. The provisions contained in schemes made under the Welsh Intermediate Educa- tion, 1889, and under the Endowed Schools Act, already ensure that scholarships consisting of exemption from tuition fees shall be available to scholars from public elementary schools applying for admission to schools regulated by schemes under those Acts, and it is now laid down that in the case of any other school, where a fee is chtirged, a proportion of places shall be open without payment of fee to scholars from public elemen- tary schools who apply for admission. With .egard to the increased grants, it is pointed out that the eddition.ll funds placed at the disposal of the Boa.d for the school year enable them to raise the scale from £2, .£3, £l, and £5 for the first, second, third, and fourth years, to £3 155., £1 55., £4 151., and £5 res- pectively. The fonds for the school year 1908-7, while not large enough to admit of payment for that year at these incressed rates, admit, homever, of a substantial bonus in augmentation of the grants already payable, The Board enumerates in the regulations the subjects lof the schlol course, and specify the minimum time to be given to certain snbjects. They point out the place • which the Welsh lananage should take in the curri- culum, having regard (1) to its educational value as a language, and to the ease with which children are brought into touch-with its literature ; and (2) to the pressing need for hi-lingual teachers for the elementary schools. Where, however, all the subjects taught are of educe.tional value, and where continuity of instruc- tion and adequate time is secure" for each, the board may approve a tnodified curriculum and time-table.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WEEK ENDING 27TH JuLy,ll9o7. Total. Male. Female Births Legitimate 54 2G 28 „ Illegitimate 1 1 Deaths</p>
        <p>Excess of births over deaths, 42. The ages of mortality were :—Under 1,5; 1-5, 0; 5-15, 0; 15-25, 1 ; 25-65, 3; 65 and upwards, 4. Total, 13. The deaths included :-1 from phthisis. 1 from cancer, 1 from pneumonia, 1 premature birth, 9 from all other causes.</p>
        <p>The following cases of infoctions disease have been reported during the week :—scarlet fever, 8 cases; enteric fever, 1 case.</p>
        <p>Number of patients in hospital :—Scarlet fever, Borough 33, Newton 1 ; total, 34. Enteric fever, Borough 1, Newton 1. total 2. Diphtheria, Borough 2, Newton 2 ; total 4. Other diseases, 0. Totals, Borough 33 ; Rural District 0, Newton, 4; total, 40. Weather Return —lean temperature 58 Fah.,and there- fore 7 deg. lower than the preceding week. Maximum temperature 77 Fah.on the 21st Jul v. ginimum tempera- ture 49 on the 22nd July. Rainfall '52 inch. General direction of wind S.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>P. E. GAPFIKIN, Acting Nledical Officer of Health.</head>
        <p>P. E. GAPFIKIN, Acting Nledical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>WEITCE CITY, MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>The holiday seasons have commenced, mid for those who are looking for outdoor recreation and amusement there can he no better place than that City of Pleasure, the White City at Old Trafford, Manchester. Many excursion part4s have been arranged and no doubt many more thonsands will avail themselves of visiting this great amusement enterprise during the August Bank Holiday Season. The general expression is one of satisfaction. To make it more interesting to all, the Management have secured several new and entertaining free attractions for their visitors, so that the simple price of admission charge will not only permit the visitor to enjoy the beautiful promenade, illuminations, &amp;a., but the Circus, the Venetian Fête, the Water Pantomime, can be seen without extra cost. Those, however, who desire to visit the many Side Shows, can do so at a nominal fee. The Restaurant and Catering Department are in full swing, and fully equipped to accommodate those who desire Teas, Luncheons and Dinners, at reasonable rates, quick service and giving entire satisfaction. In the Magnificent Ball-room, the Roller Skating Rink, full preparations have been made to accommodate the lovers of these enjoyments. A visit to' the White City will amply repay all. No mattqr how large the crowd may be there is appl- accommodation. Raid or shine it is the most delight' fill holiday resort, there is plenty of cool delightfu shade as well as plenty of cover in case of rain.</p>
        <p>Warrington Agricultural Show. The eighteenth annual show of the Warrington and District Agricultural Society vill he held in Walton Park on Thursday, 15th August. Prizes, cupsin p Presidenta &amp;c . years,agri evgives s offeredas jr st to raj theciGilbert b :Ist ori oef ne aboutnaclui, 7lr sut 0. e; As value ,f,5, and the vice-president, Col. Leigh, a silver cup value .15. while Lady Green all santadplettown,) silverß ret h e r tcol Inpit*ss:ahrmiozgvdeeiayshl gives hasMtthore. been tFh six: monthsroeffgeuroffers arl blacksmithoythrsrteehhefoodrreat thereabouts be spacialot buhete.sstprevious to the date of the show itiKineilnrcitnveogrrttaahinnya specifiedeiuo.r,.sraemscslersass.sensG..oulAaldnr:d AlR.Te..ssArsin. Whittaker and Messrs. Day and Sons also offer sscHpeuecngiaryhle prizes. Medals are offered by the Shire Horse Society, the Hunters' Improvement Society, and the Hackney Horse Society. There are classes for shires, hunters, hackneys, harness horses, jumpers, , tradesmen's and tenant farmers' turnouts, cattle, pigs, cheese and butter.—The Yorkshire Hussars Band will be in attendance.— There will also be a flower show, the entries for which close to-day. Particulars will be found in our advertising columns. ofseoro srf eg:a ::w2hesi aepc cahe duringhce hi al prize ze</p>
