      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>SEY Droiat CHILDREN-</head>
        <p>SEY Droiat CHILDREN-</p>
        <p>As promised two or three weeks since, I am sending several of the pocket mirrors to membens of our Corner, for the best weekly stories, etc., sent me. These little ,prizes will be awarded weekly for the best contributions sent to me.</p>
        <p>I expect many of you are away from home at this time, enjoying yourselves by the sea and in the country. Well, I must not grum- ble, as I have already had my little time this year among the Welsh mountains. I wonder how many of you know Barmouth, Aberystwyth, Penmanmaer, or Snowdon; as soan as you are old enough, try and see some of these delight- ful places. You are sure to enjoy yourselves amongst the warm-hearted Welsh people. Well, I mustn't go on talking and thinking of my holiday, YOUR holiday is the one to interest you now. With all good wishes and love from UNCLE NED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>...--..-4.....---</p>
        <p>THE SHILLING AND THE SOVEREIGN. Some years ago the lateAbukv Buecleuch purchased a cow from a prsoirin the neigh- bourhood of D,alkeith, an left order ii to send it to his palace the following morning. Accord- ing to agreement, the cow was sent, and the duke, who happened to he in his morning dress, walking in the avenue, espied the little fellow ineffectually attempting to drive the animal forward to its destination. The boy, sot knowing the duke, bawlad out to him: "Come here, mun, an' help us, an', aa sure as onything, I'll give you half I get!" (4, _ This last solicitation had the desired effect. The duke went and lent a helping hand. "And now," said the duke as they trudged along, "how much do you think ye'll get for this job?" "Oh, dinna ken," said the boy, "but I'm sure o' something, for the folk up by the house are good to a' bodies." As they approached the house, the duke darted from the boy, and entered by a different way. He called to a servant, and put a sovereign into his hand, saying, "Give that to the boy that brought the cow." The duke returned to the avenue, and was soon rejoined by the boy. "Well, how much did you get?" said the duke. "A shilling," said the boy, "an' there's half o' it t' ye." "But you surely got more than a shilling," said the duke. "No," said the boy. with utmost earnestness, "an' don't you think it's plenty." "I do not," said the duke, "there must be some mistake; and, as I'm acquainted with the duke, I think I can get you more." The boy consented: back they went. The duke rang the bell, and ordered his servants to assemble. •</p>
        <p>"Now," said the duke to the boy, "point me out the person that gave you the shilling." "It was that chap there with the apron," pointing to the butler. The delinquent confessed, fell on his knees, and attempted an apology, but the duke, inter- rupting him, ordered him to give the boy the sovereign, and quit his service instantly. "You have lost," said the duke, "your money, your situation, and your character, by your covetousness. Learn henceforth that honesty is the best policy." The boy by this time recognised his assist ant in the person of the duke, and the duke was so delighted with the sterling worth of the boy that he ordered him to be sent to school, kept there, and provided for at his own 'expense. ELIZABETH BREARLEY. '</p>
        <p>HELPFULNESS. As we meet and touch each day The many travellers on our way, Let every such brief contact be A glorious, hel,pful ministry. The contact of the soil and seed. Each giving to the other's need, Each helping on the other's best, And blessing each, as well as blest. BENJAMIN FARRAR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>HOW SHE SAVED HIM PAYING DUTY,</head>
        <p>HOW SHE SAVED HIM PAYING DUTY,</p>
        <p>A gentleman had occasion to travel, to the different Australian ports on his way home from India, and, not wishing to be encum- bered with much luggage, he only took his portmanteau with him. Before leaving India he purchased a valuable shawl to take home to his wife. On landing at. Adelaide, where he had occasion to stay two days, his portmanteau was searched, and he had to pay duty on the shawl. At Melbourne again he was charged. "But I have paid duty at Adelaide," he demurred.</p>
        <p>"Can't help it, sir," replied the official; "you must pay again—this is another colony." Having to go on to Sydney, he thought he would be all right there, Sydney being a free- trade port. On being asked what his luggage contained, he mentioned the shawl. "Is your wife on board V' was the query. "No; she is in gl an d." "Oh, then, you will have to pay duty." The man protested that he had paid twice already. "Can't help it, sir; this is another colony."</p>
        <p>So he paid again. Going up the-river to Brisbane, he mentioned the fact of having to pay so often for the shawl. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Oh, my dear sir," said a lady who over- heard him, "I can wear the shawl until we go on shore, and then they cannot charge you for it" The gentleman thought this an excellent plan, but when be got on shore the woman was gone, and alas! the shawl too.</p>
        <p>"sever interfere with a man who is sneezing. When so engaged he requires to devote his whole attention to the business.</p>
        <p>4 _</p>
        <p>When -Dublin Cathedral was reopened, after restoration at the expense of a Mr. Wise, the archbishop took for his text, "Go thou and do like Wise."</p>
        <p>"Is your horEe perfectly genus:, Mr. Dab- ster ?" "Perfectly gentle, air. The only fault he has got, if that be a fault, is a painful habit of extending his hinder hoofs now and then." "By extending his hinder hoofs you don't mean kicking, I hope 4" "Some people call it kicking, Mr. Green. But it's only a slight reaction of the muscles."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
