      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>THE THEFT DURING A FIRE.</p>
        <p>William Cliff, of no settled abode was charged on remand at the Warrington Police abode, on Saturday with stealing seven watches and other jewellery, of the value of £7, from the shop of Benedict, Tyrer, Butter- market-street. He pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable stated that between 8.45 and 9 p.m. on Thursday, July 18th, a fire was discovered upon the premises of Mr. Ty rer. Prisoner was engaged with others in assisting to remove various articles from the shop and whilst doing this he apparently took the watches, ts:c. Informa- tion came to the knowledge of Supt. Monaghan, who arrested prisoner in a lodging-house with the property in his possession. Prieoner was remanded last Saturday so that inquiries could be made into his antecedents,but the police have failed to find any previous convictions against him for felony. Prisoner, who had nothing to say, was committed for two months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>MILITIAILLV'S ESCAPADE.</head>
        <p>MILITIAILLV'S ESCAPADE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Monday, Michml Duffy, Oldham, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly at Bank Quay Station on Saturday night, and also with assaulting Georgia Beswick, a porter. Beswick stated that he was on the low level platform at seven o'clock on Saturday night, when he saw prisoner chasing a young gentleman round the platform: Witness told Duffy to be quiet, and prisoner then got hold of witness by the coliar and topped him in the face. He also kicked witness. Foreman Bennett came to his assistance, and they tied prisoner's legs to prevent him kicking. A policeman was sent for, and prisoner was locked up. Duffy was mad drunk.—Prisoner said he had too much drink, and did not remember anything. The Chief Constable (Al r. Nicholls) said prisoner had been serving with the Militia during the past month, ind on Saturday he finished his, training. He had Hs. 24. d. in his possession.—A fine of 10s. and costs was inflicted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>FATHER'S DISGRACEFUL CONDUCT.</head>
        <p>FATHER'S DISGRACEFUL CONDUCT.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Tuesday before Mr C. Jackson Holmes and other magistrates, James Scott Purvis was charged with neglecting his tour children in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering and injury to health and with assaulting his daughter Grace Scott Purvis on the 20th July and other days. Prisoner pleaded guilty to neglect but not guilty to the assaults.—Mr. Browne prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. and said that defendant's wife through his neglect bad, unfo; tunately, on Friday the -27th of July attempted to commit suicide by throwing herself into the river Mersey. Prisoner had worked for Mr. McDowell, coach and cab painter, and also for Mr. Hayward, Wilderspool-causeway, and was getting from Us. to £2 per ten days, but he only gave his wife Cs. 6d. They lived in Cumberland-street and they had four children and a baby four months old. inspector Cox had had the case under his supervision since 1903. Defenaant received army reserve pay. Mr. Browne said that army reserve pay was a curse, because prisoner bad gone on the spree seven times out of ten and had not given his wife a halfpenny.—Prisoner said that the last time he received his pension, which was -22 ss. 6d., be gave his wife £2 and kept ss. 6d. for himself.—The Chairman ordered prisoner to go to gaol for tlfree months and said that it was a most disgraceful case. It was prisoner's fault that his wife attempted to commit suicide. Defendant ought to think himself very lucky in being dealt with so leniently.</p>
        <p>SWEET-HUNGRY. For stealing a pair of boots, value 45., from the dwell- ing-house of James Connor, Foundry-street, a little boy named Wiiiiam Ling, Morris-court, Warrington, was oraered by the Warrington magistrates on Wednesday, to receive six strokes wifh the bircbrod. When asked why he took the boots the boy replied that he was hungry. The money was afterwards spent on sweets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>FAMILY DESERTION.</head>
        <p>FAMILY DESERTION.</p>
        <p>Joseph Turner,'o,--o'f Wigan, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Wednesday with having run 'away Irma the borough and left his two children -chargeable to the Union. Mr. B. Shearer relieving officer said prisoner decamped on the 24th of July and left Lis two children chargeable to the Union. He was working as a collierat Wigan where he was apprehended. In October 1599 a maintenance order of 10s. a week was granted to his wife, but he had not given her a penny.— Mrs. Turner who lives at 2 Cartwright-street,corrouorated this statement and said that she kept house for her brother and she could not stand pt isoner's conduct any longer. He came home drunk and behaved scandalously. —Pr;soner was sent to gaol tor three months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>THEFT FROM A PAWNSHOP.</head>
        <p>THEFT FROM A PAWNSHOP.</p>
        <p>Edward Mull;s.ls,llial-street was charged on remand on Thursday before Mr.Ctiarles Wu ite(chairman),and Mr. j.Etymer Yonug,with stealing from the shop of Benedict Tyrer,Buttermarket-street,on the 29th of July,a number of dress materials to the value of 7s. tid.—Prisoner pleaded trlOt guilty.—Charles Littler, manager for Mr. Tyrer 4leposed to having seen prisoner in the shop on the date mentioned. He went out and when he came back both the prisoner and the articles were missing.— Thomas Mannion, assistant to Mr. Littler, manager at Mr. Tyrer's, said when he was going from the shop he saw Mullins end made room for him to pass with the goods under his coat.—P.C. Gilchrist said at about five minutes past five on Monday evening he was on duty in Sankey-street when he saw prisoner and asked him to come with him to the Police Station and Mullins said : " Why ;• I have done nothing." The Constable said he had better come down wit,u him. When they arrived at the Police Station, P.C. Gilchrist charged him with the theft and prisoner denied it. —The Chief Constable said prisoner had a very bad character. He had been coavicted on 14 previous occasions.—Mullins was sent to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. LITTLETON DEWHORST, J.P.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. LITTLETON DEWHORST, J.P.</p>
        <p>Profound regret was occasioned in Lymm and district by the death of Mr. George Littleton Dewhurst, J.P., •ex-high sheriff of Cheshire, who passed away at the residence of his cousin, Mr. Arthur Dewhurst, Cherry- lane, Lynn!), on Friday evening last. The deceased geutlernan, who was 44 years of age, was on a visit to Lymm when he was seized with illness which unfortun- ately developed into pneumonia. He was attended by Dr. Burrows, of Lymm, Dr. Jones, of Altrincham, and several specialists, but their efforts were unavailing. Mr. Dewhurst was one of the most highly respected public men in Cheshire and was head of the firm of Messrs. G. and I. Devrhurst and Co., cotton spinners, Manchester. He was a member of the Lymni Sub- Administrative Education Committee and a governor of the Grammar School and rendered valuable service in each capacity. Standing Oft. siin. high, he was a tine specimen of an English gentleman and took a keen interest in sport. Be was presiden:. of the Lymm and 'Oughtrington Cricket clubs, and as a mark of respect both organisations cancelled their matches on Saturday. In politics Mr. Dewhurst was a staunch Conservative, being a vice-presideut of the Lymm Conservative Club. He was a devoted Churchman and during the time of his residence at Beechwood regularly attended the Parish Church, where he held the office of churchwarden for some years. He was a warden of St. Peter's, Oughtrington—of which church he was the patron—for the years 1904-5 and G. His wife wos the daughter of the late Sheriff Davidson, of Midlothian, and Dr. Randall Davidson,the present Archbishop of Canterbury 'belongs to the same family. Great sympathy is felt with the widow and children—a son and a daughter—in their bereavement. Sympathetic references to the death of Mr. Dewhurst were made on Sunday at Lymm Church by the Rev. G. B. Thurston and at Oughtrington Church by the Rev. E. P. Wethered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>TUE FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>TUE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>Impressive scene; were witnessed at the funeral which took place at Lymm Church on Tuesday. The whole village was in mourning, business establishments being closed and blinds lowered. The procession was of imposing iimensions. First walked a number of the deseased's workmen carrying wreaths, and conveying two large bearers laden with other floral tributes, of which there were about is hundred. The coffin, almost hidden from view by flowers, was borne upon a Disney ,car. Then followed on foot the chief mourners and close friends of the family, the servants, the tenantry, deputations from Manchester and Preston, magistrates, Lymm and Oughtrington church officials, Lymm Urban District Council, members of the Men's Club, Lymm Conservative Club, Lyn= CrickeS,-Club, Lymm Trades- men'9 Association, representatives of other bodies in which the deceased gentleman was interested, and private carriages. There was a large attendance of ir.inting and county gentlemen, including the Earl of The combined choirs of Lymm and Oughtrington Churches met the coffin at the lych-gate and preceded it in o church, singing " 0 God, our help in ages past." The service was impressively conducted by the Rector (Bev. G. B. Thurston), and the Rector of Oughtrington ( Rev. E. P. Wethered), who were accompanied by the liev. A. T. Davidson (vicar of Scorton), Rey. F. C. Bozman (curate-in-charge at New Springs, Haigh, I formerly curate of Lymtn), Rev. D. R. Davies (curate l of Lyram), and the Rev. A. L. Foyster (curate of I Oughtrington). The choir chanted Psalm xxxix. and also sang the hymns "On the Resurrection morning" arid " Fignt the good fight." The organist played the Dead March in " Saul " as the coffin was borne to the animly yault (which , had been tastefully lined with flowers, mingled with greenery) by the folLwing ,employees at the deceased gentleman: Messrs. G. Bennett, J. Cartwright, F. Parris, R. Davenport, C. Hayes, G. Holt, G. Simpson and G. Bolt. The dark oak coffin bore the following inscription :- 4' George Littleton Dewburst, eldest son of the late George Bakewell Dewhurst, born July 12th, 1863, died Julylo7."</p>
        <p>Ki DN EY is a deceptive diseaie—thousands TROUBL, have it and don't know it. If you want quick results you can make ~00 mistake by using Dr.Kilmer's Swamp-Root, the grea kidney remedy. At chemists in Is. I,ld. and 2s. 3d. sizes mple bottle by post free, also pamphlet telling you .:now to find out if you have kidney trouble. Dr. Kilmer f:70., Dept. 329 Temple Chambers, London. B.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>TRADE UNIONISTS AND SOCIALISM,</head>
        <p>TRADE UNIONISTS AND SOCIALISM,</p>
        <p>Sir, —I was most surprised at the dogmatic tone assumed by your correspondent Socialist Trades Unionist in his assertions relating to the work and objects of the I.L.P. His statement that it is the work of the LL.P. which has made a Labour party possible is certainly something new-to myself at least, and his statement that there is not a single member of the Labour party in the House of Commons who will not declare that the I.L.P. section of his supporters were the best workers is open to question. There are many Labour M.P.'s whom I could name whose bitterest enemies and most persistent persecutors were to be found among the members of the I.L.P. for the simple reason that they refused to sacrifice their manhood and perjure themselves by signing a ticket which they could not in the interest of the workers cmscientiously adhere to. I believe in giving honour where honour is due, and when the Liberal party or any other politicians have passed measures for the betterment of the workers let them receive the praise to which they are justly entitled. Why should the workers I spend their time and energy on an impossible ideal (that the means of production and distribution shall be owned and controlled by the government and that competition shall be abolished) which closely and carefully examined means the elemination of all individuality from everyone but the governing class, and' makes the rest• of the community the slaves nnd tools of the party in power, be it good, bad, or indifferent that may by the chance vote of the people happen to obtain a majority. As a free and though some what illiterate subject of a free country I claim for myself and fellow trade unionists the right to use our judgment and influence, faulty though it may be, and to follow along lilies which we believe calculated to produce the best results for ourselves and fellow workers whether they may be Liberal, Labour or Pro- gressive Labour League.</p>
        <p>LABOURER.</p>
        <p>"IMPULSIVE YOUTH."</p>
        <p>Sir,—We older and therefore more experienced trades unionists are becoming a little weary of the persistent attacks on our political beliefs by the "Socialistic " young men in a hurry. A most glaring example of these offensive tactics appeared in your last week's " Voice of Labour" column, where a writer, under the nom de plume of " S icialist Trades Unionist" audaciousl3; claims that the " Warrington Trades Unionists " have nothing to lose by co-operation with the 1.L.P., rather have they a good deal to gain. This ardent young enthusiast e vidently has not yet learned the plain fact that the overwhelming majority of the trades unionist rank and file are either Liberals or Tories.</p>
        <p>The Independent Labour Party is an avowedly Socialistic political organisation, and therefore diametrically opposed to both Toryism and Liberalism. How on earth can it possibly be advantageous for the Trade Union movement ti become allied with those who seek the overthrow of the two political parties which have the sympathy and support of probably 75 per cent. of the intelligent organised workers ?</p>
        <p>ACROSS THE ATLANTIC ON A cATrbE BOAT. "I was fortunate in securing passage from New York on a fourteen thousand-ten first-class passenger and cattle boat," writes a contributor to the " Windsor " in an interesting article entited "The veal Cattle- Boat." "It is my experience on this boat—as typical of the better conditions of the cattle transportation— that I set forth to show the true cattle-boat and cattle- man. I wish to be perfectly fair—and give a clear, unbiassed picture of what it means to cross the Atlantic as cattle-helper oe a line of this standing. `"The traveller may work his passage from Hoboken, Montreal, Boston, or Philadelphia, and in some cases be paid the sum of one pound for services. From. ports other than New York, return passage is generally given with no work attached on the return trip, but you must board the same steamer, which means that you can only remain in England a few days. Out of New York, no cattle- helpers are given free transportation for labour, none are paid for their work, and no return passage is given, for the human transportation on cattle-boats leaving New York City is in the hands of agents who charge an average of two pounds. This sum paid to them gives you the pleasure of feeding and waterlog cattle between that port and Hull, Liverpool, Glasgow, Manchester, Bristol, or London, as the case may be. You are not allowed to return on the same boat or any other boat of the line, nor is it possible to buy your way back as a cattleman—you must return as a passenger to New York. If you have mapped out a trip, and do not wish to put, up with unnecessary trouble, I would advise you to start from New York, pay the two pounds, and return on a reasonably priced passenger steamer." • "The cattle-boat of fiction and the one of reality are vastly different," writes this contributor 4 For my part, I think that most descriptive stories Pf this life have been overdrawn. The stories cling too close to the tragic; many of them are written by those who have petty grievances to air, they are for the most part too dark, too sombre, too hopeless, and in a large measure unjust and unfair, for conditions on a cattle- . _ boat have undergone radical changes within the last At the Lancashire Chaneery Court, at Manchester, on decade. The cattlemen and foremen are of a higher Monday, a motion in the action Warrington Slate Corn- grade, men of more parts and experience in their work, pany v. Warren, came up for henring before his Honour more humane and more intelligent,while cattle-shipping Dyson's Dioramas. Vice-Chancellor 0. Leigh Clare. Both the parties in has become so important h factor of ocean traffic that A visit to the Parr Hall, Warrington, next week the action are well known in Warrington, the plaintiffs the business has been lifted from a comparatively should not be missed to see and bear Mr. Joshua carrying on the business of builders, merchants, &amp;c., at chaotic state to one that is as thoroughly organized as Bold-street, whilst until recently the defendant acted the mail or passenger service. The burden of proof of Dyson's Dioramas,Gipsy Choir and Animated Pictures as their secretary and commercial traveller. The motion this advancement lies 'n the&amp;eat tthrlipt fwomee,rltyo.adathiornd which commence with a sacred pictorial song ser- was for an injunction to restrain the defendant unti consignment was lost on vice on Sunday at 8.15. A splendid programme will after the trial of an action pending between the parties the better lines, the loss of a single bullock is 3con- be rendered including 'a superb series ef animated or until further notice from carrying on businesssimilar sidered a rare thing." pictures of the life of Christ. Mr. Dyson's combine- to or in competition with that of the plaintiff Corn- MOTORS ON SUBMARINE BOATS. tion is still as fresh as ever. There is at all times Pany, , and the proceedings arose out of an agreement t een the parties a clause in which read : "The