      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>SLATE COMPANY it MR. J. E. WARREN.</head>
        <p>SLATE COMPANY it MR. J. E. WARREN.</p>
        <p>Interim Injunction Refused.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Lady Greenall distributed the prizes at the King's School, Chester, on Wednesday, having motored 210 miles to attend the function.</p>
        <p>Musical Success. At the recent examination of Trinity College, 142nd..m, Miss Alice Wright was successful in passing the intermediate section for pianoforte playing. At the recent examination of Trinity College, London, Miss Alice Wright, pupil of Mrs. A. Booth, was successful in passing the intermediate section for pianoforte playing.</p>
        <p>WOOD 111ANTELs,I1tchen Ranges and Fire Grates, largest stock in the district.—J. S. GARLAND, Iron- monger, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Accident. On Tuesday night at about six o'clock a number of boys were hanging on to a lurry in Orford-lane when one, nlmed Arthur Cooper, fell off and got under the wheel, which passed over his back. Mr. Cheadle very kindly took the boy into his house and called in Dr. Ferguson. Cooper was afterwArds conveYed to his father's house in Orford-lane, and is progressing satis- factorily. Conservative Drive. , The fourth drive in connection with the Warrington Jnnior Conservative Association was held on Tuesday evening to Fearnhead, Culcheth and Winwick. A cold wind prevailed, and to some extent marred the pleasure of the drive, which was joined by about 500. A halt was made at Croft for refreshments, and a stroll about the village. The return journey was safely accomplished shortly before ten o'clock. Personal. On Tuesday, Captain Withington, of Culcheth Hall, completed fifty years' service as a county magistrate, having been placed on the Comrnissioner of the Peace for the county of Lancaster on July 30th, 1857. Mr. A. E. Spann, son of Mr. Richard Spann, carriage and van builder, Friars'-green, has been successful in obtaining the first honours King's medal, the premier award of the City and Guilds' Institute, London, for van, &amp;c., design, together with the first prize offered by the Worshipful Wheelwrights' Company open to all England. Mr. Spann is the first Lancashire man to have gained thes:l distinctions, a fact which renders his success especially gratifying. Miss Ethel Mottram has gained the associateship of the Royal College of Art for painting. In 1903 she left Warrington School of Art with a senior Art Scholarship from the Cheshire County Council (150 for 2 years). This was renewed for a third year,after which Miss Mottram gabled a Royal Ccllege of Art Scholarship, which will be !continued next session when she will have had 5 years of scholarship, the most given to one student. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER TIIE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Adtv. Band of Hope Picnic.</p>
        <p>Members of k Peter's Band of Hops Society held their annual picnic on Saturday, when they visited Smithport. The party left the Central Station by the 9.36 train, and on arrival at Southport the juvenile t•ection hurriedly sought the sands, where they spent an enjoyable time. The adult section of the party visited the various places of interest. The party assembled on the terrace of the meteorological station and sang well-known hymns and choruses. Tea was served at the Kimberley Cafe. • Boating on the lake and other forms of amusement were indulged in till dusk, when the party visited Lord-street, to see the illuminations. The, return journey was made by the 9.5 p.m. train. Motoring Accident.</p>
        <p>A rather serious accident occurred on Tuesday evening at L)eam. As a motor-car containing three gentlemen was coming from Lymtn Church Green to Lymm Cross, the driver appeaeed to be uncertain as to which way to turn, and in putting the brakes on heavily the car over-turned. Tries driver and owner of the car was Mr. Caldwell, of the Vincent Hotel, Manchester. He was thrown clear of the car and sustained no serious injuries. The other two gentle• men were pinned underneath the car, Which had to be raised before they could be liberated. When taken freim under the car one of them was unconscious, and was carried to a house close by, where both were medically attended. The car was completely smashed. CHINA. TEA. SETS.—Special show, of latest art patterns. See windows, J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge- street, Warrington. —Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Warrington _Ragged Schrol_ and Mission</head>
        <p>Warrington _Ragged Schrol_ and Mission</p>
        <p>Mr. James Dodds :distributed last Sunday afternoon the prizes to the scholars attending these schools, and in the course of a few remarks said he had presided at many meetings attended by Cabinet Ministers and persons of high rank but he had never presidad over a meeting that gave him greater pleasure than the one he was now addressing, he was pleased to see the clean appearance of the children, and the orderly way in which they conducted themselves was a credit to the workers. Prizes were then handed to the scholars and a kind word spoken to each one. One little incident was,worthy of notice; a blind girl who has attended the school for some time,not having received a prize, Mr. Dodds asked for har to be brought on the platform and after,- speaking a few kind words to her presented her with a gold coin. Several hymns were given by the scholars, and on the proposition of Mr. Skelland seconded by Mr. Owen a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Dodds.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. C. Andersen as Parliamentary Candidate.</p>
        <p>The Oldham branch of the Independent Labour party have resolved to recommend the National Administrative Council of the Independent Labour p irty to nominate Mr. William C. Anderson to be placed on the list of Parliamentary candidates officially recognised by the National Labour Representation Committee. It is the intention of the Oldham Inde-- pendent Labour. Party to bring forward Mr. Anderson as their candidate for the Parliamentary representative of the borough. Before taking that step, however, they will require the endorsement of the Oldham and District Labour Representation Committee, an offshoot of the local Trades Council. Mr. Anderson, who delivered a series of addresses at .Warrington last week, was for some years the Manchester district organising secretary of the Shop Assistants' Union. He has abandoned that office, and is now engaged in lecturing and organising work for the Independent Labour Party. NEW STRAWBERRY JAM.—ASHLEY'S Sankey st.— Advt.</p>
        <p>Similar correspondance has been running through the" Manchester Evening News," one contributor writing as follows : —Sir, May Ibe allowed to point out that though the rank and file belonging to trades unions are not Socialists, yet their councils, their committees, their wirepullers and leaders are mostly made up of Socialists to-day. These are the men who possess power, and mean to possess more power before they have done. They lay their commands on the rank and file even to bidding them vote against the Liberal, and woe betide any one of their number who dares to call himself a Liberal, as witness the case of Mr. Vivian (M.P. for Birkenhead) only last week. These Socialists have captured most of the official places belonging to trades unions, and the members of Parliament who were run by the L.R. C. are nearly all Socialists, Mr. Kelley being an exception. Last year a dead set was made at Richard Bell, his crime being that he would not call himself a Socialist. It is time the working man's eyes were opened to the tyranny of his leaders and the utter worthlessness of the tales preached at the Socialist meetings. A LIBERAL.</p>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, August 3, 1907 • THE VOICE OF LABOUR. MAGAZINE CUTTINGS. Edited by John Workman. Readers of this column are invited to assist in making it interesting ancrhelpful to social workers by contributing themselves. Correspondents must write on one side of the paper only, and endeavour to make their letters and notes short and to the point. A nom-de-plume may be used, but the real name of the writer should be forwarded in confidence to the Editor of the column. M.S.S. must be culdressed to John Workman, " Examiner " Office, Warrington and should be sent in not later than Wednesday in each week.</p>
        <p>of Queen Alexandra," these being splendid reproduction of miniatures and oil portraits by famous modern painters. As if the above were not enough for one shilling there are in" The Smart Set Annual 32 pages of humorous NEW EVIDENCE AS TO THE END OF line drawings, depicting the fancies and foibles of the NANA SAHIB. period by some of the cleverest artists in England. General Harris writes: "In November, 1858, I* was Then there are also in "The gmart Set Annual "a ordered, with a detachment of three companies, first to multitude of brilliant burlesques, topical travesties, Byram Ghat and then to a ford on the Upper Gogra, amusing sketched, epigrams and witticisms, making this called Chilari Ghat. A small party of seventeen Royal new publication one of the brightest and merriest ever Engineers, under Richard Harrison, presently joined rue, i.ssued in Great Britain or Ireland. with orders to construct a bridge, and a regiment of Pioneers unarmed, with a lieutenant in command, to help. I 'commanded the whole. "Now these Musbees had never been enlisted by Government before; low-caste men, all—or almost all— expert thieves' and treacherous. I had a guard of them always at the ford, and lived myself in a tent close by. Now this ford was only about thirty miles from the Terai, into which the rebel forces, with the Nana, had been driven, and, for all the deserters from his force,it was the most convenient to get back to Western Oude, or Rohilcund. Moreover, it was the only one where ' they could not meet some European force. Very shortly I found that, through my native officers, I was thoroughly posted up in all the Nana's movements. There was, as you know, a lac of rupees reward for him, dead or alive. Two of my subandars were always at me to allow them three or four days' leave to capture him. They kept me informed of his movements like a Court circular. I always told them that I was on duty for a certain purpose, and it was impossible I could give any man leave. One Thursday, Ram Sing came to me, begging me still more strongly than before, saying the Nana was getting much worse—he was, as I knew suffering from fever and ague, and had an enlarged "My boy," said the old gentleman, in a spleen—and he told me that the Nana had had his little kindly tone, "there's only one thing that finger cut off, and had burnt it as an offering to Kali, stands between you add success." "And what with a view of propitiating the goddess. Two days is that?" asked the youth. "If you worked !after this Ram Sing and the other subandar came and as hard at working," exclaimed the old said: 'No one will get the reward now; he died and' gentleman, "as you do at trying to find some was burnt yesterday. And I feel quite sure it was true, y to avoid working, you would easily ac- for I bad known for some weeks all about his move- wa ments."—From "Amongst the Mutiny Cities of India" quire both fame and fortune." in the " Cornhill Magazine" for August. THE FATE OF' CROSBY HALL. I First Artist: "I received a magnificent I tribute to my skill the other day at the ex- The Hall of Crosby Place still stands. And it should hibition:" Second Artist. "What was it?" continue to stand, for the education of men still living, First Artist: "You know my picture, 'A and of those who will come after us, in the safeguarding Storm at Sea'? Well, a man and his wife of the nation. Balzac said the character of a people cani be told by its architecture. That great Frenchman were looking at t, and I heard the man say, (who has been described by our greatest living poet as ` come on, my dear; that picture makes me 2) Shakespeare without his lyrical faculty) did not err, sick. Two centuries of history can be learnt from Crosby Hall without the help of cheap histories or cheaper guide-" In some parts of Ireland it is a custom books. among bank clerks to speak of one another A Lord Mayor of London built it in the fourteenth as "officers" of the bank; but little Jim century who gave his own name to the place, and then Bender, the. recently - imported Cockney rode north to Ravenspur to welcome Edward IV. Here waiter in a county Mayo hotel, was not aware was a regal beginning to a foundation; but the associa- of this custom. -"Have you seen any of our tions of Royalty with Crosby Place do not end here, or officers here this morning?" asked a lordly its tenancy by Kings' and Queens' servants. In the knight of the quill of Jim a few days ago. spacious days of Elizabeth the four hundred noble- Jim glanced keenly at his interrogator. rogator. men and gentlemen who formed the suite of the d„lin, Duo de 13iron, Ambassador from France, were ussir,„ he answered, promptly. Itisn't seen by errant, or lazy, Londoners passing in three minutes ago since one of 'em went out and out of doors still open for public entertainment, with his sword be'ind 'is ear!" The chatter of a busy public restaurant has therefore been vented on walls which have listened to the inner- most secrets of French diplomacy at a moment when that diplomacy was speoially hostile to England. The associations of poetry and martial valour next invade the house, and Londoners, who, it is to be hoped, will never live to see Crosby Place pulled down, will remember that it was once tenanted by the sister of the hero who died on the battlefield of Zutphen. Mary Counte.es of Pembroke lived at Crosby Place for some years and has ineffaceably connected the house with the memory of Sir Philip Sidney. . . . But a more ominous tenancy has to be chronicled: for Crosby Place, as it was then called, is indissolubly connected with the memory of one of England's ablest and wickedest kings. The halting strides of Richard 111. have sounded in that fine hall, where, till lately, city clerks snatched a hasty dinner. Fancy conjures up the picture of this murderous dissembler shambling about Crosby Hall, listening with a false smile on his lips for that expected foot-tread which was to convey to him the fate of two young princes who barred the path to a Royal Succession. And the scene of such an episode as this, stamped deeply, as it should be, into the nation's history, is in danger of being shifted for ever by the hand of a con tractor! The auctioneer may put up for sale the palace where Richard Plantagenet schemed for a crown, and listened at wind-stirred arras for murderous tidings. The prospect, it is to be hoped, is preposterous. And one single hope lends strength to this belief. Among the many distinguished proprietors of this last of the great merchant princes' houses of the City of London one name has not been mentioned in this paper. Yet it is one of the most distinguished. Sir Thomas More lived at Crosby Hall. ro a friend, and addressed to Crosby Hall, was sent his last letter before he ascended the scaffold on Tower Hill ; and it is the hope of the writer that an additional plea for the 6reservation of a fine old house may be found in the fact that in that house Sir Thomas More wrote his "Utopia."—From an article by W. Outram Tristram with beautiful illustrations by Hedley Fitton, in the Lgust (Summer) number of, the "Pail Mall Magazine."</p>
        <p>At the commencement of a geography les- son the master of a small school asked one of his pupils tr) tell him of what the earth con- sists, and was promptly answered, "Land and water." He then varied the question slightly, that the fact might be impressed on the. boy's mind, and asked: "What, then, do land and, water make?" to which came the immediate response: "Mud." -</p>
        <p>"The boy has evidently been eating too much between meals," said the doctor. "Non- sense !" replied the boy's father; "a boy can't eat in his sleep." "How .do you mean?" "I mean that each of his meals begins when he gets up in the morning, and ends' when he goes to bed."</p>
        <p>New Curate: "Your husband is a con- firmed invalid, is he not?" ; Mrs. Billyus : "Confirmed, sir? No, sir; he ain't Church of England." New Curate: "I mean, is he a permanent invalid?" Mrs. Billyus - "Per- manent? "Lor', no! Doctor says he can't last through the winter."</p>
        <p>Recently a little girl was taken to London by her parents. On her return home she was de6cribing- the places she had seen to her youthful friends. One of them asked : Did you see the Old Bailey, where they used to hang the murderers?" "No,' replied the girl, "I don't think so; but I saw the Royal Academy, where they hang the artists.</p>
        <p>"There now," exclaimed a little American girl the other day, rummaging in a drawer, "uncle has gone to heaven without his spec- tacles." ,</p>
        <p>He: "Did it hurt much to have your ears pierced?" She (with ear-rings) : `Oh, no. They have been bored so much that they didn't mind it."</p>
        <p>"Look here," remarked a violinist resent- fully, 'you were talking all through my solo." Oh, don't worry about that," replied the man addressed. "I assure you, I wasn't Saying anything that you would particularly want to hear."</p>
        <p>"Papa," said a little boy, "ought the master to punish me for what I did not do?" "Certainly not, my boy," replied the father. "Well, said the little 'fellow, "he.--did to-day when I didn't do my sum."</p>
        <p>Contributor: "You see, I followed your rules and wrote only on one side •of the paper." Editor: "You can do still better." Contributor (eagerly) : "How?" Editor :• "Don't write on either side."</p>
        <p>Benson : "I notice that that young widow doesn't have her widow's weeds so much in evidence now." Johnson: "No; she's clear- ing those weeds away. I believe she sees sig:is of a second crop of orange blossoms."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Jones: "Here is a letter I want you to post, dear. It is to my milliner, countermand- ing an order for a hat." Mr. Jones: "Here; take this piece of cord and tie the letter to my liz:;nds,•so that I won't forget 'it."</p>
        <p>Alan in the corner of the car: "Wet morn- ii. sir.'' Wan in the other corner (crossly): " Yes ; hut you have omitted to remark the st:•app, fact that the rain is coming down in- stead of going up as usual."</p>
        <p>Dolly: "I don't see how young Wiggins could possibly be a greater bore than he is. He does nothing but • giggle all the time." Polly: "Well, if he didn't giggle he might talk, and perhaps that would be worse."</p>
        <p>" I have many beautiful thoughts," said the 1.311 g-haired person, who imagines he is a born puet, ." that I propose soon to give to the pub- lic." "That's a good idea, young man," jowled the cynical old man. "It's certain you could never induce the public to buy</p>
        <p>:laud: "I have just received an offer of m zariago which came ,by post this morning. He said that his love for me was very great, but .that his- income was small." Marie :, " a pay. -Who was it from?" Maud: really didn't notice ; that was enough."</p>
        <p>Theatre Manager (in newspaper office) : "I wish to see the dramatic Critic about a new play I am going to produce." Sharp Business Manager: 'Yes, Sir. He is, in the_editorial- room ; but before going up, please leave your valuables in the safe." •</p>
        <p>itecently a bashful young woman from a backwoods county in Virginia went into a, local store carrying three chickens. She in- quired the price of chickens, and at the same time put them on the counter. `,` Will they lay there?" asked the clerk, who did not know that the chickens' legs were tied. She bit her in embarrassment a moment, and. said : "No, sir; they are roosters.-</p>
        <p>In blowin out a candle hold it aloft and blow upWard. This will prevent the scatter- ing of the grease.</p>
        <p>Never leave medicine, drink, or food • un- covered in a sick room; .they are best kept out of the room altogether.</p>
        <p>A lemon should always be kept near the kitchen sink, as a slice of raw lemon will remove all stains from the hanes.</p>
        <p>The Shank bones of mutton, so little valued in general, if well. soaked, add to the richness of gravies and soups for sick-room broths.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Prospect Park (at midnight): "Morti- mer, I think I hear a burglar down in the front hail!" Mr. Prospect Park (philosophi- cally) : "Henrietta, with two bicycles, Willie's wooden horse, the hat-rack, um- brella-stand, and the baby's mail-cart for him to run into, you wouldn't only ' think ' you laea,rd a .burglar if one was really there !"</p>
        <p>"This ends-tt !" said she, coldiy, all is over between us. I'll thank you to return my letters." "Very well," he replied, "I'll send them to you by district messenger the first thing in the morning." "Oh! there's no par- ticular hurry. You can bring them when you call to-morrow evening."</p>
        <p>_oimaizezt,!s.o DARE 10 BE A DANIEL This phrase has come to be a proverbial one. The youthful heroism of Daniel in Babylon has a lesson for all ages. There is risk, how- ever, of the words being used i- too jaunty and self-sufficient a way. r 9. There are many who can loudly sing, "Dare to be a Daniel," who would be found great cowards in the face of real trial and danger.' Thirty years ago, more or less, there was a boy in Scotland who would go to sea. His name was James, and his father was a. respected citizen of • a town six Miles from- the sea.</p>
        <p>On James's first voyage to Calcutta he kept up the habit of praying in the forecastle bawls turning in to his hammock, as a matter of course. He had been accustomed to do so regularly at home, and he continued regularly to do so at sea. in much the same way as he put off and on his clothes. Nobody said anything to him on tir matter, but Bob Shearer, an able seaman. watched him. In Calcutta some of the seamen left the ship, and others were engaged in their place to work the ship home. One of these was "a rough" from Whitechapel, whose name was Robert. But no sailor since the days of Noah was ever called Robert, so, to distinguish him and Shearer, the one was called English Bob and the other Scotch Bob.</p>
        <p>One night, soon after the homeward voyage began, James was on he knees when. th,e eye: of. English Bob happened to fall on him. "I declare," he cried with an oath, "here's a younker praying. Did you ever?" And thereupon he flung a heavy shoe at his head with excellent aim..</p>
        <p>Before James had time to rise, Scotch Bob had the coward by the throat and told him oome upstairs and settle with him at onoe.</p>
        <p>The result was—l merely mention it as a matter of fact, and do not express an opinion on the best way of treating bullies and cowards —that English Bob got soundly and whole- somely thrashed.‘ That night James went into his hammock without praying. But he had not time to fall asleep before Scotch Bob came and 'pitched him out.</p>
        <p>"What do you mean, you young coward.? say your, prayers, like, a man. Do you think I'm goir..g to fight for you and be ddsgraced thil way?"</p>
        <p>And so James never again failed to kneel. before he slept, and feels to this day that his being ashamed of his Father hi heaven and of the Saviour who died for him, was well rebuked by the friendly courage of Bob Shearer. Long Mier, when his name had a title before. it, and he was at the head of his profession,. :fames had pleasure in finding Bob Shearer's mother. and bringing her to visit the mother who taught him to pray. James is still living, and it is from himself we have this story.</p>
        <p>THE SQUIRE AND HIS SERVANT.</p>
        <p>A. Scottish squire was once riding with his man, and whilst descending a hill the squire said: "I saw a badger go in that hole in the bank, John." "Will you hold the horse, sir, whilst I go and get a spade?" John got a spade, and began to dig whilst the squire sat in the trap Smiling. After John had been :Egging for about half-an-hour, he said, "He's not here now?" "I daresay it isn't; it is ten years since I saw.him. go in." H. COPP.</p>
        <p>-4.</p>
        <p>-A SHILLING'S WORTH OF</p>
        <p>A. fellow who 'came by the railroaa, being a stranger, strolled about for some time on the "outskirts" of a town in search of a barber. He finally discovered one, and requested the operator to take off a shilling's worth of hair. The barber trimmed his locks very neatly, soaped up the remainder very handsomely, and then combed and brushed him up till his head looked as if it belonged to some other person than himself.</p>
        <p>"Are you done?" aeked the stranger, as the barber removed the napkin from ,hie neck.</p>
        <p>"Yes, sir," said the barber, with a polite bow.</p>
        <p>---`IA-re you certain that you took off a shAling's worth 7';</p>
        <p>"Yes, sir, there's a glass; you can ITk for yourself." "Well," said the stranger, "if you think you have a shillings worth off, I don't Iknow as I've got any use for it, and I haven't got any .change, so you may just take the hair for your trouble."</p>
        <p>On :hearing this, the barber made a jump for the man. whereupon he made a jump for the door, which not being bolted, he bolted himself.</p>
        <p>-----4--</p>
        <p>"Professor,- said a graduate, trying to ba pathetic at parting, "I am indebted to you for all I know."</p>
        <p>"Pray do not .mention such a trifle," was the not very flattering reply.</p>
        <p>Tommy was a little rogue, whom his mother had hard work to manage. Their wooden house in the Statess was raised a few feet from the ground. and Tommy, to escape a well- deserved whipping, ran from his mother and crept under the house. Presently the father came home, and hearing where the boy had taken refuge, crept under to bring him out. As he approached on his hands and knees Tommy asked, "Is she after you, too t"</p>
