      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>THE LIGHTING OF THE VILLAGE.</p>
        <p>The lioghting of the village was discussed at the monthly meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee held at the Hall Cafe on Tuesday evening. .Mr. G. Pilling presided and there were present : Messrs. J. H. Clarke, J. W. Bonchier, H. J. Westbrook, G. J. Brown, T. It. Westbrook, F. Warburton, J. McDermid, J. Whitley, J. Hallows, J. Mynekyme, J. Swinton (surveyor), and W. E. Hough (assistant clerk). Mr. Whitley reported that he and Mr.McDermid had had a conference with the manager of the Corporation Gas Works, with reference to the question of lighting the village. Mr. Haddock had agreed to instal incandescent and light the lamps all the year round at a cost of 545. per annum. For the existing lamps it would workout at an annual cost of £194 10s. He moved that the offer be accepted.—Mr. AlcDermid seconded. The offer of Mr. Haddock meant that they would have three months extra lighting at an increased annual cost of £2 17s. Gd. with the additional advantage of having GO candle power lights as against the old 18 candle power ordinary burners.—Mr. Brown expressed his satisfaction that such a satisfactory arrangement had been made. Under the old conditions to light the village for twelve months would have cost £2os.—Mr. Clarke, Mr. El. J. Westbrook and Mr. Bouchier also expressed their pleasure at the arrangement and it was unanimously decided to accept the offer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE.</head>
        <p>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>The first annual inspection of the Stockton Heath Company of the above took place at the Church Room, Stockton Heath, on Friday last at 8 p.m., the Inspecting officer being Captain H. Owen, of the Ist Volunteer Battalion S.L. Regt. The officers present were the Assistant Chaplain, the Rev. H. A. Lester, Captain R. H. Lepage, Lieutenant J. S. Shotten and Lieutenant A. H. Austin. There were also present Mrs. Lester and Mr. A. Down. .Tho Company paraded in utmost full StrAngth, there being only two absentees. Those present included Corporals Isherwood, Sankey, Spencer and Williams, Buglers Rushton and Woodward, and twenty rank and file-26 all told. Captain Owen and Mrs. Lester proceeded along the lines and inspected the equipment. There was considerable difficulty in decid- ing the order of merit in this respect, as the whole Brigade presented a remarkably clean and neat appear- ance, but the prizes were awarded as follows r—lst Bugler Rushton ; 2nd, Pte. Oakes; 3rd, Corp. Isherwood. After the equipment inspection, Captain Lepage put the boys through their Company drill and physical drill. Another item in the proceedings was boxing, by , eight members of the brigade, the combatants being (1) Corporal Sankey and Private Knight ; (2), Privates Dakes and Hertree ; (3), Corporal Isherwood and Private Sherratt ; (4), Corporal Williams and Private Welsby.—The Inspecting Officer placed the prize winners in the following order :—First, Corporal Isher- wood ; second, Corporal bankey ; third,‘Private Oakes. Equipment was then again donned, ard the prize- winners were called to the front to receive their prizes, which were handed to them by Mrs. Lester. The prizes for the boxing competition were provided by Captain , Owen, and those for equipment by Mr. A. DoWn. The Company thec formed up in line, and Captain Owen ,briefly addressed them. ; Preparations are now well advanced for the Brigade lCamp which will be from the 3rd to the 10th of August. ITwenty members of the Stockton Heath Company I together with their officers will join the camp for the North Western Distict, which will be situate at Voryd Farm, near RhA and at lixhich 2,500 members of the Brigade, from various parts of the country, will be present. On Sunday last, a Church Parade was held,— Captain Lapage' Lieutenant Austin and twenty-two , members of the Prigade attending morning service at ISt. Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath. On Monday evening, the medical inspection of the Company took place at the Church Room, the result of the examina- tion going to prove the excellent physical condition of the boys. The Inspecting Medical Officer was Doctor Collins. Tbis was followed by carbine drill,—carbines having now been procured for the use of those who are going to camp. The Company will leave Bank Quay Station this afternoon (Saturday), to join the c amp at Rhyl.</p>
        <p>Ig*</p>
