      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Child Life.</head>
        <p>Child Life.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Heywood, phy9ical director of the Birming- ham Y.111.C.A. has just published some striking statistics contrasting the measurements of children living in the model village of Bournville with those of children resident in the Floodgate-street area—one of the worst slums in Birmingham. It was found as a result of several hundred measure- ments that the Bournville girl of seven was three incebs taller and four pounds heavier than the slum child of the same age, while the Bournville boy of seven was two and a half inches taller, and eight ponds heavier than the Floodgate-street child, the lung capacity of the latter being also much less. Special measurements were taken of children of "hook-and-eye "carders, who in many cases at thirteen ooked only ten or eleven years of age. The comparison was in this case as follows :</p>
        <p>Height. Bournville Carder</p>
        <p>Weight. 4st. 131 b. 3st. 111 b.</p>
        <p>Bournville better by... 71n. Ist. 21b. The above figures wil be of interest to all who care for child life—and who does not ? But we must guard ourselves against supposing that the few years of life I spent amongst the healthy conditions existing in Bournville, and like places is capable of producing sch striking differences. Probably in the one case we are dealing with the products of ancestry whose conditions of life, moral and physical, have been more or less good and wholesome. In the other case, we have the product of, it may be,two or three generations of slum dwellers 3. each generation by heredity and environment having ' done its part to impair the physique and enfeeble the character. These influences in so far as they have been continu- ous through several lives manifest themselves in their cumulative results in the present generation. Amongst the slum dwellers we find men stunted, ill-developed, with stamina often lowered by excessive drinking and as often by deficient eating ; women shrunken, with interests restricted and instincts perverted; children inheriting all the weaknesses of their parents, with colourless faces, anannic blood, frail in body, feeble in brain, and too often distorted in soul. It is not from such stock as this that Bournville or any other Garden City or Suburb draws its inhabitants. It is only just to say right here that in spite of environment many of the poor manage to conserve much that is healthy in body and mind, and for such no word of appreciation is too high. But the circum- stances of their lives are against the posiibility of natural healthy abounding life. What interest can such take in things outside the daily struggle, what concern can they have for their citizenship? What is Empire Day to the child whose world is limited by dull streets, and whose home is an alley ? , What matter to the mother that upon the British Empire the sun never sets, if the same sun never rises or shines on her back yard? We lament the rate of infant mortality in certain Wards of our town. But even if we were able to keep alight the spark of vitality in these little mites, could we guarantee to them a fair chance in life? If we could evade death's stroke, could we ward off the wounds of dirt, foul air and bad feeding? Could we offer them light and sunshine? Could we promise them the healthy fostering care of mother Nature, with her earth and streams, her flowers and tress? Could we find them places wherin to build sand-castles, and green fields in which to pluck the daisies and weave them into chains to garland baby necks? Lessons in school about Nature are poor substitutes for actual contact. A boy may take a languid interest in a tree and learn its name from a picture ; he would learn far more if he could climb the tree, be rocked by the wind on the highest bow, and be spanked for tearing his trousers in his climbing. A girl may learn some- thing of flowers and roots from books, and from speci- mens brought to school from the country, but such knowledge is colourless and uninteresting compared with that which comes from the tending of a rose at a cottage door, or the joy of helping father to dig from the dark earth a fatally of monster potatoes. Back to the land is a call to which, as far as daily work is coneerned, those who are better employed in forge or factory may give no heed, but the massing of homes in crowded town streets has driven English child life out of its rightful Eden, and back to the land, the garden, the sunshine, and the playfield must now be our motto for the children's sake.</p>
        <p>But let Warrington Garden Suburb enthusiasts labour under no delusion that Garden Suburbs provide a short cut by which the slum child will immediately return to his lost Eden. The stunted frames, the narrow outlooks, the defective minds, have taken generations to create, and it will only be by long years of patient toil and effort that the road from alum-land to garden-land will bt, Math/ vl.oor, 6.„ Aomworocc.l. by tho -rroorr foot, of the poorest parents and children in our towns. . . . .</p>
        <p>Wit this cs no reason why we should not make a beginning by clearing the road, and building the bridges. The way will be made easier for the weak, if those who are strong and able smooth the track by removing the stumbling blocks out of the path of those who are feeble. Penketh, July 30th, 1907.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>VOLUNTEER ENCAMPMENT.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER ENCAMPMENT.</p>
        <p>Departure To-Morrow,</p>
        <p>The members of ,the Ist V.B. P.W.V., South Lanes Regt. to-morrow morning leave for their annual camp training. This year they will be under canvas at Llanberis, and general regret is felt that the period of training has been reduced from a fortnight to one -week, having in view the beauties of the spot to be visited. The men will parade at ten o'clook and will</p>
        <p>then march to Bank Quay Station to entrain for Llanberis. In the first train will travel Lieutenant. Colonel J. A. Edelsten, Sergeant-Major G. Ingram, band, drums, and buglers, Captain and Acting- Adjutant H. G. Roberts, regimental staff, MI, Cyclist, B, and C Companies, while in the second train thers will be Major J. D. Fairclough, D, E, F, G, H, I Companies, Colour-Sergeant-Instructors Allardice and Cleaver. G Company will fall in at the Drill Hall, Newton, at 8.45. The annual inspection will take place in camp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>CHURCH PARADE.</head>
        <p>THE CHURCH PARADE.</p>
        <p>The annual Church Parade was held on Sunday after- noon when the men to the number of 380 attended service at St. Paul's Church. Parading at the Drill Hall at 130, the men,who presented a smart appearance, marched to the church by way of Sankey-street, Horse- market-street, and Bewsey-road, the route bein lined with spectators. The procession was headed by the band, under the conductorship of Mr. Richardson. The ()Ulcers present were Lteutenant-Colonel Edelsten (com- manding officer), Major J. D. Fairclough, Major B. Fairclough, Major Appleton, Captain 11. Owen, Captain R. Smith, Capt tin P. N. Avery, Lieutenant F. R. Monks, Lieutenant J. T. Picton, Lieutenant R. IL Lapage. Second Lieutenant E. Fairclough,Second Lieutenant M. Woods, Captain and Quartermaster Potter, V.D., and Captain and Acting-Adjutant H. G. Roberts, The sermon was preached by the Rev. W. Bracecamo, chaplain of the Battalion, who took for his text" Fools make mock of sin." He (the preacher), said to those who were present on the occasion of the 'annual church parade. " Don't be fools. Try and think of higher things." They were all proud to belong to such a corps as the Ist,. Vol. Batt., Prince of Wales's Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment), and he was proud of the Volunteers when they had been on the march, and had set an example to others. They would shortly be going to camp at Llanberis, and while there he urged them to think of the text. Never mind what he had said but remember the text, and not be under tbe impression that, because they were away from home, they could do what they liked. He wished that those who gambled would give the habit up, as it ruined both body and soul. It was wrong, after working hard all the week, to gamble their money away. They must not get too much to drink, but remember to live pure and holy lives, do their duty to their King and couutry, and to those placed over them by the :King, and remember when they were away in that lovely spot where they would be encamped for a week to pray to God to make them realize that if they sinned they were fools, and would have to suffer for their folly. As a soldier speaking to soldiers, he asked them to" quit themselves like men," and lead honest, upright straight lives. Addressing those in the con- gregation who had followed the Volunteers to Church, the Vicar said he hoped the day was not far aistaht when they would be compelled to come forward and rerve their country. Why should the Volunteers do duty for others who spent a lazy time at home? They ought to be wearing scarlet coats themselves. The members of the corps were doing duty for those who were not members, but he hoped the tables would be I turned shortly, and that they would have a Government who would deal with the question and make all of them soldiers. To all he said, "Be soldiers not only of the King, but of the King of Kings." Suitable hymns, such as "Fight the good fight," and " Onward, Christian soldiers" were sung, the band of the regiment supplying the accompaniments. Mr. E. Wilkes was at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>ADVANCE PARTY.</head>
        <p>THE ADVANCE PARTY.</p>
        <p>The ad -ance party left on Wednesday morning for Llanberis, under the direction of Captain and Quarter- master A. F. 0. Potter, V.D., and it includes Sergeant- Instructor M. Newell, Quartermaster Sergeant, Armoure Sergeant, two sergeants, two corporals, and 20 privates also one non-commissioned officer, three transport men and one bugler.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>MR. J. HOWES' REMINISCENCES.</head>
        <p>MR. J. HOWES' REMINISCENCES.</p>
        <p>TWENTY-FIVE YEARS' FIGHT WITH THE TORIES.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Howes is one of the best known political campaigners in the country and his record of service in the progressive cause is one of which any man might be justly proud. His has been a strenuous career, full of incident, and his work as a lecturer and debater has brought him into touch with all sorts and conditions of men. Possessing a deep knowledge of the great questions of the day and the power of expressing himself readily and lucidly, Mr. Howes is always listened to with interest, and perhaps nowhere are is sterling abilities mere appreciated than in Warrington where the prominent part he played in the recent progressive triumph will not readily be forgotten. Mr. Howes has a vast store/. of interesting reminiscences, and he has just issued a very readable record of his political experiences in the shape of a book entitled "Twenty-five years' fight with the Tories." The work extends over 338 pages and cannot fail to be of value to the political student. It is written in Mr. Howes' characteristic homely and telling style. The author gives some details of his early life and of his entry into politics. "Having been thrown upon my own resources ever since I was thirteen years of age, I have frequently had to rub against the sharp corners of existence, and having graduated in the workshop, I soon learned to take a deep interest in all questions affecting the working classes. At sixteen years of age I became a member of a political debating society, and commenced the study of politics." Mr. klowes relates how his attention was first directed to tlfe Irish question and points out that his further study of general history and politics soon revealed to him that the injustices practised in Ireland were only part of a system imposed by the rich upon the poor in this country, as long as the wealthy ones had the power to work their own sweet will. "Continuing my investigations, I became an enthusiastic reformer, and I foolishly imagined that it was only necessary to bring i the facts of mis- government before the people n order to obtain their earnest co-operation in the direction of reformation. I had not then read the lives of the devoted reformers of the past, or I should have known how thankless a task is working for the public. However, I continued to spend all my spare cash (which was not much) on books, and my spare time in reading them, and I soon became in great demand at our workingmen's meetings and debating classes, especially as I, with a few others from the boot factory, had developed a love for controversial politics. Whenever there was a Tory meeting in our neighbourhood we were always prepared to go and move an amendment, if we got the chance, and if not we would arrange a counter-demonstra- tion and challenge the Tory speakers to come and oppose us, we promising them the fullest facilities for their criticism. We had, in those days, a great idea of fairness and any amount of courage and so confident were we of the strength of our case that when we got an opponent, we felt inclined to exclaim 'The Lord hath delivered him into our hands." Mr. Howes and his comrades conducted a vigorous campaign in Nottingham and after their day's work they often held meetings with the agricultural labourers in the surrounding villages. On one occasion a poster announcing a meeting in Nottingham was destroyed by the Chief Constable and the incident led to a summons against the Chief—which was dis- missed—and a question in Parliament. One of the exciting passages in the book describes a meeting at Derby when Mr. Howes and other young men acted as a bodyguard to protect Sir Charles and Lady Dilke. A. disgraceful scene was witnessed at the meeting, a gang of roughs endeavouring to storm the plat- form. Blood flowed freely and windows were smashed. Mr. Howes was able to save Mr. G. J. Holyoake from a very severe blow which might, perhaps, have endangered his life. Describing the incident Mr. Howes says : " During the melee a number of roughs gained possession of the platform, from which some of us were trying to eject them. Mr. Holyoake was standing on the floor of the hall with his back to the platform, when one of the opponents, armed with the thick part of a fishing rod, on the end of which was a strong brass ferrule,raised this weapon in the air, and was bringing it down with all his for.o towards the head of Mr. Hoylake. I just had time to spring at the man, wrench the stick from him and help him, head first, over the rail of the platform. I carried that part of the fishing-rod back with me to Nottingham, as a memento of the occasion." In 1875 Mr. Howes removed to Leeds and became the president of the Workingmen's Labour Repre- sentation League. At this time he formed a lasting friendship with Dr. F. R. Lees, the great temperance I and political reformer, and at his suggestion signed I the temperance pledge. "After over twenty-five years' I experience " says Mr. Howes " I can say with great , confidence that it was one of the best thinas I ever did, , and I look upon that pledge—which I keep by me— as one of my most valuable possessions." Mr. Howes' first public political debate occurred in 1877, the subject being the disestablishment and disendowment of the English Church. He next had a skirmish with the lords of the manor of Hunslet with regard to commoners' rights and he tells an interesting story of the litigation which ensued. Although Mr. Howes and his friends were defeated in the action, feeling ran so high that the Leeds Corporation eventually secured the land for the people and it has been laid out as a park and recreation ground. With an enthusiasm born of conviction, Mr. Howes pursued his public work and the book is one long record of debates, election campaigns, and efforts to maintain the people's rights. Meetings in byegone days were not by any means as orderly as those to which we have now become accustomed, and Mr. Howes has many exciting incidents to recount. On one occasion a man brought with him a large bull dog which barked loudly when there was any cheering or disorder. The Labour League in Leeds achieved some notable victories under Mr. Howes' leadership and the local Tories were furious. The League worked splendidly to bring about the return of the Right Hon. W. E. Gladstone who contested Leeds and Midlothian at the same election and was returned for both places by large majorities. The Grand Old Man spent a week in Leeds addressing great meetings and Mr. Howes thus refers to the visit. "How Leeds did demonstrate! The largest public buildings in the town were all too small to accommo- date the audiences. I was on the platform at most of the meetings with Mr. Gladstone, and frequently in his private company. No one knew Mr. Glad- stone unless they were privileged to see him away from his public work. It was then that the full charm of his presence and character seemed to manifest itself. It was an inspiration to a political worker to have this opportunity of associating with a man so truly good and great. Mrs. Glad- stone accompanied her husband, and her presence gave us all great delight. It enabled some of us to see haw much the great statesman owed to her untiring and effectionate devotion. Ido not think my wife will over forget a few minutes' private conversation she had with Mrs. Gladstone." Mr. Howes became a lecturer to the National Reform Union in 1879 and has ever since retained his connection with that institution. He did a great deal , to secure the return of Mr. Carbutt for Monmouth in 1880. The name of the Tory candidate was Cords I and one of his war cries was "Vote for Cords and Christianity and send the infidel back to Leeds " the Tories having discovered that Mr. Carbutt was a Unitarian. Mr. Howes hunted up Mr. Cords' Parlia- mentary record and found that on one occasion, at tiso o'cl )ck in the morning, he had given a vote to retain the cat-o'-nine-tails in the Navy. "As Newport is a seafaring town I knew that this information would interest the voters, so I I engaged the largest hall in the town, and announced that I would give a lecture, entitled 'Mr. Cords and his Christianity.' When the night came the hall was plcked. I had had a large cat-o'-nine tails made, and, after I had dealt with the Tory mem- ber's political career, I exhibited the instrument of torture, reminding the audience that it was with this article of chastisement that Mr. Cords pro- posed to Christianise our British sailors. The next time he appeared in Newport he got a very mixed reception, and from this time the' cat' became one of the badges of the election." So far back as 1881 Mr. Howes held meetings in ST. HELENS ART MASTER MARRIED. Lancashire, and he records that in Oldham and dis- , A large congregation, including most of the students trict he came across a lot of Tory workingmen. The of the St. Helens Art School, assembled at Wesleyan reason there are so many Tories in Lancashire is, he Church on Thursday, on the occasion of the marriage Mrg, Liverpool, who has for a thinks, that since Mr. Gladstone disestablished of .JohnSkeapinof the English Church in Ireland in 1808, the number of years been head rnaster of the Art school at , the Gamble Institute, with Miss Emily Jane Brown, Church party has been extremely active in fourth daughter of Mr. John Brown,of Aehleigh,Windle- this part of the country, and the Liberal party, in ! street, the borough treasurer. Their presents included g several constituencies, has allowed its case to oby a silver salad bowl and servers from the art students. default. Mr. Howes puts in a word for more educa_ i The bride was attired in a cream costume with white tion in Liberal principles. Disestablishment has Tuscan hat, and the bridesmaid,Miss Constance Dutton, was also a as g dressed always been eof his pet subjects, and he hasLiverpool,in cream. Manar. G n. d titeecrt Rogerseremony f , os on lectured for the Liberation Society to further that end. performed by the Rev. David Jones, superintendent " If the voters only understood the full bearings of minister, assisted by the Rev. Dr. Mttcphail, of Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Examine'', Saturday August 3, 1907. the points at issue" he writes " Disestablishment THE HAGUE CONFERENCE. 1 THE STAFFORDSHIRE would speedily be demanded."</head>
        <p>The Examine'', Saturday August 3, 1907. the points at issue" he writes " Disestablishment THE HAGUE CONFERENCE. 1 THE STAFFORDSHIRE would speedily be demanded."</p>
        <p>Warringtonians do not need reminding that Mr. Howes is an expert on the fiscal question, but it is interesting to note that in 1882 he debated with Mr. B. Reed on the "fair trade" theory, and appears to , have had by far the best of the argument. He also early crossed swords with Mr. J. H. Bottomley and defeated him in a discussion on " Is the present Government worthy of the confidence of the people ?" So gratified were the workingmen at the results of Mr. Howes' propaganda in the Newport division that they presented him with a gold watch,yalue £4o—that sum being made up of subscriptions under half-a-crown. An important task which Mr. Howes fulfilled was the holding of a series of " County Franchise" meetings in Dorsetshire, amongst the worst-paid labourers in England. Afterwards he travelled far aid wide, lecturing and debating. He has a few words to say on Tory tactics in disturbing meetings. "During my busy and varied career I have met all sorts and conditions of men,' but the Tory swell is a great nuisance. He knows nothing of politics, but what he lacks in knowledge he makes up in Impudence and cheek. His courage is not very great, and ridicule soon overpowers him." Early in 1887 Mr. Howes found himself engaged in controversy with Mr. Chamberlain, and, singular to state, the discussion centred round a lecture delivered by Mr. Howes at Warrington, the bone of contention being the Irish land question. It was only to be expected that Mr. Howes' undoubted abilities as a controversialist would arouse the hostility of the Tories, and the following extract from the "Northern Daily Telegraph" is interesting : "There need be no surprise that one of the most, brilliant speakers engaged by the National Reform Union, Mr. Joseph Howes, is an object of consider- able envy and abuse in Tory quarters. Mr. Howes has earned in Lancashire a popularity quite remarkable considering his years and the short time he has been befora the public. He is an eloquent speaker and the smartest of debaters, and has put to rout some of the best men the Tories have been able to put forward. As a consequence he is a common target for the Tory slanderers and their newspaper writers. In Rochdale, where Mr. Howes has taken up his residence, he has been forcibly attacked. One of the weapons that his opponents , did not scruple to use is the charge that he received no education in his early ciays. That was a weapon ' worthy only, one would think, of the poltroons who hang out mendacious journals as their banners. But it was not confined to them ; such presumably , respectable people as the " Manchester Courier (Tory) editors tried to proclaim Mr. Howes' , ignorance by publishing a letter, as he was sup- • posed to have written it, with its imperfections of spelling and punctuation. But how smart is the Radical lecturer's retort to this despicable kind of Tory 'argument.' 'What education I received in my early days was at a Church school,a real old Tory school, where I was taught to clap my hands, stamp my feet and sing God save the Queen,' and was sent out into the world unable to write nay own name.' This was the education NIL Howes got. The ability of one so trained to debate to-day any representative Tory who dares to face him on the platform, is a proof that Toryism is not strong enough to keep under the surface such indomitable energy as that possessed by the self-educated man." Some of the most absorbing chapters in the book narrate the author's experiences in the Lancaster , division commencing in 1880. The first contest lastedl over three years, and Mr. Howes says that at times it developed scenes of brutality and blackguardism which could scarcely be considered conceivable in the latter Part of the century. "A number of orderly and successful meetings were held in various parts of the constituency, and I began to think that the rowdy reputation of the district had been exaggerated ; but I was soon to be undeceived. I had not entered the Fylde Dis- trict or " Dark Continent" as it was called. When I did, I found the pure unadulterated Toryism of the natives as robust and as brutal as ever. Aided by a plentiful supply of that wonderful beverage beer.' and led on by a doctor and a clergyman's son, they gained daring the following three years a notoriety for their neighbourhood, which all decent , people might well be ashamed of." Uproarious meetings are described and the doings during this contest are a revelation of the difficulties which beset all who endeavoured to spread Liberalism in the constituency in those days. It must indeed have required considerable courage to face tiv violent mobs, but Mr. Howes never shrunk from duty and by his good humour and readiness at retort often non- plussed his opponents. His work in the Division was highly successful, the Liberal being retdrned in 1886 and again in 1892, and rowdyism in the "Dark Conti- nent" was broken up and the right of Free Speech maintained. At Burnley in 1894 Mr. Howes had a tussle with the Socialists. "I found them very weak as debaters," he says, "and during the time I was in Burnley I hope and believe that I succeeded in reducing the strength of this party." At Rea-ling in 1896 he had a three nights' debate on Socialism with Mr. Quelch, editor of " Justice," and afterwards the Liberals of the town accorded him a snecial vote of thanks for his services to the cause. Mr. Howes, of course, was bitterly opposed to the war policy or the late Tory Government. On this subject he says : The misrepresentations in the Press and the tricks of Mr. Chamberlain brought the Jingoe3 shouting to the front, until at last we were plunged into war and then a lot of Liberals lost courage and seemed afraid to hold meetings. It was said: it is of no use trying to direct people's attention to home politics now; they will attend to nothing but war.' fhis was not strictly true. The Jingo is always noisy, but all through this dreadful business there were a lot of sober, thoughtful people, opposed to the war in South Africa. I kept going on whenever I got a chance, con- demning the war and giving the people the history of the crimes that led un to it." Mr. Howes' well-known gift of humour serves him in good stead in recording his experiences, and many laughable anecdotes add to the interest of the work. We commend it to the perusal of Liberals in this district, feeling sure that it will increase their regard for a man who has done devoted work for the cause of progress and the enlightenment of the masses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>ROYAL LANCASHIRE SHOW.</head>
        <p>ROYAL LANCASHIRE SHOW.</p>
        <p>Local Successes.</p>
        <p>Bolton was this year chosen as the venue of the Royal Lancashire Agricultural Society's annual show, which opened on Thursday under conditions which augur well for its ultimate success. There was good weather, and an attendance the dimensions of which whilst being much higher than the average for the County show for the past five years, bespoke the interest which was evinced in the event by the vast population of Bolton and vicinity in especial. A large patronage may always be counted upon from all, parts of Lancashire. The arrangements on the show ground are admirable.</p>
        <p>On either side of the broad main avenue are numerous stands on which may be seen specimens of almost every article calculated to interest the agriculturist, the tenant farmer or fancier. The machinery department is of special interest by reason of the introduction of that most recent specimen of engineering ingenuity, suction plant for the producing of gas,this machine arousing more than ordinary curiosity. Of motor waggons there are many types and makes, a department which will appeal in a special way to the motoring fraternity, though, of course, the vehicles here represented are for those principally used in heavy haulage. The entries this year, although not so numerous as at Lancaster last year, or Liverpool in 1905, are very high, the total being 4,312 as compared with 3,689 the average for the past five years and I,949—the entries at Bolton in 1891.</p>
        <p>The following are the local prize winners : CATTLE.</p>
        <p>KERRYS—BuII, calved in or before 1905 (open), Lady Greenall, Walton Hall, 3. Cow or heifer, in milk calved in or before 1901 (open), Lady Greenall, 1, belfer,calved in 1905 or 1906 (open), Lady Greenall, 1 and 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>HORSES*</head>
        <p>HORSES*</p>
        <p>HACKNEYS—FiIIy, fouled in 1906, by registered sire (open), J. Ball, Southworth Hall, Warrington, 3. RIDING AND DRIVING CLASSES—Harness pair of geldings or mares, or gelding and mare, any height (open), J. Magengue, Golborne Park, Newton-le- Willows, 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PIGS.</head>
        <p>PIGS.</p>
        <p>LARGE WHITES—SOW, farrowed previous to 1906 (open), Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Walton Hall, 1. MIDDLE WfrurEs—Boar, farrowed previous to 1906 (open), Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., 1, 2 and 3. Boar farrowed in 1907 (open), Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., 2 and 3.</p>
        <p>LARGE OR MIDDLE WHITES—Sow and litter of pigs, under eight weeks (open), Sir Gilbert Greenall,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Maiden Speech by Mr. A. J. King, M.P.</head>
        <p>Maiden Speech by Mr. A. J. King, M.P.</p>
        <p>SIR E. GREY'S REPLY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thursday, Mr. A. J. King, M.P., for Knutsford Division made his maiden speech. He called attention to some aspects of the Hague C mference. He thought public opinion was awakening to the belief that war was a relic of barbarism, and should find no place in twentieth-century civilisation. The utterances during the present year of the Prime Minister and Sir Edward Grey had turned all eyes to the Hague, and whatever might be the outcome of the Conference, these declarations would have done nothing but good. They had lifted the whole question of war against war out of the somewhat limited environment of Peace Society meetings into the larger sphere of diplomacy and statesmanship. But, owing largely to statements which had appeared in the press of this country as to what had occurred at the Con- ference, a feeling of disapppointment and depression had followed closely upon the period of hope and elation, and his object in rising was to invite Sir Edward Gray to take this opportunity of making some statement which would reassure the public mind, rho special correspondent of the " Tribune " writing on July 26th, said that no delegat ) with whom he had spoken had been able to mention a single word or deed of the British delegates that had helped any- one who was trying to further arbitration or prevent war. That was a very serious state- ment, &amp;id he hoped Sir Edward Grey would be able to say something which would put the position in a prom light before the country. The same correspondent also expressed the opinion that the position of leadership in the League of Peace, of which the Prime Minister spoke at the Albert Hall, was being taken not by this country but by Germany. For his own part, he was so anxious for the success of the peace proposals that he cared little to what nation the credit belonged if only they were brought to a successful issue. He was not sure, indeed, that Germany did not better deserve the position that we, for Germany had engaged in no serious war for thirty years. He hoped that some day this country would send a message to the Hague Conference that we had decided to reduce, if not entirely to abolish, our army and navy and to refer all disputes to arbitration, trust- ing in the justice of our cause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>SIR EDWARD GREY'S REPLY.</head>
        <p>SIR EDWARD GREY'S REPLY.</p>
        <p>In the course of his reply to various speakers, Sir Edward Grey congratulated Mr. King on his interest- ing and able speech. It was, he believed, the hon. member's first speech, and it was made in a tone and manner agreeable to the whole House. (Cheers.) He trusted that Mr. King's first experience of speaking in the House of Commons had been as agree- able to him as it had been to those who listened to him. (Cheers.) Mr. King expressed his disappointment (Sir Fdward Grey continued) with the results of the Hague Conference, especi- ally on the question of armaments. He was dis- appointed that the British delegates had not been instructed to make an appeal to the other Powers present to take some active step with regard to the reduction of armaments. Well, I cannot say what resolution may be eventually proposed at the Confer- ence. but if any resolution will be accepted by the delegates of the other Powers, the British delegates will be ready to propose it. We are very reluctant to make any proposal at the Conference which might turn what is and what ought to be a friendly Conference into one divided by controversy. (Cheers.) But with re- gard to making an appeal to the other Powers to meet us on the subject of the reduction of armaments,that appeal has been made emphatically and in the most public way by the Prime Minister. I supported it on at least one occasion in this House, and even if no definite results can be achieved at the Conference I trust that, at any rate, we shall have prevented the subject from dropping out of sight, and that the appeals which have been made to public opinion generally, though they may not have borne a direct result, may indirectly have helped the study of the question forward. I remain as impressed as I have ever been with what is almost the pathetic helplessness of mankind under the burden of armaments. (Cheers). It is an enormous burden on the great countries of the world, and it is natural that people should feel that, if there were only good-will to discuss the matter with each other, it ought to be possible to do something to reduce the burden. (Cheers). And yet they remain as holpless under the burden, apparently as ever.</p>
        <p>It is natural in these circumstances, feeling how much might be done if nations would only agree and how little is done, that there should be strong pressure on one nation to step out in advance and set an example. The difficulty of one nation stepping out in advance of another is that while there is a chance of reforming the world there is also a chance of that nation suffering martyrdom.--(Cheers.) We must therefore proceed at such a pace as will carry the leading nations of the world with us. He had no desire (he continued) to derogate from the prestige due to the prominent and ' honout able part which the German delegates have taken at the Conference. But some atten- tion ought also to be paid to what the British delegates had done. The British delegates were the first to support the United States in regard to general arbitration. They were also in favour of the transforma- tion of the Hague Conference into a permanent tribunal, so that it should be a recognised body to which any nation could appeal, and whose machinery could be brought into operation on an incident like that of the Dogger Bank. All those things made for peace, and in all those the British delegates had taken an honourable part. The question of the abolition of contraband of war belonged to another department of the Conference, which did not relate so much to the prevention of war as to making it when it broke out between two nations less disagreeable and terrible. That was an important piece of work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>SOAP LIBELS.</head>
        <p>THE SOAP LIBELS.</p>
        <p>Reported Action by Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited.</p>
        <p>It is reported that Messrs. Joseph Crosfield anl Sons, Limited, have decided to claim damages from the" Daily Mail" and associated newspapers for libel respecting the soap combine. The sum mentioned is £20,000. The report is, however, neither confirmed nor denied by the firm. So far it only stands as a rumour, though our information came from a reliable source.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>FOOTING THE BILL</head>
        <p>FOOTING THE BILL</p>
        <p>The Defendant Company's Position.</p>
        <p>It is very improbable that the outside shareholders as such will be affected to any important extent by the actions as in the base of the Associated News- papers„ Ltd, the whole of the deferred shares are hEld by the management and their friends, who were, of course, the original vendors. The capital of the com- pany, it will be remembered, is .£1,600,000 in £1 shares, of which £500,000 five per cent. cumulative preference and £600,000 ordinary shares only were offered to the public. These ordinary shares, more- over, rank for a cumulative preference dividend of 7 percent. per annum, after which the £500,000 deferred shares take all suplus profits subject to the annual provision of £20,000 for reserve until that fund totals £250,000. Thus it will be seen that the deferred shareholders, or otherwise the vendors themselves, will be the real sufferers. Even if the/directors decide to draw upon the reserve fund, which at present stands at :00,000, the position will remain much the same, as this policy would only mean the deferring to a more distant date the timst when the fund will have reached the stipulated .£250,000, after which all surplus profits will go to the deferred shareholders.</p>
        <p>Last year the surplus profits, after the provision ' for reserve, amounted to £78,000, of which the deferred shares received a 10 per cent. dividend, which absorbed £50,000, the balance brought forward amounting to £8,000; so it will be seen that ordinary shareholders have little to fear so far as their dividend for the current year is concerned. Even if the dividend were not paid in full this time, which is very unlikely, it would have to be paid up next year before the deferred shareholders received a, penny, as the ordinary share dividend is a cumulative one.—Daily Report.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>SHIPBUILDING RECORD.</head>
        <p>SHIPBUILDING RECORD.</p>
        <p>On the Clyde during the present month the vessels launched have aggregated 68,000 tons, beating all records for July, and raising the output for this year to the record total of 371,000 tons. A Japanese steamer which was floated was the first merchant vessel to be fitted with turbine engines. Unfortunately fresh orders are few, and two yards are without an order.</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>ELECTION. Great Liberal-Labour Victory. -- MAJORITY INCREASED.</head>
        <p>ELECTION. Great Liberal-Labour Victory. -- MAJORITY INCREASED.</p>
        <p>The result of the North Staffordshire election was declared at Stoke-on-Trent on Thursday. The figures were :</p>
        <p>Albert Stanley (Lib. and Lab.) T. NV. Twyford (C) 5,047</p>
        <p>Majority 2,349 At the general election the Liberal majority was 2,110, so that Mr. Stanley has been able to show that there is a hastening forward rather than "a set-back," as one of the Conservative speakers put it during the contest, in the progressive cause. A large number of people assembled outside the Town Hall to hear the result, which was declared at half-past eleven, and they gave rousing cheers when the figures were made in° wn.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stanley, when interviewed after the election, said there could be no uncertainty about the cause of his victory. First,they had in the division perfect union between the Labour and the Liberal forces ; and secondly, they had a clear and sound programme based on the principle that progressives would continue to work together to obtain solid advance. Mr. Stanley afterwards made a motor tour of the division, and was received almost as heartily in the agricultural as in the mining districts, although, of course, as a miners' repesentative of many years' standing, he was better known to the miners than to the farm labourers.</p>
        <p>In the course of the afternoon a number of messages of congratulation were sent to Mr. Stanley. Mr. Murray MacDonald, M.P., wrote; "I am directed by the Committee of the Cobden Club to congratulate you on your over-whelming victory over Tariff Reform in the election for North-West Staffor&amp;hire. 'We rejoice in the loyalty of the working men under your leadership to the cause of Free Trade.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>PREVIOUS ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>PREVIOUS ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Until the last general election, North-West Stafford- shire was a Conservative seat. Former polls were :- 1892.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Heath (C) Mr. L. K. H. Shoobridge (L) 5106</p>
        <p>Conservative majority 233 1895.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Heath (C) Mr. L. K. H. Shoobridge (L)</p>
        <p>Conservative majority 668 1900.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Heath (C) Mr. J. Lovett (L)</p>
        <p>Conservative majority l6ll 1906.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Billson (L) Sir J. Heath (C)</p>
        <p>Liberal majority MR. A. STANLEY, M.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stanley was born in 1863, at the small Shropshire village of Dark Lane. His father was a miner, and at ten years of age the son went to work in the mine. Not many years later he became a member of the miners' union and in 1884, when he became twenty-one, he was elected agent to the Cannock Chase Miners'Association, an appointment he still holds. Mr. Stanley is also secretary to the Midland Counties Federation and a member of the Committee of the Miners' Federation of Great Britain. It may, indeed, be said that in the counsels of tha Federation the views of no mmber are regarded more highly. Mr. Stanley is a clear and forcible speaker. He is a member of the Staffordshire County Council and of the County Educa- tion Committee. After the declaration of the poll, Mr. Twyford, the defeated candidate, said of him :—" I respect Mr. Stanley. I respect him as my member, and I acknowledge him as such with very great pleasure. I trust that those who supported me will take the same view of it as I have done, and look upon him as a member who will, I am sure, serve them faithfully and well."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. Re-Opening on Monday Next.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. Re-Opening on Monday Next.</p>
        <p>When the new Theatre opens its doors on Monday I next (Bank Holiday), it will reveal to its patrons the new modern up-to-date dramatic house, which the well- known proprietors, Messrs. Johnson and Potter, after so many years association with Warrington, and at con- siderable expense, have provided so that the public may enjoy the most popular productions of the stage. The theatre has a long frontage to Rylands-street, uct here am o situated all the public entrances extending the whole length of the building. In the centre of this frontage is the grand entrance to the stalls and pit stalls, and on the left the entrances to the pit, circle, gallery, and back of pit. On the right is an early door communicating with all parts of the house. The auditorium or public portion of the theatre is divided on the ground floor into pit, pit stalls and orchestra stalls ; over the pit is the lower circle tier, and on the top a large gallery specially arranged in its seature so that all occupants can have an uninterrupted view of the stage. There are also four private boxes. Numerous exits are provided to all parts of the theatre and as the building is isolated there is no difficulty in the audience getting out quickly in case of emergency. In the main entrance is the box office and inner vesti- bule doors lead to the crush room with a ladies' retiring room on the left. Retiring rooms are also provided in various Darts of the theatre. The orchestra floor is sunk about 18ins. below the Invel of the stalls, from which it is screened by brass tails and curtains, and the pit floor being on the rake, or incline, gives the audience an uninterrupted view of the stage. The 'proscenium opening dividing the stage from the auditorium is provided with a fire resisting curtain. The stage which is lofty aud well-lighted con- tains the usual fly galleries and grid, approached by a spiral staircase. The stage is fitted up with all modern appliances suitable for the best productions, and it has the usual property-rooms adjoining. The corner of Rylands-street and the side street is devoted to three storeys] of dressing rooms, fitted up with lavatories, dressing tables, S;c., and there are the usual conveniences on each floor. These rooms are approached by a fireproof staircase leading direct to the stage and entrance. Bill posting, storage and other rooms are also provided, including the manager's office, which is situated adjoining the early door. The catering for the audience is provided for by a stalls and circle refreshment room situated on the right of the main crush room (but opproached only from the stalls) and there is also a bar at the.rear of the pit in the basement. All the public staircases are fire- proof. An electric plant and gas engine are provided in a fireproof room, in the basement, and the whole of the theatre, including the stage,is lighted bye lectricity, with a complete system of gas lighting provided for in case of emergency. In the centre of the elliptic dome of the auditorium ceiling is a sunburner which acts as an exhaust ventilator, and lights up the whole of the auditorium with gas ff required. The decorative treatment of the interior has had I careful consideration and is worked out in shades of salmon pink and reds relieved with gold, and the upholstery and hangings are in deep crimson. The doors, screens and dados of the main entrance which are in mahogany, and the mosaic flodr and tiling are in harmony with the general scheme of decoration. The decorative figure subject over the proscenium opening has been specially painted for the theatre, likewise all the scenery, which is composed of some very pleasing sets. For the cold weather a system of heating by hot water is provided, and all the dressing rooms are heated by pipes which will also serve as drying rails. The whole of the works have been designed and completed in just over six months. Messrs. Hughes and Stirling the well-known Liverpool contractors have carried out the main contract for the building, with Mr. Heinle as general fOreman. Niessrs. Fred Wilkins and Bro., of Liverpool, have execand the.whole of the scenery, upholstering, electric lighting and plant and also the tiro resisting curtain. Messrs. Goodall, Lamb and Heighway, of Manchester, have had the contract for the decorations and painting. Messrs. Tanner, of Liverpool, have done the plaster work. Messrs. Greenhalgh, of Warrington, have manufactured and laid the wood-block flooring. Mr. Acton, of Warrington, the gas piping and fittings. The sunburner has been supplied by Messrs. Stott, of Manchester. rho heating apparatus by Messrs. Leech, Bros. of Manchester. Messrs. Hope and Sons, Birming- ham the verandah and stained glass. Messrs. Conway I and Co., Manchester the mosaic and terazzo floors. The sanitary fittings by Messrs. Doulton and Co.,Manchester. Locks and door furniture by Messrs. Charles Smith and I Sons, Birmingham. The steelwork in the galleries has been executed by the Trafford Park Steelwork Co., and the other ironwork by Messrs. Park of Wigan. The Architect for the whole of the works is Mr. F. Bennett Smith, F.R.1.8.A., of Manchester, who has had a large experience in theatre construction. Amongst theatres that he has been connected with the following may be mentioned :—The Palace Theatre of Varieties, Manchester; the Prince's and Gaiety Theatres, Man- chester; Exeter Theatre Royal; Lyceum, Crewe; Bed- ford Leigh Theatre; the Grand, Rawtenstall, and the Tivoli Music Hall,l3arrow-in-Furness. "BESIDE THE BONNIE BRIER BUSEI."</p>
        <p>Mr. William Moßison and his special company will open the Theatre on Monday evening witn the charm- ing play "Beside the Bonnie Brier Bush," from the St. James' Thestre,London.Great things are expectod of Mr. Moßison, who has risen to the rank of one of the best tragedians on the English stage. The" London Daily Chronicle" has described Mr. Mollison as " the soundest all-round Shakespearian actor that we have," and the "Morning Post" says of him : " Here is the only actor we have who has the grand manner, the magnificent voice, and the style of the great Salvini." A crowded house for Monday and the rest of the week should be assured.</p>
        <p>777- --;":7Z;;;;:lr:.-,;;TZ</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>DEATH OF MR, W. G. BENNETT.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR, W. G. BENNETT.</p>
        <p>We regret to announce that Mr. William Grange Bennett, eldest son of Mr. John Bennett, Grange Mount, Padgate, died after a short illness of pneumonia, at his residence Calla House, Garstang, on Saturday. Mr. Bennett will be best remembered locally from the fact that up to the time of his marriage to Miss Nellie Baxter of Acton Grange, 20 years ago, he resided at Bewsey Old Hall, afterwards settling at Garstang as a cattle dealer and farmer, which business he carried on up to the time of his desth. isa a Wesleyan he had occupied nearly all the offices open to a layman, includ- ing that of Circuit steward of the Garstang Circuit, where he was much respected by the members of his church. In politics he was an ardent Conservative.,, For many years and up to the time of his death he , held the post of secretary of Garstang, Primrose League. The deceased gentleman was also a Freemason and for a number of years a member of the Wyreside Lodge of which he was an officer to the last. His remains were laid to rest in the quiet churchyard at Garstang on Tuesday last. There was a short ser- vice at the house conducted by Rev. J. Gibson (Wesleyan minister), after which the cortege proceeded to the church, being joined as it entered the quaint old town, in which practically every house had dra v n its blinds and business was for the time suspended, by a large number of Freemasons and residents who walked in front to the church, where the final obsequies were formally conducted by the Rev. G. B. Stones (vicar of Garstang), and Rev. J. Gibson, th latter reading the lesson. As the ministers headed the precession lilt° the church, reading the opening sentences of the Burial service, the organist played very beautifully and softly "0 rest in the Lord,' the hymns" 0 God our help in ages past," and" Let all the saints terrestrial sing," the hymns being sung by the large congregation present. Among those present at the funeral were :—Master Clifford Bennett (son), Mr. John Bennett (father),Rev. J.Grange Bennett (London), Mr. Joseph Bennett, Dr. Bennett, Mr. Leonard Bennett (Padgate), and Mr. F. S. Bennett (London), brothers. Mr. W. J. Bennett and Mr. J. S. Banks (Poulton-le- Fylde), Mr. John Bennett (Urmston), brother-in-law. Mr. Tom Fairhurst (Southport), nephew, Mr. James Warburton, Bewsey Farm, and Mr. Thomas Baxter (Hill Cliff e). _</p>
        <p>The following is a list of those who sent wreaths : Father and Mother, Rev. J. and Mrs. Grange Bennett (London), Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Bennett (Padgate), Dr. and Mrs. Bennett (Padgate), Mr. and Mrs. J. S. Bennett (London), Mr. Leonard and Misses Bennett (Padgate), Mr. and Mrs. William Bennett (Padgate), Mrs. Thomas Bennett and family (Padgate), Mrs. Harris and Mr. and Mrs. Newton (Padgate), Mr. and Mrs. W. J. and Mr. Alfred Bennett (Poulton), Mr. and Mrs. Walter Bennett (Padgate), Mr. and Mrs. Herbert Hatton (Thel- wall), Mr. and Mrs. George Hutchinson, (Poulton), Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Baxter (Hill Cliffe), the Woking relatives, Mr. and Mrs. J. S. Banks (Poulton), Mr. and Mrs. Bartlett (Garstang), Miss Turner (Garstang),Mr. and Mrs. W. Robinson (Garstang), Mr. and Mrs. W. Crozier (Garstang), Mr. and Mrs. J. Ball (Winmarleigh), teachers and members of Winifred's and Hilda's Sunday School Class, teachers and members of Clifford's Sunday School Class, Primrose League Garstang Habitation, and the Wyreside Masonic Lodge</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-03_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>FIDDLER'S FERRY SAILING AND BOATING CLUB.— This club held their first race of the season on Saturday from Fiddler's Ferry to Rock Ferry for prizes provided by the club. An excellent start was made at high water and with a nice sailing breeze and the race finished at Rock Ferry in the following order :—lone (Knowles and Thompson), shours Gmin. 30sec., Nina (Whittaker). shours 21min, lOsecs ; Maggie (Meacock), shours 23 min. ; Thelma (H. Warburton), shours, 38min. 'The Fag did not start. The officials of the Tranmere Sail- ing Club kindly acted as timekeepers for the day. PAROCHIAL TRlP.—Church workers from St. Mary's and Penketh Mission had their annual trip on Saturday when they visited Southport. The party numbered about 80, and reached Southport at 11 a.m. Dinner was served at 12 o'clock, after which the visitors enjoyed the numerous attractions that Southport offers. The Marine Lake, the Botanical Gardens, the Baths, and shore amusements (including the helter-skelter and flying machine) were well patronised in turn. A very substantial tea was provided. The Vicar of the parish (the Rev. J. R. Jones) afterwards delivered a short address, thanking all the party for the splendid assist- ance they had rendered him, and encouraging them at the same time to re-double their efforts for the well- being and good of the church of which they were all members. Further pleasures were then indulged in, and after a most happy and sociable day the party returned, Sankey being reached at ten o'clock. The arrangements were excellent and in this connection thanks are due to Mr. Wovds. GARDEN PARTY.—Saturday last was a red letter day in the calendar of the members of the United Free Church Council and friends. The President (Mr. C. E. Parker) gave the members a cordial invitation to a garden party at his residence, Penketh Lodge. The , members and friends to the number of 80, availed them- selves of the kind invitation. The party began to arrive at 3.30 p.m., and were warmly welcomed by Mr. and Mrs. Parker, and invited to amuse themselves with various games and walks round the grounds. The rose garden was much admired, especially by those who have garden suburb ideas in their minds.—Mr. Parker. who is president of the newly-formed company' • showed how interested he was in the question by exhibiting a poster on the lawn, announcing a meeting which was held on Monday last at Cairo-street, Warrington, to advance the business of the above company ; inviting those to attend who were interested in the question. Tea was a popular item on the programme. After tea the guests adjourned to the lawn ; some to enjoy the various games, others preferring to walk round the grounds. A very pleasant day was brought to a close about seven o'clock, one and all thanking Mr. and Mrs. Parker for their hospitality.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETY NEWS.— On Friday last the Penketh United Order of Druids held their monthly Lodge night, which was well attended by the members. Bro. J. Graver, N.G.A., presided and was supported by Bro. J. Leigh, P.G.A. This Lodge is a branch of the Warrington Equalized District of Druids and one of the most thriving Friendly Society lodges in the district. During the evening, the quarterly report and balance sheet which contained the quinquen- nial valuation of the assets and liabilities of the Warring- ton District Order of Druids was before the members. As there was little time it was suggested that a special meeting be called of the members to discuss this matter and other matters of importance to the Lodge. , After the usual lodge business was gone through three presentations were made. Bro. J. Pennington was pre- sented with an illuminated emblem of the Order for procuring new members, and a silver =edallion was given to Bro. James Smith for similar reasons. Bro. A. T. Clay, in accordance with a resolution of last Lodge night,was presented also with a silver medallion. Bro. David Houghton, Past Grand Master, made the presentation and in well chosen words wished the recipients every possible good. (Applause.) Bro. Pennington thanked the members for their kind expres- sion and Bro. Clay also made a suitable response. This concluded the business of the evening.</p>
      </div>
