        <p>SUNNY SOUTHPORT,' 65,000 GUIDE BOOKS to -9ais popu.lar ana attractive Watering Place en the Lancashire Coast are to be GIVEN AWAY POST FREE. Intending visitors should apply at once to J. WILKINSON, TOWN HALL, SOUTHPORT.</p>
        <p>PADGATE. _ WALKING D.—After two pc stponements on account of wet weather, Padgate Walking Day was held on Saturday when a very pretty spectacle was presented. Headed by the band of the Cottage Homes the children were formed in processional order and marched round the village to maadal strains. An unusually attractive feature was introduced,the children from the Industrial Schools wearing the character costumes in which they were attired for the rose queen festivities recently held at the schools. After the tour of the village the youngsters adjourned for tea at the schools and later proceeded to a field where the day was spent in games and racing for prizes. FIRE AT TIIE RAILWAY SlDlNG.—Considerable excitement was occasioned on Saturday by an outbreak of file at the Cheshire Lines Company railway siding near Padgate Station. Two waggon loads of straw had been left in the siding and shortly before twp o'clock it was discovered that they were on fire, Bving been ignited, it is supposed, by a spark from a passing train. The Cieshire Lines Company's fire brigade at once pro- ceeded to check the outbreak and the Warrington Brigade was also summoned, Deputy Superintendent Turner and a full complement of men being quickly on the spot. A good supply of water was obtained from the mains and an hour's work saw the lire extinguished. The bulk of the straw was destroyed and three railway wagons also suffered considerable damage.</p>
